(function(){(window.PhysicaNeetYear=window.PhysicaNeetYear||{})[2026]=[{id:"neet-2026-q001",pn:1,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"Kinetic Theory",st:"Degrees of freedom, Cp/Cv",q:"An ideal gas is made of polyatomic molecules. Each of the molecules has three translational, three rotational and f number of vibrational modes. If the ratio of heat capacities C\u209A/C\u1D65 of the gas is 8/7, then the value of f is:",o:["3","2","1","4"],c:3,s:`Internal energy of one mole: U = (3/2 k_B T + 3/2 k_B T + f k_B T) N_A.
So U = (3 + f) k_B T N_A = (3 + f)RT.
C\u1D65 = dU/dT = (3 + f)R.
C\u209A = C\u1D65 + R = (4 + f)R.
C\u209A/C\u1D65 = (4 + f)/(3 + f) = 8/7 (given).
So f = 4.`,sub:"Physics",ch:"Kinetic Theory"},{id:"neet-2026-q002",pn:2,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"Units and Measurements",st:"Vernier calliper zero error",q:"One main scale division of a Vernier calliper is equal to 1 mm and the number of divisions on the Vernier scale is 10. When both the jaws touch each other, the Vernier scale shifts to the left of zero of the main scale in such a way that 4th Vernier division coincides with a division of the main scale. If this Vernier calliper measures the length of the wire to be 1 cm, the actual length of the wire is :",o:["0.96 cm","1.00 cm","1.04 cm","0.60 cm"],a:"No option is correct",s:`The Vernier scale is to the left of zero, so there is a negative zero error.
Zero error = \u2013(10 \u2013 4) \xD7 L.C. (L.C. = 0.01 cm).
Zero error = \u2013(10 \u2013 4) \xD7 0.01 = \u20130.06 cm.
Zero correction = \u2013 Zero error = \u2013(\u20130.06) = 0.06 cm.
Correct reading R\u2032 = R + zero correction = 1 + 0.06 = 1.06 cm.
No option gives 1.06 cm, so no option is correct.
Note: Printed answer: No option is correct (working gives 1.06 cm)`,sub:"Physics",ch:"Units and Measurements"},{id:"neet-2026-q003",pn:3,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"Semiconductor Electronics",st:"p-n junction biasing, depletion width",q:"Three identical p-n junction diodes D\u2081, D\u2082, and D\u2083 are connected across a battery as shown in the figure. If the width of the depletion regions of D\u2081, D\u2082 and D\u2083 are W\u2081, W\u2082 and W\u2083, respectively, then the correct option is:",o:["W\u2083 = W\u2081 > W\u2082","W\u2083 > W\u2082 > W\u2081","W\u2082 > W\u2081 = W\u2083","W\u2081 > W\u2082 > W\u2083"],c:1,s:`D\u2081 is forward biased.
Depletion region of a forward-biased diode (W\u2081) is the minimum.
D\u2083 is reverse biased.
Depletion region of a reverse-biased diode (W\u2083) is the maximum.
D\u2082 is unbiased.
Its depletion region (W\u2082) lies between W\u2081 and W\u2083.
So W\u2083 > W\u2082 > W\u2081.`,fig:{w:250,h:225,alt:"Three branches across a 5 V battery: 1 k\u03A9 with D1 (forward), 1 k\u03A9 with D2 (middle branch not joined to the right wire), and D3 (reversed) with 1 k\u03A9.",d:[["l",47,33,80,33,""],["p","M80 33 L82.7 28 L85.4 38 L88.1 28 L90.8 38 L93.5 28 L96.2 38 L98.8 28 L101.5 38 L104.2 28 L106.9 38 L109.6 28 L112.3 38 L115 33"],["l",115,33,152,33,""],["p","M152 25L152 41L170 33Z","F"],["l",170,25,170,41,""],["l",170,33,204,33,""],["t",77,18,"1 k\u03A9",""],["t",150,16,"D\u2081",""],["l",20,95,80,95,""],["p","M80 95 L82.7 90 L85.4 100 L88.1 90 L90.8 100 L93.5 90 L96.2 100 L98.8 90 L101.5 100 L104.2 90 L106.9 100 L109.6 90 L112.3 100 L115 95"],["l",115,95,152,95,""],["p","M152 87L152 103L170 95Z","F"],["l",170,87,170,103,""],["l",170,95,178,95,""],["c",180,95,1.5,"F"],["c",194,95,1.5,"F"],["l",204,95,232,95,""],["t",77,80,"1 k\u03A9",""],["t",150,78,"D\u2082",""],["l",47,150,82,150,""],["p","M100 142L100 158L82 150Z","F"],["l",82,142,82,158,""],["l",100,150,140,150,""],["p","M140 150 L142.7 145 L145.4 155 L148.1 145 L150.8 155 L153.5 145 L156.2 155 L158.8 145 L161.5 155 L164.2 145 L166.9 155 L169.6 145 L172.3 155 L175 150"],["l",175,150,204,150,""],["t",86,135,"D\u2083",""],["t",140,135,"1 k\u03A9",""],["l",47,33,47,150,""],["l",204,33,204,150,""],["l",20,95,20,193,""],["l",232,95,232,193,""],["l",20,193,122,193,""],["l",128,193,232,193,""],["l",122,184,122,202,""],["l",128,189,128,197,"B"],["t",128,218,"5 V","m"]]},sub:"Physics",ch:"Semiconductor Electronics"},{id:"neet-2026-q004",pn:4,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"Oscillations",st:"SHM velocity-displacement graph",q:"Consider a spring-mass simple harmonic oscillator in one dimension. The mass of the particle is m kg and the spring constant is k Nm\u207B\xB9. At a given instant, the extension of the spring is x-meter and the speed of the particle is v ms\u207B\xB9. On the x - v plane, if the graph of v as a function of x is a circle, then the correct option is:",o:["k = m","k = m\xB2","k = \u221Am","k = 1/m"],c:0,s:`Velocity in SHM: v = \u03C9\u221A(A\xB2 \u2013 x\xB2).
v\xB2 = \u03C9\xB2(A\xB2 \u2013 x\xB2).
v\xB2/\u03C9\xB2 + x\xB2 = A\xB2.
This is the equation of a circle, so \u03C9\xB2 = 1.
k/m = 1.
So k = m.`,sub:"Physics",ch:"Oscillations"},{id:"neet-2026-q005",pn:5,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"Electromagnetic Waves",st:"EM wave in a dielectric medium",q:"An electromagnetic wave travelling in a lossless dielectric medium having a dielectric constant, \u03B5\u1D63 = 9, has the electric field, E\u2093 = E\u2080 sin (kz \u2013 2\u03C0 \xD7 10\u2076t) Vm\u207B\xB9 where E\u2080 is the amplitude and k is the wave vector. Among the following options, the incorrect choice is",o:["The wavelength of the electromagnetic wave inside the medium is 300 m","The magnetic field is given by the relation B_y = (B\u2080/v) sin(kz \u2013 2\u03C0 \xD7 10\u2076t) where v is the speed of the electromagnetic wave inside the medium","The direction of propagation of the electromagnetic wave is along +z","The speed of the electromagnetic wave inside the medium is 10\u2078 ms\u207B\xB9"],c:0,s:`E\u2093 = E\u2080 sin(kz \u2013 2\u03C0 \xD7 10\u2076t) = E\u2080 sin(kz \u2013 \u03C9t).
So the wave travels along +z.
Speed in the medium: v\u2098 = v\u2080/\u221A\u03B5\u1D63 = c/\u221A9 = (3 \xD7 10\u2078)/3 = 10\u2078 m/s.
Frequency f\u2080 = \u03C9/2\u03C0 = 10\u2076 Hz, and it stays constant.
Wavelength in the medium: \u03BB\u2098 = v\u2098/f\u2080 = 10\u2078/(2\u03C0 \xD7 10\u2076/2\u03C0) = 100 m.
So the wavelength inside the medium is 100 m, not 300 m, and option (1) is incorrect.
Note: in option (2), B\u2080 should be replaced by E\u2080.
Note: Option (2) as printed uses B\u2080 where E\u2080 is meant (publisher notes this); answer (1) is still the incorrect statement`,sub:"Physics",ch:"Electromagnetic Waves"},{id:"neet-2026-q006",pn:6,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"System of Particles and Rotational Motion",st:"Linear momentum of a system of particles",q:"A frictionless circular wire of unit radius is fixed on the horizontal plane. Two-point particles of unit mass start moving simultaneously from point A (\u03B8 = \u03C0/2) with identical uniform angular speeds in opposite directions, and meet again at point B (\u03B8 = \u2013\u03C0/2). During this time, which of the following figures schematically represent the magnitude of the total linear momentum P of the system, as a function of \u03B8?",o:["Graph 1: a curve like |cos \u03B8| with two downward cusps: P starts at a maximum at the left, falls to 0 at the first cusp, rises to a smaller peak and falls to 0 at the second cusp","Graph 2: a single smooth semicircular arch starting at 0 at \u03B8 = \u03C0/2 and returning to 0 at \u03B8 = \u2013\u03C0/2","Graph 3: a straight line falling from a maximum at \u03B8 = \u03C0/2 to 0 at \u03B8 = \u2013\u03C0/2","Graph 4: a horizontal constant line"],c:1,s:`Both particles have the same speed v, moving in opposite directions, at angle \u03B8 from the horizontal.
Net momentum: P_net = \u2013mv sin\u03B8 \xEE \u2013 mv cos\u03B8 \u0135 + mv sin\u03B8 \xEE \u2013 mv cos\u03B8 \u0135.
P_net = \u20132mv cos\u03B8 \u0135.
At \u03B8 = \u03C0/2, P_net = 0.
At \u03B8 = \u2013\u03C0/2, P_net = 0.
So P is zero at both ends and largest in the middle, like cos \u03B8.
The most suitable graph is a single smooth arch.
Note: Option graphs are drawn pictures; the option texts in o are my descriptions, see dia. Printed answer (2) = single arch from 0 to 0`,fig:{w:350,h:345,alt:"Circle with points A (top) and B (bottom), angle \u03B8 from the horizontal, two particles leaving A in opposite directions, and four P versus \u03B8 graphs (1) to (4).",d:[["c",80,60,30],["l",80,24,80,96,"d"],["l",35,60,125,60,"d"],["t",70,18,"A","e"],["t",70,110,"B","e"],["p","M72 25C62 28 54 36 50 46","a"],["p","M88 25C98 28 106 36 110 46","a"],["p","M92 60A12 12 0 0 0 87 50"],["t",96,56,"\u03B8",""],["l",45,210,45,150,"a"],["l",45,210,135,210,"a"],["t",43,148,"P","e"],["t",137,214,"\u03B8",""],["t",45,224,"\u03C0/2","m"],["t",99,224,"\u2013\u03C0/2","m"],["t",3,184,"(1)",""],["l",235,210,235,150,"a"],["l",235,210,325,210,"a"],["t",233,148,"P","e"],["t",327,214,"\u03B8",""],["t",235,224,"\u03C0/2","m"],["t",289,224,"\u2013\u03C0/2","m"],["t",193,184,"(2)",""],["l",45,310,45,250,"a"],["l",45,310,135,310,"a"],["t",43,248,"P","e"],["t",137,314,"\u03B8",""],["t",45,324,"\u03C0/2","m"],["t",99,324,"\u2013\u03C0/2","m"],["t",3,284,"(3)",""],["l",235,310,235,250,"a"],["l",235,310,325,310,"a"],["t",233,248,"P","e"],["t",327,314,"\u03B8",""],["t",235,324,"\u03C0/2","m"],["t",289,324,"\u2013\u03C0/2","m"],["t",193,284,"(4)",""],["p","M45 154C45 180 60 195 70 210"],["p","M70 210C75 190 90 175 99 175"],["p","M235 210C235 160 289 160 289 210"],["l",45,254,99,310,""],["l",235,270,315,270,""]]},sfig:{w:150,h:115,alt:"P versus \u03B8: a single semicircular arch from zero at \u03C0/2 to zero at \u2013\u03C0/2.",d:[["l",20,90,20,10,"a"],["l",20,90,120,90,"a"],["t",18,10,"P","e"],["t",124,94,"\u03B8",""],["t",20,106,"\u03C0/2","m"],["t",88,106,"\u2013\u03C0/2","m"],["p","M20 90C20 25 90 25 90 90"]]},sub:"Physics",ch:"System of Particles and Rotational Motion"},{id:"neet-2026-q007",pn:7,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"Atoms",st:"Rutherford scattering N(\u03B8) graph",q:"In Geiger-Marsden experiment, the number of scattered \u03B1-particles N(\u03B8) is plotted as a function of scattering angle \u03B8. Which of the following options represents the correct plot?",o:["Graph 1: N(\u03B8) starts near 10\u2076 at 0\xB0, rises slightly then falls smoothly to about 10\xB3 near 170\xB0","Graph 2: N(\u03B8) starts near 10\u2077 at 0\xB0 and falls steeply then flattens to near 10 at 180\xB0 (decreasing curve, hyperbola-like)","Graph 3: bell-shaped curve, low (about 10) at 0\xB0 and 180\xB0, with a flat maximum near 10\u2076 between about 80\xB0 and 100\xB0","Graph 4: straight line rising from about 10\xB2 at 0\xB0 to about 10\u2076 at about 150\xB0"],c:1,s:`Number of \u03B1-particles scattered at angle \u03B1: N \u221D 1/sin\u2074(\u03B1/2).
So N(\u03B8) = K/sin\u2074(\u03B8/2).
N is large for small \u03B8 and decreases rapidly as \u03B8 grows.
So the graph is a steeply falling curve.`,fig:{w:610,h:295,alt:"Four log-scale plots of N(\u03B8) against \u03B8 in degrees: (1) slowly falling from about 10\u2076, (2) steeply falling from about 10\u2077, (3) bell-shaped peaking near 90\xB0, (4) rising straight line.",d:[["l",60,115,60,10,"a"],["l",60,110,225,110,"a"],["t",60,6,"N(\u03B8)","s"],["t",228,113,"\u03B8 (in degree)","s"],["l",57,110,60,110,""],["t",55,113,"10","es"],["l",57,95,60,95,""],["t",55,98,"10\xB2","es"],["l",57,80,60,80,""],["t",55,83,"10\xB3","es"],["l",57,65,60,65,""],["t",55,68,"10\u2074","es"],["l",57,50,60,50,""],["t",55,53,"10\u2075","es"],["l",57,35,60,35,""],["t",55,38,"10\u2076","es"],["l",57,20,60,20,""],["t",55,23,"10\u2077","es"],["l",60,110,60,113,""],["t",60,123,"0","ms"],["l",84.9,110,84.9,113,""],["t",84.9,123,"30","ms"],["l",109.8,110,109.8,113,""],["t",109.8,123,"60","ms"],["l",134.7,110,134.7,113,""],["t",134.7,123,"90","ms"],["l",159.6,110,159.6,113,""],["t",159.6,123,"120","ms"],["l",184.5,110,184.5,113,""],["t",184.5,123,"150","ms"],["l",209.4,110,209.4,113,""],["t",209.4,123,"180","ms"],["t",15,70,"(1)",""],["p","M60.0 35 L72.5 34.2 L84.9 34.2 L109.8 41.0 L134.7 50.0 L159.6 62.0 L184.5 72.5 L201.1 80.0"],["l",340,115,340,10,"a"],["l",340,110,505,110,"a"],["t",340,6,"N(\u03B8)","s"],["t",508,113,"\u03B8 (in degree)","s"],["l",337,110,340,110,""],["t",335,113,"10","es"],["l",337,95,340,95,""],["t",335,98,"10\xB2","es"],["l",337,80,340,80,""],["t",335,83,"10\xB3","es"],["l",337,65,340,65,""],["t",335,68,"10\u2074","es"],["l",337,50,340,50,""],["t",335,53,"10\u2075","es"],["l",337,35,340,35,""],["t",335,38,"10\u2076","es"],["l",337,20,340,20,""],["t",335,23,"10\u2077","es"],["l",340,110,340,113,""],["t",340,123,"0","ms"],["l",364.9,110,364.9,113,""],["t",364.9,123,"30","ms"],["l",389.8,110,389.8,113,""],["t",389.8,123,"60","ms"],["l",414.7,110,414.7,113,""],["t",414.7,123,"90","ms"],["l",439.6,110,439.6,113,""],["t",439.6,123,"120","ms"],["l",464.5,110,464.5,113,""],["t",464.5,123,"150","ms"],["l",489.4,110,489.4,113,""],["t",489.4,123,"180","ms"],["t",295,70,"(2)",""],["p","M340.0 20 L344.1 33.5 L348.3 44.0 L356.6 59.0 L373.2 75.5 L398.1 89.0 L431.3 98.0 L464.5 104.0 L489.4 107.0"],["l",60,265,60,160,"a"],["l",60,260,225,260,"a"],["t",60,156,"N(\u03B8)","s"],["t",228,263,"\u03B8 (in degree)","s"],["l",57,260,60,260,""],["t",55,263,"10","es"],["l",57,245,60,245,""],["t",55,248,"10\xB2","es"],["l",57,230,60,230,""],["t",55,233,"10\xB3","es"],["l",57,215,60,215,""],["t",55,218,"10\u2074","es"],["l",57,200,60,200,""],["t",55,203,"10\u2075","es"],["l",57,185,60,185,""],["t",55,188,"10\u2076","es"],["l",57,170,60,170,""],["t",55,173,"10\u2077","es"],["l",60,260,60,263,""],["t",60,273,"0","ms"],["l",84.9,260,84.9,263,""],["t",84.9,273,"30","ms"],["l",109.8,260,109.8,263,""],["t",109.8,273,"60","ms"],["l",134.7,260,134.7,263,""],["t",134.7,273,"90","ms"],["l",159.6,260,159.6,263,""],["t",159.6,273,"120","ms"],["l",184.5,260,184.5,263,""],["t",184.5,273,"150","ms"],["l",209.4,260,209.4,263,""],["t",209.4,273,"180","ms"],["t",15,220,"(3)",""],["p","M60.0 257.0 L68.3 258.5 L84.9 255.5 L97.3 230 L109.8 192.5 L122.2 173.0 L134.7 171.5 L147.1 173.0 L159.6 192.5 L172.1 230 L184.5 252.5 L201.1 258.5"],["l",340,265,340,160,"a"],["l",340,260,505,260,"a"],["t",340,156,"N(\u03B8)","s"],["t",508,263,"\u03B8 (in degree)","s"],["l",337,260,340,260,""],["t",335,263,"10","es"],["l",337,245,340,245,""],["t",335,248,"10\xB2","es"],["l",337,230,340,230,""],["t",335,233,"10\xB3","es"],["l",337,215,340,215,""],["t",335,218,"10\u2074","es"],["l",337,200,340,200,""],["t",335,203,"10\u2075","es"],["l",337,185,340,185,""],["t",335,188,"10\u2076","es"],["l",337,170,340,170,""],["t",335,173,"10\u2077","es"],["l",340,260,340,263,""],["t",340,273,"0","ms"],["l",364.9,260,364.9,263,""],["t",364.9,273,"30","ms"],["l",389.8,260,389.8,263,""],["t",389.8,273,"60","ms"],["l",414.7,260,414.7,263,""],["t",414.7,273,"90","ms"],["l",439.6,260,439.6,263,""],["t",439.6,273,"120","ms"],["l",464.5,260,464.5,263,""],["t",464.5,273,"150","ms"],["l",489.4,260,489.4,263,""],["t",489.4,273,"180","ms"],["t",295,220,"(4)",""],["p","M344.1 243.5 L464.5 171.5"]]},sfig:{w:270,h:150,alt:"N(\u03B8) versus \u03B8: a steeply falling concave-up curve that flattens at large \u03B8.",d:[["l",20,130,20,10,"a"],["l",20,130,240,130,"a"],["t",20,8,"N(\u03B8)","s"],["t",244,134,"\u03B8","s"],["p","M25 20C40 80 80 110 130 118C180 124 210 127 230 128"]]},sub:"Physics",ch:"Atoms"},{id:"neet-2026-q008",pn:8,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"Ray Optics and Optical Instruments",st:"Apparent depth, refraction at a plane",q:"Consider three media P, Q and R with refractive indices 1, 1.25, and 1.5 respectively. The medium Q having a thickness of 5 cm is placed between extended media P and R as shown in the figure. An object O is placed at the centre of medium Q. If viewed from medium P near the normal direction, the apparent depth of O is h\u2081. For similar observation from medium R, the apparent depth is h\u2082. The value of |h\u2081 \u2013 h\u2082|, in cm, is:",o:["1","2","3","0"],c:0,s:`(i) Object viewed from medium P (real depth = 2.5 cm in Q).
Apparent depth h\u2081 = h_real/\u03BC = 2.5/1.25 = 2 cm.
(ii) Object viewed from medium R.
Apparent depth h\u2082 = (Q\u03BC_R) \xD7 2.5, taking the refractive index of R relative to Q.
h\u2082 = 2.5 \xD7 (1.5/1.25) = 3 cm.
So |h\u2081 \u2013 h\u2082| = 1 cm.`,fig:{w:195,h:200,alt:"Medium Q (n = 1.25, 5 cm thick) between P (n = 1) above and R (n = 1.5) below, with object O in the middle of Q.",d:[["l",10,5,10,60,"d"],["l",185,5,185,60,"d"],["r",10,60,175,70],["r",10,130,175,65,"f"],["t",180,45,"nP = 1","es"],["t",180,122,"nQ = 1.25","es"],["t",180,190,"nR = 1.5","es"],["l",36,80,36,68,"a"],["l",36,110,36,122,"a"],["t",36,100,"5 cm","ms"],["c",96,87,1.5,"F"],["t",86,85,"O","e"]]},sfig:{w:195,h:200,alt:"Medium Q (n = 1.25, 5 cm thick) between P (n = 1) above and R (n = 1.5) below, with object O in the middle of Q.",d:[["l",10,5,10,60,"d"],["l",185,5,185,60,"d"],["r",10,60,175,70],["r",10,130,175,65,"f"],["t",180,45,"nP = 1","es"],["t",180,122,"nQ = 1.25","es"],["t",180,190,"nR = 1.5","es"],["l",36,80,36,68,"a"],["l",36,110,36,122,"a"],["t",36,100,"5 cm","ms"],["c",96,87,1.5,"F"],["t",86,85,"O","e"]]},sub:"Physics",ch:"Ray Optics and Optical Instruments"},{id:"neet-2026-q009",pn:9,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"Units and Measurements",st:"Dimensions of constants",q:"Consider that \u03C3\u209B, k_B, b represents Stefan-Boltzmann constant, Boltzmann constant and Wien\u2019s displacement law constant, respectively. The dimension of \u03C3\u209B k_B\u207B\xB9 b is",o:["[L\u207B\xB9K\u207B\xB2]","[L\u207B\xB9T\u207B\xB3K\u207B\xB3]","[L\u207B\xB9T\u207B\xB9K\u207B\u2074]","[L\u207B\xB9T\u207B\xB9K\u207B\xB2]"],c:3,s:`\u03C3\u209B = MT\u207B\xB3K\u207B\u2074.
k_B = M\xB9L\xB2T\u207B\xB2K\u207B\xB9.
b = M\u2070L\xB9T\u2070K\xB9.
\u03C3\u209B k_B\u207B\xB9 b = [MT\u207B\xB3K\u207B\u2074][M\u207B\xB9L\u207B\xB2T\xB2K][M\u2070LT\u2070K].
= T\u207B\xB9K\u207B\xB2L\u207B\xB9.
= L\u207B\xB9T\u207B\xB9K\u207B\xB2.`,sub:"Physics",ch:"Units and Measurements"},{id:"neet-2026-q010",pn:10,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"Waves",st:"Standing waves in organ pipes",q:"For sound waves, if the number of nodes for the 5th harmonic of an open-ended pipe is n and that for the 9th harmonic of the same pipe with one of its ends closed is m, the ratio n/m is",o:["9/5","1","3/5","5/9"],c:1,s:`Open organ pipe: number of nodes is the same as the number of harmonics.
So n = 5.
Closed organ pipe: only odd harmonics are present.
1st harmonic has 1 node, 3rd has 2 nodes, 5th has 3 nodes, 7th has 4 nodes, 9th has 5 nodes.
So m = 5.
n/m = 5/5 = 1.`,sub:"Physics",ch:"Waves"},{id:"neet-2026-q011",pn:11,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"Moving Charges and Magnetism",st:"Force between parallel current-carrying wires",q:`Two infinitely long parallel conducting wires A and B carry currents I and 2I, respectively, in the same direction. The wire A has uniform mass per unit length \u03BB and lies on an insulated floor. The wire B is kept fixed at a height h above the floor. The minimum magnitude of h so that the wire A does not rise from the floor is:
[g is the acceleration due to gravity and \u03BC\u2080 is the permeability of free space.]`,o:["\u03BC\u2080I\xB2/(\u03C0\u03BBg)","2\u03BC\u2080I\xB2/(\u03C0\u03BBg)","4\u03BC\u2080I\xB2/(\u03C0\u03BBg)","\u03BC\u2080I\xB2/(2\u03C0\u03BBg)"],c:0,s:`Force per unit length on wire A by wire B: F = (\u03BC\u2080/4\u03C0)(2I\xB72I/h) = (\u03BC\u2080/4\u03C0)(4I\xB2/h), as written by the publisher.
If F_net \u2264 mg (weight of wire A per unit length is \u03BBg), the wire does not rise from the floor.
So F \u2264 \u03BBg.
(\u03BC\u2080/4\u03C0)(4I\xB2/h) \u2264 \u03BBg.
Therefore h \u2265 \u03BC\u2080I\xB2/(\u03C0\u03BBg).
Note: Publisher writes force as (\u03BC\u2080/4\u03C0)(2I\xB72I)/h, which is not the product of the currents I and 2I; it simplifies to \u03BC\u2080I\xB2/(\u03C0h) = \u03BC\u2080(I)(2I)/(2\u03C0h), same as the correct value, so answer (1) holds`,sfig:{w:285,h:120,alt:"Wire B above carries 2I to the right; wire A below carries I to the right with mass per unit length \u03BB; a double arrow marks the gap h.",d:[["t",2,24,"wire-B","s"],["l",50,20,260,20,""],["l",215,20,222,20,"a"],["t",205,14,"2I","m"],["t",2,97,"wire-A","s"],["l",50,93,260,93,""],["l",135,93,142,93,"a"],["t",138,108,"I","m"],["t",264,97,"\u03BB",""],["l",95,20,95,93,"b"],["t",101,60,"h",""]]},sub:"Physics",ch:"Moving Charges and Magnetism"},{id:"neet-2026-q012",pn:12,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"Alternating Current",st:"Series LCR resonance",q:`An ac voltage V = 220 sin(2 \xD7 10\xB3t) Volt is applied to a series LCR circuit. Then the current amplitude in this circuit is:
(Given : L = 10 mH, C = 25 \u03BCF, R = 100 \u03A9)`,o:["5.5 A","11.0 A","22.0 A","2.2 A"],c:3,s:`Angular frequency of the source \u03C9 = 2 \xD7 10\xB3 rad/s.
Inductive reactance X_L = \u03C9L = (2 \xD7 10\xB3) \xD7 (10 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3) = 20 \u03A9.
Capacitive reactance X_C = 1/(\u03C9C) = 1/[(2 \xD7 10\xB3) \xD7 (25 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076)] = 20 \u03A9.
X_L = X_C, so the circuit is at resonance and impedance Z = R.
Current amplitude i\u2080 = V\u2080/Z = V\u2080/R = 220/100 = 2.2 A.`,sub:"Physics",ch:"Alternating Current"},{id:"neet-2026-q013",pn:13,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"Electrostatic Potential and Capacitance",st:"Potential energy of point charge",q:`Consider a fixed uniformly charged insulating sphere with radius R and total charge +Q. A point charge \u2013q (q<<Q) with mass m is released from rest at a distance of 3R from the centre of the charged sphere. When the point charge reaches the surface of the sphere, its speed is:
(\u03B5\u2080 is the permittivity of vacuum, neglect gravitational forces).`,o:["\u221A(2Qq/(3\u03C0\u03B5\u2080mR))","\u221A(Qq/(3\u03C0\u03B5\u2080mR))","\u221A(Qq/(4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080mR))","\u221A(3Qq/(4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080mR))"],c:1,s:`The charge starts at A (distance 3R, speed 0) and reaches the surface B (distance R).
Gain in kinetic energy = q \xD7 (potential difference), as the charge is attracted.
(1/2)mv\xB2 = q[KQ/R \u2212 KQ/(3R)].
(1/2)mv\xB2 = (KQq/R)(2/3).
v\xB2 = (KQq \xD7 4)/(3Rm) = (1 \xD7 Qq \xD7 4)/(4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080 \xD7 3Rm) = Qq/(3\u03C0\u03B5\u2080Rm).
v = \u221A(Qq/(3\u03C0\u03B5\u2080mR)).`,fig:{w:350,h:125,alt:"Fixed sphere of radius R with charge +Q; point B on its surface and point A holding charge -q at distance 2R from B on a dotted line, with u = 0 at A.",d:[["c",113,55,48],["p","M65 55 Q113 42 161 55 Q113 68 65 55 Z","f"],["c",113,55,2,"F"],["l",113,55,138,18,"a"],["t",104,36,"R"],["t",75,12,"+Q"],["l",162,55,270,55,"d"],["c",162,55,2,"F"],["c",270,55,2.5,"F"],["t",166,67,"B","s"],["t",274,40,"-q"],["t",274,52,"A"],["l",166,78,268,78,"b"],["t",217,74,"2R","m"],["t",274,82,"(u = 0)"],["t",113,120,"(fixed)","m"]]},sub:"Physics",ch:"Electrostatic Potential and Capacitance"},{id:"neet-2026-q014",pn:14,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"Dual Nature of Radiation and Matter",st:"Photoelectric effect graphs",q:"A beam of light falls on a metal surface such that photo-electrons are generated. If power of the light source starts to decrease linearly with time t, then variation of the photocurrent I and magnitude of the stopping potential |V| with time is best represented by:",o:["I decreases linearly with t (straight line falling to zero); |V| decreases linearly with t","I is constant with t; |V| decreases linearly with t","I is constant with t; |V| is constant with t","I decreases linearly with t; |V| is constant with t"],c:3,s:`Power of the source decreases, so the number of photons per second decreases linearly with time. The source is the same, so the energy of each photon is the same.
Power P \u221D nE, so P \u221D n (number of photons per second).
So photocurrent I decreases linearly with time.
Photon energy is constant, so the stopping potential |V| stays constant with time.`,fig:{w:440,h:195,alt:"Four options, each a pair of graphs of photocurrent I and stopping potential |V| against time t: (1) both fall linearly, (2) I constant and |V| falls linearly, (3) both constant, (4) I falls linearly and |V| is constant.",d:[["t",2,44,"(1)"],["l",42,70,42,8,"a"],["l",42,70,130,70,"a"],["l",42,20,97,70,""],["t",36,25,"I","e s"],["t",82,83,"t","m s"],["l",142,70,142,8,"a"],["l",142,70,230,70,"a"],["l",142,20,197,70,""],["t",136,25,"|V|","e s"],["t",182,83,"t","m s"],["t",212,44,"(2)"],["l",252,70,252,8,"a"],["l",252,70,340,70,"a"],["l",252,20,307,20,""],["t",246,25,"I","e s"],["t",292,83,"t","m s"],["l",352,70,352,8,"a"],["l",352,70,440,70,"a"],["l",352,20,407,70,""],["t",346,25,"|V|","e s"],["t",392,83,"t","m s"],["t",2,144,"(3)"],["l",42,170,42,108,"a"],["l",42,170,130,170,"a"],["l",42,120,97,120,""],["t",36,125,"I","e s"],["t",82,183,"t","m s"],["l",142,170,142,108,"a"],["l",142,170,230,170,"a"],["l",142,120,197,120,""],["t",136,125,"|V|","e s"],["t",182,183,"t","m s"],["t",212,144,"(4)"],["l",252,170,252,108,"a"],["l",252,170,340,170,"a"],["l",252,120,307,170,""],["t",246,125,"I","e s"],["t",292,183,"t","m s"],["l",352,170,352,108,"a"],["l",352,170,440,170,"a"],["l",352,120,407,120,""],["t",346,125,"|V|","e s"],["t",392,183,"t","m s"]]},sfig:{w:260,h:195,alt:"I versus t is a straight line falling to zero; |V| versus t is a horizontal line.",d:[["l",30,80,30,15,"a"],["l",30,80,105,80,"a"],["l",30,28,80,80],["t",24,24,"I","e s"],["t",65,95,"t","m s"],["l",30,165,30,100,"a"],["l",30,165,105,165,"a"],["l",30,112,80,112],["t",24,108,"|V|","e s"],["t",65,180,"t","m s"]]},sub:"Physics",ch:"Dual Nature of Radiation and Matter"},{id:"neet-2026-q015",pn:15,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"Gravitation",st:"Escape speed",q:"Two planets P\u2081 and P\u2082 with equal mass have radii R\u2081 and R\u2082, respectively, where R\u2082 = R\u2081/2. The escape speeds of P\u2081 and P\u2082 are v\u2081 and v\u2082, respectively. Then v\u2082/v\u2081 is:",o:["1","\u221A2","2","1/\u221A2"],c:1,s:`Escape speed v_e = \u221A(2gR) = \u221A(2GM/R).
M is the same for both, so v_e \u221D 1/\u221AR.
v\u2082/v\u2081 = \u221A(R\u2081/R\u2082) = \u221A2.`,sub:"Physics",ch:"Gravitation"},{id:"neet-2026-q016",pn:16,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"Electrostatic Potential and Capacitance",st:"Work done in electric field",q:`A unit positive point charge is taken slowly through an infinitesimally thin tube that is inside a charged dielectric sphere of radius R, having uniform positive charge density \u03C1, as shown in the figure. The initial and final positions of the charge are marked by A and B at distance 2R and 3R respectively, from the centre of the sphere. In this process, the magnitude of the total work done on the point charge is \u03C1R\xB2/(n\u03B5\u2080). The value of n is :
(\u03B5\u2080 is the permittivity of vacuum)`,o:["6","9","18","2"],c:2,s:`Work done W = q(V_B \u2212 V_A).
W = q[KQ/(3R) \u2212 KQ/(2R)] = (Kq/R)(\u2212Q/6).
|W| = KQq/(6R) = (1/(4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080)) \xD7 (q/R) \xD7 (1/6) \xD7 \u03C1 \xD7 (4/3)\u03C0R\xB3.
|W| = \u03C1R\xB2/(18\u03B5\u2080), as q = 1.
So n = 18.`,fig:{w:280,h:125,alt:"Charged sphere of radius R with density rho and centre O; A at distance 2R and B at distance 3R from O; a dashed arrow from A to B passes through the sphere as a thin tube.",d:[["c",159,64,46],["l",159,64,242,34],["l",27,51,159,64],["l",242,34,27,51,"da"],["l",205,37,114,47,"dB"],["c",242,34,2.5,"F"],["c",27,51,2.5,"F"],["c",159,64,1.5,"F"],["t",250,34,"A"],["t",10,66,"B"],["t",76,73,"3R","m"],["t",196,68,"2R"],["t",164,78,"O"],["t",128,84,"\u03C1"]]},sub:"Physics",ch:"Electrostatic Potential and Capacitance"},{id:"neet-2026-q017",pn:17,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"Thermodynamics",st:"Cyclic process heat",q:"One mole of an ideal monatomic gas undergoes a cyclic process as shown in the figure. The total heat supplied to the gas is:",o:["500 J","600 J","800 J","400 J"],c:1,s:`For any cyclic process, the change in internal energy \u0394U = 0.
Total heat exchange \u0394Q = W + \u0394U = W.
W = area of the rectangle = (300 \u2212 100)(5 \u2212 2).
W = 200 \xD7 3 = 600 J.`,fig:{w:300,h:190,alt:"P-V diagram: rectangle a b c d with a at V=2, P=300, b at V=5, P=300, c at V=5, P=100, d at V=2, P=100; the cycle runs a to b to c to d to a, clockwise.",d:[["l",53,160,53,15,"a"],["l",53,160,240,160,"a"],["t",58,12,"P [N/m\xB2]","s"],["t",244,172,"V[m\xB3]","s"],["l",49,67,57,67],["l",49,120,57,120],["t",44,71,"300","e s"],["t",44,124,"100","e s"],["l",98,156,98,164],["l",172,156,172,164],["t",98,177,"2","m s"],["t",172,177,"5","m s"],["r",98,73,75,52],["l",125,73,140,73,"a"],["l",173,92,173,106,"a"],["l",145,125,130,125,"a"],["l",98,110,98,96,"a"],["t",88,70,"a","e s"],["t",180,70,"b","s"],["t",180,138,"c","s"],["t",88,138,"d","e s"]]},sfig:{w:300,h:190,alt:"P-V diagram with dashed guide lines at P = 300 and 100 and V = 2; the rectangular cycle between V = 2 and 5 and P = 100 and 300 is drawn with clockwise arrows.",d:[["l",62,155,62,20,"a"],["l",62,155,245,155,"a"],["t",64,12,"P(N/m\xB2)","s"],["t",250,172,"V (m\xB3)","s"],["l",62,57,115,57,"d"],["l",62,118,115,118,"d"],["l",115,118,115,155,"d"],["l",199,150,199,160],["l",115,57,203,57],["l",203,57,203,118],["l",203,118,115,118],["l",115,118,115,57],["l",150,57,160,57,"a"],["l",203,80,203,92,"a"],["l",165,118,155,118,"a"],["l",115,95,115,82,"a"],["t",52,60,"300","e s"],["t",52,121,"100","e s"],["t",114,172,"2","m s"],["t",199,172,"5","m s"]]},sub:"Physics",ch:"Thermodynamics"},{id:"neet-2026-q018",pn:18,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"Electromagnetic Waves",st:"EM spectrum applications",q:`The following table presents the part of the electromagnetic spectrum and their corresponding major applications.
P. Microwave \u2014 I. For purifying the water
Q. UV rays \u2014 II. For warming the food
R. Gamma rays \u2014 III. For AM and FM communication systems
S. Radio wave \u2014 IV. For treating the Cancer cells
The correct option is:`,o:["P-I, Q-IV, R-II, S-III","P-II, Q-I, R-IV, S-III","P-II, Q-IV, R-III, S-I","P-I, Q-II, R-III, S-IV"],c:1,s:`Microwave is used for warming food.
UV rays are used for purifying water.
Gamma rays are used for treating cancer cells.
Radio waves are used for AM and FM communication systems.
So P-II, Q-I, R-IV, S-III.`,sub:"Physics",ch:"Electromagnetic Waves"},{id:"neet-2026-q019",pn:19,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"Laws of Motion",st:"Banking of roads",q:"A car travels on a circular racetrack of radius 50 m, which is banked at an angle \u03B8. If the car travels at a speed 10 ms\u207B\xB9, then the wear and tear on its tyres is minimum. Taking the acceleration due to gravity to be 10 ms\u207B\xB2, the value of \u03B8 is:",o:["tan\u207B\xB9(2/5)","tan\u207B\xB9(\u221A3/2)","tan\u207B\xB9(2\u221A3)","tan\u207B\xB9(1/5)"],c:3,s:`Wear and tear is minimum when no friction is needed, so tan \u03B8 = v\xB2/(rg).
tan \u03B8 = (10 \xD7 10)/(50 \xD7 10) = 1/5.
\u03B8 = tan\u207B\xB9(1/5).`,sub:"Physics",ch:"Laws of Motion"},{id:"neet-2026-q020",pn:20,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"Motion in a Straight Line",st:"Average speed",q:"Consider a particle moving along a straight line, whose position as a function of time is given by s(t) = \u03B1t\xB2 \u2013 \u03B2t + \u03B3, where \u03B1 = 1 ms\u207B\xB2, \u03B2 = 6 ms\u207B\xB9 and \u03B3 = 5 m. The average speed of the particle, in ms\u207B\xB9 from t = 0 to t = 6 s is:",o:["6","3","0","12"],c:1,s:`Position s = \u03B1t\xB2 \u2212 \u03B2t + \u03B3 = t\xB2 \u2212 6t + 5 \u2026(i).
Velocity v = ds/dt = 2t \u2212 6 \u2026(ii).
v = 0 gives 2t \u2212 6 = 0, so t = 3 s (the particle turns back).
In the v-t graph, v goes from \u22126 at t = 0 to 0 at t = 3 and to +6 at t = 6.
Distance d = A\u2081 + A\u2082 = (1/2) \xD7 3 \xD7 6 + (1/2) \xD7 3 \xD7 6.
d = 9 + 9 = 18 m.
Average speed = distance/time = 18/6 = 3 ms\u207B\xB9.`,sfig:{w:290,h:215,alt:"v-t graph: a straight line from v = -6 at t = 0 through zero at t = 3 to v = 6 at t = 6; area A1 below the axis for 0 to 3 and area A2 above it for 3 to 6.",d:[["l",33,203,33,28,"b"],["l",33,118,270,118,"a"],["l",33,178,138,57],["l",33,57,138,57,"d"],["l",138,57,138,118,"d"],["t",38,26,"v","s"],["t",26,61,"6","e s"],["t",26,122,"0","e s"],["t",26,184,"-6","e s"],["t",274,122,"t","s"],["t",85,134,"3","m s"],["t",138,134,"6","m s"],["t",42,136,"A\u2081","s"],["t",112,104,"A\u2082","s"]]},sub:"Physics",ch:"Motion in a Straight Line"},{id:"neet-2026-q021",pn:21,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"Electrostatic Potential and Capacitance",st:"Electrostatic shielding",q:"A point charge Q is placed inside a cavity within a solid isolated conducting sphere. Consider points A, B and C as shown in the figure, where the magnitudes of the electric fields are E_A, E_B, E_C, respectively. The points B and C are at the same distance from the center of the solid sphere. The correct option is :",o:["E_A \u2260 0, E_B = E_C","E_A = 0, E_B > E_C","E_A \u2260 0, E_B < E_C","E_A = 0, E_B = E_C"],c:0,s:`Due to the charge Q inside the cavity, E_A \u2260 0.
The charge spreads uniformly on the outer surface of the conducting sphere.
B and C are at the same distance from the centre of the sphere.
So E_B = E_C (electrostatic shielding).`,fig:{w:350,h:165,alt:"Solid conducting sphere with an off-centre cavity containing charge Q and point A; points C and B lie outside the sphere on the left and right along a dotted horizontal line.",d:[["l",15,82,338,82,"d"],["c",160,82,75,"f"],["c",193,82,25],["t",178,74,"Q","s"],["c",193,82,1.5,"F"],["t",183,100,"A","s"],["c",198,98,2,"F"],["c",41,82,2.5,"F"],["t",38,70,"C","s"],["c",275,82,2.5,"F"],["t",270,70,"B","s"]]},sfig:{w:300,h:195,alt:"Sphere with minus charges on the cavity wall around Q and plus charges spread uniformly on the outer surface; centre O, points C on the left and B on the right outside on a dotted line.",d:[["l",5,90,295,90,"d"],["c",130,100,75,"f"],["c",166,82,25],["t",162,83,"Q","s"],["t",183,85,"\u2212","s"],["t",145,85,"\u2212","s"],["t",151,72,"\u2212","s"],["t",164,66,"\u2212","s"],["t",177,72,"\u2212","s"],["t",211,103,"+","s"],["t",208,125,"+","s"],["t",200,145,"+","s"],["t",186,162,"+","s"],["t",169,176,"+","s"],["t",149,184,"+","s"],["t",127,187,"+","s"],["t",105,184,"+","s"],["t",85,176,"+","s"],["t",68,162,"+","s"],["t",54,145,"+","s"],["t",46,125,"+","s"],["t",43,103,"+","s"],["t",46,81,"+","s"],["t",54,61,"+","s"],["t",68,44,"+","s"],["t",85,30,"+","s"],["t",105,22,"+","s"],["t",127,19,"+","s"],["t",149,22,"+","s"],["t",169,30,"+","s"],["t",186,44,"+","s"],["t",200,61,"+","s"],["t",208,81,"+","s"],["c",128,90,1.5,"F"],["t",122,102,"O","s"],["t",160,108,"A","s"],["c",33,90,2.5,"F"],["t",29,82,"C","s"],["c",227,90,2.5,"F"],["t",229,82,"B","s"]]},sub:"Physics",ch:"Electrostatic Potential and Capacitance"},{id:"neet-2026-q022",pn:22,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"Mechanical Properties of Fluids",st:"Terminal velocity",q:"In the measurement of viscosity of liquids using terminal velocity experiment, spherical balls of same radius but having different densities are used. The variation of the terminal velocity (v) with the ratio of density of spherical ball (\u03C3) to density of the liquid (\u03C1), is best represented by:",o:["Graph 1: v rises linearly with \u03C3/\u03C1 (dotted extension to the left of \u03C3/\u03C1 = 1)","Graph 2: v rises linearly with \u03C3/\u03C1 (dotted extension to the left of \u03C3/\u03C1 = 1)","Graph 3: v is constant for \u03C3/\u03C1 \u2265 1","Graph 4: v = 0 at \u03C3/\u03C1 = 1 and rises linearly after it"],c:3,s:`Terminal velocity V_T = (2r\xB2/9\u03B7)(\u03C3 \u2212 \u03C1).
V_T = (2r\xB2/9\u03B7) \u03C1 (\u03C3/\u03C1 \u2212 1) \u2026(I).
Compare with y = mx + c \u2026(II).
Equation (I) is a straight line with a negative intercept, so v is zero at \u03C3/\u03C1 = 1 and rises linearly after that.
Note: options 1 and 2 are similar rising lines with dotted extensions; exact positions in dia may be imprecise`,fig:{w:350,h:235,alt:"Four graphs of v against sigma/rho, each with a vertical line at 1: (1) rising line through the origin, dotted before 1; (2) rising line whose dotted part starts on the axis before 1; (3) horizontal line from 1; (4) rising line starting at the axis at 1.",d:[["t",13,55,"(1)"],["l",60,95,60,17,"a"],["l",60,95,153,95,"a"],["l",100,95,100,19],["t",57,13,"v","e s"],["t",58,109,"0","m s"],["t",100,109,"1","m s"],["t",158,99,"\u03C3/\u03C1","s"],["l",60,95,100,73,"d"],["l",100,73,152,46],["t",188,55,"(2)"],["l",235,95,235,17,"a"],["l",235,95,328,95,"a"],["l",275,95,275,19],["t",232,13,"v","e s"],["t",233,109,"0","m s"],["t",275,109,"1","m s"],["t",333,99,"\u03C3/\u03C1","s"],["l",250,95,275,81,"d"],["l",275,81,327,53],["t",13,171,"(3)"],["l",60,211,60,133,"a"],["l",60,211,153,211,"a"],["l",100,211,100,135],["t",57,129,"v","e s"],["t",58,225,"0","m s"],["t",100,225,"1","m s"],["t",158,215,"\u03C3/\u03C1","s"],["l",100,178,153,178],["t",188,171,"(4)"],["l",235,211,235,133,"a"],["l",235,211,328,211,"a"],["l",275,211,275,135],["t",232,129,"v","e s"],["t",233,225,"0","m s"],["t",275,225,"1","m s"],["t",333,215,"\u03C3/\u03C1","s"],["l",275,211,328,178]]},sfig:{w:300,h:140,alt:"V_T against sigma/rho: a straight line rising to the right, dotted where below the horizontal axis, crossing the axis at sigma/rho = 1, with a negative intercept on the V_T axis.",d:[["l",58,100,58,15,"a"],["l",8,60,170,60,"a"],["t",54,12,"V_T","e s"],["t",176,64,"\u03C3/\u03C1","s"],["l",58,92,98,60,"d"],["l",98,60,150,22]]},sub:"Physics",ch:"Mechanical Properties of Fluids"},{id:"neet-2026-q023",pn:23,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"Dual Nature of Radiation and Matter",st:"Photoelectric cells stopping potential",q:"A ray of light with wavelength \u03BB is incident on three different photoelectric cells namely 1, 2 and 3. The threshold wavelength of these photoelectric cells are \u03BB\u2081, \u03BB\u2082, and \u03BB\u2083, respectively and the magnitude of stopping potentials of these cells are V\u2081, V\u2082 and V\u2083, respectively. The relation between \u03BB and threshold wavelengths are \u03BB\u2081 < \u03BB, \u03BB\u2082 > \u03BB and \u03BB\u2083 >> \u03BB. The correct option is:",o:["V\u2081 = 0, V\u2082 > V\u2083","V\u2081 > V\u2082, V\u2083 = 0","V\u2081 < V\u2082, V\u2083 = 0","V\u2081 = 0, V\u2082 < V\u2083"],c:3,s:`An electron is ejected only if the photon energy is greater than the work function, \u03C6 = hc/\u03BB.
For cell 1, \u03BB\u2081 < \u03BB, so \u03C6\u2081 > \u03C6 (work function of cell 1 is greater than the photon energy).
So no electron is ejected and V\u2081 = 0.
Einstein equation: photon energy \u03C6 = \u03C6\u2080 + eV.
For cells 2 and 3: \u03BB\u2082 > \u03BB and \u03BB\u2083 >> \u03BB, so \u03C6 > \u03C6\u2082 and \u03C6 >> \u03C6\u2083 (work functions are smaller than photon energy).
So V\u2083 > V\u2082.`,sub:"Physics",ch:"Dual Nature of Radiation and Matter"},{id:"neet-2026-q024",pn:24,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"Moving Charges and Magnetism",st:"Field at centre of circular arc",q:"A current I\u2080 flows through a metallic circular loop of radius r as shown in the figure. Resistance of the segment ABC is half that of ADC. Magnitude of magnetic field at the centre O of the loop is :",o:["\u03BC\u2080I\u2080/(4r)","\u03BC\u2080I\u2080/(2r)","\u03BC\u2080I\u2080/(2\u03C0r)","\u03BC\u2080I\u2080/(12r)"],c:3,s:`The current I\u2080 enters at A and splits into I\u2081 in segment ABC and I\u2082 in segment ADC.
Both segments are in parallel, so I\u2081R_ABC = I\u2082R_ADC.
R_ABC = R/2, so I\u2081(R/2) = I\u2082R, which gives I\u2081/2 = I\u2082.
Also I\u2081 + I\u2082 = I\u2080, so 2I\u2082 + I\u2082 = I\u2080.
I\u2082 = I\u2080/3 and I\u2081 = 2I\u2080/3.
Each semicircle gives a field \u03BC\u2080I/(4r) at O, but the two fields point in opposite directions (one out of the page, one into the page).
B\u2080 = B\u2081 + B\u2082 = \u03BC\u2080I\u2081/(4r) (out) + \u03BC\u2080I\u2082/(4r) (in)
B\u2080 = \u03BC\u2080\xB72I\u2080/(4r\xD73) \u2212 \u03BC\u2080I\u2080/(4r\xD73) = \u03BC\u2080I\u2080/(12r).`,fig:{w:240,h:200,alt:"Circular loop of radius r with centre O; current I0 enters at A at the top and leaves at C at the bottom; left arc ABC is drawn thick and a dashed radius r points to the lower right.",d:[["c",120,100,60],["p","M120 41 A59 59 0 0 0 120 159","B"],["p","M120 40 A60 60 0 0 0 120 160","B"],["p","M120 39 A61 61 0 0 0 120 161","B"],["l",120,30,120,170,"d"],["l",120,8,120,36,"a"],["l",120,164,120,192,"a"],["t",130,18,"I\u2080"],["t",130,184,"I\u2080"],["t",107,56,"A","m"],["t",107,148,"C","m"],["t",66,104,"B"],["t",162,104,"D"],["c",120,100,2,"F"],["t",104,112,"O"],["l",120,100,162,142,"da"],["t",150,118,"r"]]},sfig:{w:240,h:210,alt:"Loop with current I\u2080 entering at A and leaving at C; the current splits into I\u2081 in the left arc ABC and I\u2082 in the right arc ADC.",d:[["c",120,105,55],["l",120,50,120,160,"d"],["l",120,12,120,48,"a"],["t",128,26,"I\u2080"],["l",120,162,120,198,"a"],["t",128,190,"I\u2080"],["t",104,66,"A"],["t",104,154,"C"],["t",70,110,"B"],["c",120,105,2.5,"F"],["t",106,118,"O"],["t",160,110,"D"],["l",74,64,67,80,"a"],["t",50,66,"I\u2081"],["l",166,64,173,80,"a"],["t",176,66,"I\u2082"]]},sub:"Physics",ch:"Moving Charges and Magnetism"},{id:"neet-2026-q025",pn:25,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"Work, Energy and Power",st:"Work done by variable friction",q:"A particle of mass M moves along a horizontal x axis from x = 0 to x = L. The coefficient of kinetic friction varies as a function of x as \u03BC\u2096(x) = \u03BC\u2080 \u2212 \u03B1x, where \u03BC\u2080, \u03B1 are constants of appropriate dimensions, so that \u03BC\u2096(L) = 0. The total work done by the frictional force during the motion is n\u03BC\u2080MgL, where g is the acceleration due to gravity. The value of n is:",o:["1","1/3","1/2","3"],c:2,s:`Given \u03BC\u2096(x) = \u03BC\u2080 \u2212 \u03B1x and \u03BC\u2096(L) = 0 = \u03BC\u2080 \u2212 \u03B1L, so \u03BC\u2080 = \u03B1L.
Magnitude of work by friction: W_f = \u222BdW_f.
dW_f = f\u2096dx = \u03BC\u2096N dx = \u03BC\u2096Mg dx = (\u03BC\u2080 \u2212 \u03B1x)Mg dx.
W_f = \u222B\u2080\u1D38 \u03BC\u2080Mg dx \u2212 \u222B\u2080\u1D38 \u03B1Mg x dx = \u03BC\u2080Mg[x]\u2080\u1D38 \u2212 \u03B1Mg[x\xB2/2]\u2080\u1D38.
n\u03BC\u2080MgL = \u03BC\u2080MgL \u2212 (\u03BC\u2080/L)Mg(L\xB2/2) = \u03BC\u2080MgL/2.
So n = 1/2.`,sub:"Physics",ch:"Work, Energy and Power"},{id:"neet-2026-q026",pn:26,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"Gravitation",st:"Kepler's law of periods",q:"In a solar system, the time-period of revolution of a planet tracing a circular orbit of radius R is proportional to:",o:["R^(3/2)","R\xB2","R\xB3","R^(1/2)"],c:0,s:`In planetary motion, T\xB2 \u221D R\xB3.
So T \u221D R^(3/2).`,sub:"Physics",ch:"Gravitation"},{id:"neet-2026-q027",pn:27,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"System of Particles and Rotational Motion",st:"Parallel axis theorem",q:"A solid sphere A of radius R and mass M is attached at a point to a smaller solid sphere B of radius r < R and mass m < M. Assume that the line joining their centres lies along the horizontal. The moment of inertia of the system calculated about a vertical axis passing through the centre of A is I_A and that calculated about a vertical axis passing through the centre of B is I_B. The difference I_A \u2212 I_B is:",o:["(m \u2212 M)(R + r)\xB2","(m \u2212 M)(R \u2212 r)\xB2","0","(M \u2212 m)(R + r)\xB2"],c:0,s:`Use I_C = (2/5)mR\xB2 for a solid sphere about its own centre, and the parallel axis theorem.
About the axis through the centre of A: I_A = (2/5)MR\xB2 + (2/5)mr\xB2 + m(R + r)\xB2 ...(1)
About the axis through the centre of B: I_B = (2/5)MR\xB2 + M(R + r)\xB2 + (2/5)mr\xB2 ...(2)
Subtracting (1) and (2): I_A \u2212 I_B = (m \u2212 M)(R + r)\xB2.`,fig:{w:330,h:155,alt:"Two views of a large sphere A touching a smaller sphere B on its right, a dotted line joining the centres; the vertical dashed axis passes through the centre of A and is labelled I_A in the left view, and through the centre of B and is labelled I_B in the right view.",d:[["c",70,80,40],["c",122,80,12],["l",70,80,122,80,"d"],["l",70,15,70,145,"d"],["t",32,52,"A","e"],["t",138,56,"B"],["t",75,22,"I_A"],["c",230,80,40],["c",282,80,12],["l",230,80,282,80,"d"],["l",282,15,282,145,"d"],["t",192,52,"A","e"],["t",298,56,"B"],["t",287,22,"I_B"]]},sfig:{w:330,h:180,alt:"Big sphere M of radius R and small sphere m of radius r touching it; in (1) the vertical dashed axis I_A passes through the centre of M, in (2) the axis I_B passes through the centre of m.",d:[["c",70,80,40],["c",122,80,12],["l",70,80,122,80,"d"],["c",70,80,2,"F"],["l",70,15,70,145,"d"],["t",40,48,"M","e"],["t",138,62,"m"],["t",84,74,"R","s"],["t",118,76,"r","s"],["t",75,22,"I_A"],["t",90,168,"(1)","m"],["c",230,80,40],["c",282,80,12],["l",230,80,282,80,"d"],["c",230,80,2,"F"],["l",282,15,282,145,"d"],["t",200,48,"M","e"],["t",298,62,"m"],["t",244,74,"R","s"],["t",278,76,"r","s"],["t",287,22,"I_B"],["t",250,168,"(2)","m"]]},sub:"Physics",ch:"System of Particles and Rotational Motion"},{id:"neet-2026-q028",pn:28,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"Ray Optics and Optical Instruments",st:"Combination of thin lenses",q:"The lens combination as shown in the figure, consists of two lenses, L\u2081 and L\u2082, of the focal lengths +10 cm and \u221210 cm, respectively. The position of the image formed is:",o:["60 cm to the left of the concave lens","30 cm to the right of the concave lens","60 cm to the right of the concave lens","20 cm to the left of the concave lens"],c:0,s:`For the convex lens L\u2081: u = \u221230 cm and f = +10 cm.
Using 1/v \u2212 1/u = 1/f: 1/v + 1/30 = 1/10.
1/v = 1/10 \u2212 1/30 = 2/30, so v = +15 cm.
This image acts as the object for the concave lens L\u2082, which is 3 cm away, so u\u2032 = +12 cm and f\u2032 = \u221210 cm.
Using 1/v\u2032 \u2212 1/u\u2032 = 1/f\u2032: 1/v\u2032 \u2212 1/12 = \u22121/10.
1/v\u2032 = 1/12 \u2212 1/10 = \u22121/60.
v\u2032 = \u221260 cm.
Final image is formed at 60 cm to the left of the concave lens.`,fig:{w:300,h:165,alt:"Object O (upward arrow) on a dotted principal axis, 30 cm from convex lens L1 which is 3 cm from concave lens L2.",d:[["l",8,70,292,70,"d"],["l",20,70,20,38,"a"],["t",20,86,"O","m"],["p","M200 22 C186 50 186 90 200 118 C214 90 214 50 200 22 Z"],["p","M244 22 H262 C258 50 258 90 262 118 H244 C248 90 248 50 244 22 Z"],["t",200,14,"L\u2081","m"],["t",253,14,"L\u2082","m"],["l",20,135,200,135,"b"],["l",200,135,253,135,"b"],["t",110,154,"30 cm","m"],["t",227,154,"3 cm","m"]]},sub:"Physics",ch:"Ray Optics and Optical Instruments"},{id:"neet-2026-q029",pn:29,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"Semiconductor Electronics",st:"Zener diode in reverse bias",q:"An ideal Zener diode with breakdown voltage of \u22123 V is reverse biased with a negative input voltage V\u1D62 = \u22125 V. The magnitude of voltage difference between point B and A is:",o:["2 V","1 V","0 V","3 V"],c:0,s:`The input voltage magnitude (5 V) is greater than the Zener voltage magnitude (3 V), so the Zener diode is in breakdown.
The voltage across the Zener is V_zener = 3 V.
Total voltage V_total = 5 V.
The rest of the voltage falls across the resistor between B and A.
|V_BA| = 5 \u2212 3 = 2 V.`,fig:{w:200,h:130,alt:"Rectangular circuit: top wire has point C, a Zener diode, point B, resistor R and point A in order; the bottom wire holds a battery.",d:[["p","M20 40 V110 H92"],["p","M108 110 H180 V40 H170"],["l",92,100,92,120],["l",98,104,98,116,"B"],["l",98,110,108,110],["l",20,40,45,40],["l",45,40,60,40],["p","M60 33 L60 47 M60 33 L57 31 M60 47 L63 49"],["p","M60 40 L74 33 L74 47 Z"],["l",74,40,100,40],["p","M100 40 H108 L111 35 L116 45 L121 35 L126 45 L131 35 L134 40 H170"],["t",45,26,"C","m"],["c",45,40,2.5,"F"],["c",100,40,2.5,"F"],["t",100,26,"B","m"],["t",122,26,"R","m"],["c",165,40,2.5,"F"],["t",165,26,"A","m"],["l",165,40,180,40]]},sfig:{w:200,h:130,alt:"Same circuit: top wire C, Zener diode, B, resistor R, A; battery on the bottom wire.",d:[["p","M20 40 V110 H92"],["p","M108 110 H180 V40 H170"],["l",92,100,92,120],["l",98,104,98,116,"B"],["l",98,110,108,110],["l",20,40,45,40],["l",45,40,60,40],["p","M60 33 L60 47 M60 33 L57 31 M60 47 L63 49"],["p","M60 40 L74 33 L74 47 Z"],["l",74,40,100,40],["p","M100 40 H108 L111 35 L116 45 L121 35 L126 45 L131 35 L134 40 H170"],["t",45,26,"C","m"],["c",45,40,2.5,"F"],["c",100,40,2.5,"F"],["t",100,26,"B","m"],["t",122,26,"R","m"],["c",165,40,2.5,"F"],["t",165,26,"A","m"],["l",165,40,180,40]]},sub:"Physics",ch:"Semiconductor Electronics"},{id:"neet-2026-q030",pn:30,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"Electrostatic Potential and Capacitance",st:"Energy stored in capacitors",q:"Three identical capacitors, P, Q and S, each of the capacitance C, are connected to a battery of voltage V, as shown in the figure. If the energy stored in the capacitor P and total energy stored in the system are U_P and U_T, respectively, then the ratio U_P/U_T is:",o:["1/3","1/2","1/6","2/3"],c:2,s:`C_P = C_Q = C_S = C.
P and Q are in series with each other, so each gets V/2.
This series pair is in parallel with S, so S gets the full V.
U_P = (1/2)C(V/2)\xB2 = (1/2)(CV\xB2/4) = CV\xB2/8.
U_T = U_P + U_Q + U_S = (1/2)C(V/2)\xB2 + (1/2)C(V/2)\xB2 + (1/2)CV\xB2
U_T = CV\xB2/8 + CV\xB2/8 + (1/2)CV\xB2 = (6/8)CV\xB2 = (3/4)CV\xB2.
U_P/U_T = (CV\xB2/8)/(3CV\xB2/4) = 1/6.`,fig:{w:260,h:180,alt:"Capacitors P and Q in series on the top branch, capacitor S on a parallel branch below, and a battery V on the bottom wire across the combination.",d:[["l",60,30,97,30],["l",97,22,97,38],["l",103,22,103,38],["l",103,30,167,30],["l",167,22,167,38],["l",173,22,173,38],["l",173,30,200,30],["l",60,30,60,100],["l",200,30,200,100],["l",60,100,127,100],["l",127,92,127,108],["l",133,92,133,108],["l",133,100,200,100],["l",60,68,30,68],["l",30,68,30,150],["l",200,68,230,68],["l",230,68,230,150],["l",30,150,127,150],["l",127,141,127,159],["l",133,145,133,155,"B"],["l",133,150,230,150],["t",100,16,"P","m"],["t",170,16,"Q","m"],["t",140,118,"S"],["t",130,172,"V","m"]]},sfig:{w:260,h:160,alt:"Redrawn circuit: P and Q in series on the top branch, S on the middle branch, battery V on the bottom branch, joined by the left and right vertical wires.",d:[["l",30,30,97,30],["l",97,22,97,38],["l",103,22,103,38],["l",103,30,167,30],["l",167,22,167,38],["l",173,22,173,38],["l",173,30,230,30],["l",30,30,30,130],["l",230,30,230,130],["l",30,80,127,80],["l",127,72,127,88],["l",133,72,133,88],["l",133,80,230,80],["l",30,130,127,130],["l",127,121,127,139],["l",133,125,133,135,"B"],["l",133,130,230,130],["t",100,16,"P","m"],["t",170,16,"Q","m"],["t",140,98,"S"],["t",130,152,"V","m"]]},sub:"Physics",ch:"Electrostatic Potential and Capacitance"},{id:"neet-2026-q031",pn:31,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"Electromagnetic Induction",st:"Induced emf and power in a loop",q:"A conducting loop of finite resistance lies on the x \u2212 y plane. There is a constant magnetic field in the z direction. The area of the loop varies with time t, as A = A\u2080(1 + sin t) in appropriate units. The figure that correctly indicates the qualitative behaviour of the power P dissipated in the loop as a function of time is:",o:["Graph (1): P starts at a non-zero value, falls in a concave-down arc to zero (cusp), then rises again","Graph (2): P is constant (horizontal line)","Graph (3): P rises smoothly and increasingly from zero","Graph (4): P shows two humps, zero at t = 0, a zero in between, and zero at the end"],c:0,s:`A = A\u2080(1 + sin t).
Flux \u03C6 = BA = BA\u2080(1 + sin t).
Emf \u03B5 = \u2212d\u03C6/dt = \u2212BA\u2080 cos t.
I_induced = \u03B5/R = \u2212(BA\u2080/R) cos t.
Power P = I\xB2R = B\xB2A\u2080\xB2cos\xB2t/R.
So P \u221D cos\xB2t: non-zero at t = 0, falls to zero, then rises again (graph 1).`,fig:{w:330,h:240,alt:"Four P versus t graphs: (1) P falls from a non-zero value to zero at a cusp then rises again; (2) P is a constant horizontal line; (3) P rises from zero, increasingly steeply; (4) two humps with zero at the start, between them and at the end.",d:[["l",50,100,50,15,"a"],["l",50,100,170,100,"a"],["t",38,23,"P","e"],["t",174,104,"t"],["t",46,114,"0","e"],["p","M50 45 C80 45 95 65 100 100"],["p","M100 100 C105 70 120 50 150 48"],["t",32,65,"(1)","e"],["l",200,100,200,15,"a"],["l",200,100,320,100,"a"],["t",188,23,"P","e"],["t",324,104,"t"],["t",196,114,"0","e"],["l",200,40,300,40],["t",182,65,"(2)","e"],["l",50,215,50,130,"a"],["l",50,215,170,215,"a"],["t",38,138,"P","e"],["t",174,219,"t"],["t",46,229,"0","e"],["p","M50 215 C100 210 135 195 150 150"],["t",32,180,"(3)","e"],["l",200,215,200,130,"a"],["l",200,215,320,215,"a"],["t",188,138,"P","e"],["t",324,219,"t"],["t",196,229,"0","e"],["p","M200 215 C200 160 240 160 240 215"],["p","M240 215 C240 160 285 160 285 215"],["t",182,180,"(4)","e"]]},sfig:{w:170,h:120,alt:"P versus t graph: P starts at a non-zero value, falls to zero at a cusp, then rises again.",d:[["l",30,100,30,15,"a"],["l",30,100,150,100,"a"],["t",18,23,"P","e"],["t",154,104,"t"],["t",26,114,"0","e"],["p","M30 45 C60 45 75 65 80 100"],["p","M80 100 C85 70 100 50 130 48"]]},sub:"Physics",ch:"Electromagnetic Induction"},{id:"neet-2026-q032",pn:32,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"Current Electricity",st:"Temperature dependence of resistance",q:"Consider two circuits, (A) and (B), each having two resistors. One of them has a positive temperature coefficient of resistance, +\u03B1, while the other one has a negative temperature of coefficient, \u2212\u03B1, as shown in the figure. The current through these circuits are denoted by I_A and I_B. At initial temperature, the resistance of the two resistors is R\u2080. As the temperature is increased, the correct option that describes the variation of current in these circuits is:",o:["I_A decreases while I_B increases","I_A increases while I_B decreases","Both I_A and I_B remain constant","I_A remains constant while I_B increases"],c:3,s:`Circuit A: the two resistors are in series.
R_eq = R\u2080[1 \u2212 \u03B1\u0394T] + R\u2080[1 + \u03B1\u0394T] = 2R\u2080 = constant.
So I_A = V/(2R\u2080) stays constant.
Circuit B: the two resistors are in parallel.
R_eq = R\u2080(1 + \u03B1\u0394T)\xB7R\u2080(1 \u2212 \u03B1\u0394T) / [R\u2080(1 + \u03B1\u0394T) + R\u2080(1 \u2212 \u03B1\u0394T)]
R_eq = R\u2080(1 \u2212 \u03B1\xB2(\u0394T)\xB2)/2.
R_eq decreases with rise in temperature.
So I_B = V/R_eq increases with rise in temperature.`,fig:{w:360,h:185,alt:"Circuit A: battery V with two resistors R0(1 \u2212 \u03B1\u0394T) and R0(1 + \u03B1\u0394T) in series, current I_A. Circuit B: battery V with the same two resistors in parallel branches, current I_B.",d:[["p","M93 85 H10 V135 H190 V85 H107"],["l",97,76,97,94],["l",103,80,103,90,"B"],["t",84,78,"V","e"],["l",150,85,126,85,"a"],["t",140,76,"I_A","m"],["p","M41 135 L45 130 L50 140 L55 130 L60 140 L65 130 L69 135"],["p","M131 135 L135 130 L140 140 L145 130 L150 140 L155 130 L159 135"],["t",55,152,"R\u2080(1 \u2212 \u03B1\u0394T)","m"],["t",145,152,"R\u2080(1 + \u03B1\u0394T)","m"],["t",100,172,"(A)","m"],["p","M283 40 H250 V135 H340 V40 H297"],["l",290,31,290,49],["l",294,35,294,45,"B"],["t",282,32,"V","e"],["l",325,40,305,40,"a"],["t",318,32,"I_B","m"],["l",250,88,340,88],["p","M281 88 L285 83 L290 93 L295 83 L300 93 L305 83 L309 88"],["p","M281 135 L285 130 L290 140 L295 130 L300 140 L305 130 L309 135"],["t",295,107,"R\u2080(1 \u2212 \u03B1\u0394T)","m"],["t",295,152,"R\u2080(1 + \u03B1\u0394T)","m"],["t",295,172,"(B)","m"]]},sfig:{w:360,h:185,alt:"Circuit A with the two temperature-dependent resistors in series and circuit B with them in parallel across battery V.",d:[["p","M93 85 H10 V135 H190 V85 H107"],["l",97,76,97,94],["l",103,80,103,90,"B"],["t",84,78,"V","e"],["l",150,85,126,85,"a"],["t",140,76,"I_A","m"],["p","M41 135 L45 130 L50 140 L55 130 L60 140 L65 130 L69 135"],["p","M131 135 L135 130 L140 140 L145 130 L150 140 L155 130 L159 135"],["t",55,152,"R\u2080(1 \u2212 \u03B1\u0394T)","m"],["t",145,152,"R\u2080(1 + \u03B1\u0394T)","m"],["t",100,172,"(A)","m"],["p","M283 40 H250 V135 H340 V40 H297"],["l",290,31,290,49],["l",294,35,294,45,"B"],["t",282,32,"V","e"],["l",325,40,305,40,"a"],["t",318,32,"I_B","m"],["l",250,88,340,88],["p","M281 88 L285 83 L290 93 L295 83 L300 93 L305 83 L309 88"],["p","M281 135 L285 130 L290 140 L295 130 L300 140 L305 130 L309 135"],["t",295,107,"R\u2080(1 \u2212 \u03B1\u0394T)","m"],["t",295,152,"R\u2080(1 + \u03B1\u0394T)","m"],["t",295,172,"(B)","m"]]},sub:"Physics",ch:"Current Electricity"},{id:"neet-2026-q033",pn:33,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"Work, Energy and Power",st:"Elastic collision and energy conservation",q:"Bob B of mass m at rest is hanging vertically from the ceiling via a massless string of length 10 m, as shown in the figure. Point mass A of mass m travelling horizontally with speed 10 ms\u207B\xB9 hits bob B elastically. The bob B rises h meter after the collision. Taking the acceleration due to gravity g = 10 ms\u207B\xB2 and neglecting the size of the bob, the value of h is:",o:["7","5","2.5","8"],c:1,s:`To reach the horizontal position, the bob would need a speed of 10\u221A2 m/s.
In an elastic collision of equal masses, the velocities are interchanged.
So the bob moves with 10 m/s after the collision.
By conservation of energy: mgh = (1)/(2)mv\xB2.
h = v\xB2/(2g).
h = (10)\xB2/(2 \xD7 10).
h = 5 m.`,fig:{w:300,h:200,alt:"A bob B hangs from a ceiling on a vertical string; point mass A at the same level on the left moves right at 10 ms\u207B\xB9 toward B.",d:[["l",150,15,190,15,"B"],["l",170,15,170,150],["c",170,150,3,"F"],["t",178,148,"B","s"],["c",110,150,3,"F"],["t",106,144,"A","es"],["l",115,150,145,150,"a"],["t",128,172,"10 ms\u207B\xB9","ms"]]},sub:"Physics",ch:"Work, Energy and Power"},{id:"neet-2026-q034",pn:34,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"Oscillations",st:"Floating cylinder oscillation period",q:"A cylindrical cork of uniform density floats in a liquid of density \u03C1\u2081. If the cork is depressed slightly and released, it oscillates harmonically with time period T. If the same cork floats in another liquid of density \u03C1\u2082, then the similar oscillation has time period 2T. The value of \u03C1\u2082 / \u03C1\u2081 is:",o:["2","1/2","1/4","4"],c:2,s:`Time period of a cylinder oscillating in a liquid: T = 2\u03C0\u221A(\u03C1\u209Bl/(\u03C1\u2097g)).
The density \u03C1\u209B of the cork is constant.
So T\u2081/T\u2082 = \u221A(\u03C1\u2082/\u03C1\u2081).
T/(2T) = \u221A(\u03C1\u2082/\u03C1\u2081).
\u03C1\u2082/\u03C1\u2081 = 1/4.`,sub:"Physics",ch:"Oscillations"},{id:"neet-2026-q035",pn:35,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"Kinetic Theory",st:"Mean free path",q:"The mean free path of molecules in an ideal gas A is half that of another ideal gas B. The diameter of the spherical molecules of gas A is twice the diameter of the molecules of B. If number densities of the gases A and B are nA and nB, respectively, the correct option is:",o:["nA = 2nB","nA = (1)/(4)nB","nA = (1)/(2)nB","nA = nB"],c:2,s:`Given \u03BBA = (1)/(2)\u03BBB and dA = 2dB.
Mean free path: \u03BB = 1/(\u221A2 \u03C0 d\xB2 n).
\u03BBA/\u03BBB = (1)/(2) = (dB\xB2 nB)/(dA\xB2 nA).
So nA/nB = 2\xB7dB\xB2/dA\xB2.
Put dA = 2dB: nA/nB = 2dB\xB2/(4dB\xB2) = (1)/(2).`,sub:"Physics",ch:"Kinetic Theory"},{id:"neet-2026-q036",pn:36,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"Nuclei",st:"Q value of nuclear reaction",q:`Consider the following nuclear reaction
\xB2\xB3\u2078U \u2192 \xB2\xB3\u2074Th + \u2074He
Take masses of \xB2\xB3\u2078U, \xB2\xB3\u2074Th and \u2074He as 238.050 u, 234.043 u and 4.003 u, respectively. The Q value for the reaction, in keV, is:
[Given: 1 u = 931.5 MeV c\u207B\xB2]`,o:["3730","3736","3740","3726"],c:3,s:`Q-value of a nuclear reaction is Q = \u0394mc\xB2.
Mass defect \u0394m = m(reactants) \u2212 m(products).
\u0394m = 238.050 \u2212 (234.043 + 4.003) = 0.004 u.
Q = 0.004 \xD7 931.5 MeV = 3.726 MeV = 3726 keV.`,sub:"Physics",ch:"Nuclei"},{id:"neet-2026-q037",pn:37,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"Electromagnetic Induction",st:"Self inductance of solenoid",q:"Consider a long solenoid of length l and radius r. If n is the number of turns per unit length and \u03BC\u2080 is the permeability of free space, the inductance of the solenoid is :",o:["\u03BC\u2080n\xB2r\xB2l","(\u03BC\u2080/2\u03C0)n\xB2r\xB2l","2\u03BC\u2080\u03C0n\xB2r\xB2l","\u03BC\u2080\u03C0n\xB2r\xB2l"],c:3,s:`Self inductance of solenoid: L = N\u03C6/i.
\u03C6 = BA, so L = NBA/i.
B = \u03BC\u2080ni, so L = N\u03BC\u2080niA/i, where n = N/l.
L = N\u03BC\u2080nA.
L = (N/l)\u03BC\u2080nAl.
L = \u03BC\u2080n\xB2Al.
Since A = \u03C0r\xB2, L = \u03BC\u2080n\xB2\u03C0r\xB2l.`,sub:"Physics",ch:"Electromagnetic Induction"},{id:"neet-2026-q038",pn:38,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"Ray Optics and Optical Instruments",st:"Index correction in optical bench",q:"Which of the following measurements require \u2018index correction\u2019?",o:["Measurement of gravitational acceleration using simple pendulum","Measurement of focal length of lenses using optical bench","Measurement of speed of sound using resonance tube","Measurement of resistance of a wire using meter bridge"],c:1,s:`Index correction is an adjustment needed in experiments done on an optical bench.
It is applied while measuring the focal length of lenses using the optical bench.`,sub:"Physics",ch:"Ray Optics and Optical Instruments"},{id:"neet-2026-q039",pn:39,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"Thermal Properties of Matter",st:"Thermal expansion of volume",q:"The temperature of a metallic sphere of radius R is increased by a small amount \u0394T. If the linear coefficient of thermal expansion of the metal is \u03B1, the approximate increase in the volume of the sphere is:",o:["3\u03C0R\xB3\u03B1\u0394T","4\u03C0R\xB3\u03B1\u0394T","6\u03C0R\xB3\u03B1\u0394T","2\u03C0R\xB3\u03B1\u0394T"],c:1,s:`For the metallic sphere, \u0394R/R = \u03B1\u0394T.
Volume of the sphere V = (4)/(3)\u03C0R\xB3.
So \u0394V/V = 3\u0394R/R.
Increase in volume \u0394V = 3(\u0394R/R)((4)/(3)\u03C0R\xB3) = 4\u03C0R\xB3\u03B1\u0394T.`,sub:"Physics",ch:"Thermal Properties of Matter"},{id:"neet-2026-q040",pn:40,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"Dual Nature of Radiation and Matter",st:"Momentum of photon and electron",q:`A photon and an electron, each of 20 eV energy, move in free space. The ratio of linear momentum of electron p\u2091 to that of photon pPh, p\u2091/pPh is:
[Take speed of light = 3 \xD7 10\u2078 ms\u207B\xB9, charge of electron = \u20131.6 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2079 C and mass of electron = 9 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3\xB9 kg]`,o:["1/250","225","275","2/450"],c:1,s:`E\u2091 = EPh = 20 eV.
For the electron p\u2091 = \u221A(2m\u2091E\u2091), for the photon pPh = EPh/c.
p\u2091/pPh = (\u221A(2m\u2091E\u2091)/EPh)\xB7c = \u221A(2m\u2091/EPh)\xB7c.
p\u2091/pPh = \u221A((2 \xD7 9 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3\xB9)/(20 \xD7 1.6 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2079)) \xD7 3 \xD7 10\u2078.
= (3/4) \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 \xD7 3 \xD7 10\u2078.
= 900/4 = 225.`,sub:"Physics",ch:"Dual Nature of Radiation and Matter"},{id:"neet-2026-q041",pn:41,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"Atoms",st:"Bohr orbit radius",q:`Consider that an electron is revolving in an excited state of Hydrogen atom with velocity \u221A(25.6) \xD7 10\u2075 ms\u207B\xB9. The radius of the orbit is x \xD7 10\u207B\u2079 m. The value of x is:
[Take the mass of the electron to the 9 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3\xB9 kg, charge of electron = \u20131.6 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2079 C and 1/(4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080) = 9 \xD7 10\u2079 Nm\xB2C\u207B\xB2]`,o:["3","2","1","4"],c:2,s:`Centripetal force Fc = mv\xB2/r.
So kq\u2081q\u2082/r\xB2 = mv\xB2/r.
ke\xB2/r = mv\xB2, so r = ke\xB2/(mv\xB2).
r = (9 \xD7 10\u2079 \xD7 (1.6 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2079)\xB2)/(9 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3\xB9 \xD7 25.6 \xD7 10\xB9\u2070).
r = (2.56 \xD7 10\u2079 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3\u2078)/(25.6 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2\xB9).
r = 1 \xD7 10\u207B\u2079 m.
So x = 1.`,sub:"Physics",ch:"Atoms"},{id:"neet-2026-q042",pn:42,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"Electromagnetic Induction",st:"Inductors in series and parallel",q:`Two identical inductors are connected in two different configurations P and Q, where a time varying current I(t) is flowing, as shown in the figure. The induced emf between points a and b for configuration P is EP and that for configuration Q is EQ. The ratio EP/EQ is:
[Neglect the effect of mutual inductance.]`,o:["1/2","1","2","1/4"],c:2,s:`In figure (P) the two inductors are in series, so the inductance between a and b is that of one inductor L.
EP = Eab = \u2212L dI(t)/dt.
In figure (Q) the two inductors are in parallel, giving L/2.
EQ = Eab = \u2212(L/2) dI(t)/dt.
EP/EQ = 2/1.`,fig:{w:300,h:230,alt:"Two identical coils: (P) in series with a at the top and b between them; (Q) in parallel between a at the top and b at the bottom, with current I(t) entering at the top.",d:[["l",30,20,70,20,"a"],["t",30,14,"I(t)","s"],["l",70,20,70,40],["t",76,34,"a","s"],["p","M70 40 C79 40 79 48.57142857142857 70 48.57142857142857 C79 48.57142857142857 79 57.14285714285714 70 57.14285714285714 C79 57.14285714285714 79 65.71428571428572 70 65.71428571428572 C79 65.71428571428572 79 74.28571428571428 70 74.28571428571428 C79 74.28571428571428 79 82.85714285714286 70 82.85714285714286 C79 82.85714285714286 79 91.42857142857143 70 91.42857142857143 C79 91.42857142857143 79 100 70 100"],["l",70,100,70,110],["c",70,110,2,"F"],["t",76,114,"b","s"],["l",70,110,70,120],["p","M70 120 C79 120 79 128.57142857142858 70 128.57142857142858 C79 128.57142857142858 79 137.14285714285714 70 137.14285714285714 C79 137.14285714285714 79 145.71428571428572 70 145.71428571428572 C79 145.71428571428572 79 154.28571428571428 70 154.28571428571428 C79 154.28571428571428 79 162.85714285714286 70 162.85714285714286 C79 162.85714285714286 79 171.42857142857144 70 171.42857142857144 C79 171.42857142857144 79 180 70 180"],["l",70,180,70,196],["l",30,196,70,196],["t",70,214,"(P)","ms"],["l",140,20,210,20,"a"],["t",140,14,"I(t)","s"],["l",210,20,210,40],["t",216,34,"a","s"],["l",180,40,240,40],["l",180,40,180,60],["l",240,40,240,60],["p","M180 60 C189 60 189 71.42857142857143 180 71.42857142857143 C189 71.42857142857143 189 82.85714285714286 180 82.85714285714286 C189 82.85714285714286 189 94.28571428571428 180 94.28571428571428 C189 94.28571428571428 189 105.71428571428572 180 105.71428571428572 C189 105.71428571428572 189 117.14285714285714 180 117.14285714285714 C189 117.14285714285714 189 128.57142857142856 180 128.57142857142856 C189 128.57142857142856 189 140 180 140"],["p","M240 60 C249 60 249 71.42857142857143 240 71.42857142857143 C249 71.42857142857143 249 82.85714285714286 240 82.85714285714286 C249 82.85714285714286 249 94.28571428571428 240 94.28571428571428 C249 94.28571428571428 249 105.71428571428572 240 105.71428571428572 C249 105.71428571428572 249 117.14285714285714 240 117.14285714285714 C249 117.14285714285714 249 128.57142857142856 240 128.57142857142856 C249 128.57142857142856 249 140 240 140"],["l",180,140,180,160],["l",240,140,240,160],["l",180,160,240,160],["l",210,160,210,170],["c",210,160,2,"F"],["t",216,176,"b","s"],["l",150,172,210,172],["t",210,192,"(Q)","ms"]]},sfig:{w:420,h:230,alt:"(P) coils in series each L with a and b; (Q) two parallel coils each L between a and b, reduced by an arrow to one coil L/2 between a and b.",d:[["l",20,20,60,20,"a"],["t",20,14,"I(t)","s"],["l",60,20,60,40],["t",66,34,"a","s"],["p","M60 40 C69 40 69 48.57142857142857 60 48.57142857142857 C69 48.57142857142857 69 57.14285714285714 60 57.14285714285714 C69 57.14285714285714 69 65.71428571428572 60 65.71428571428572 C69 65.71428571428572 69 74.28571428571428 60 74.28571428571428 C69 74.28571428571428 69 82.85714285714286 60 82.85714285714286 C69 82.85714285714286 69 91.42857142857143 60 91.42857142857143 C69 91.42857142857143 69 100 60 100"],["l",60,100,60,110],["c",60,110,2,"F"],["t",66,114,"b","s"],["l",60,110,60,120],["p","M60 120 C69 120 69 128.57142857142858 60 128.57142857142858 C69 128.57142857142858 69 137.14285714285714 60 137.14285714285714 C69 137.14285714285714 69 145.71428571428572 60 145.71428571428572 C69 145.71428571428572 69 154.28571428571428 60 154.28571428571428 C69 154.28571428571428 69 162.85714285714286 60 162.85714285714286 C69 162.85714285714286 69 171.42857142857144 60 171.42857142857144 C69 171.42857142857144 69 180 60 180"],["l",60,180,60,196],["l",20,196,60,196],["t",60,214,"(P)","ms"],["t",74,74,"L","s"],["t",74,154,"L","s"],["l",130,20,200,20,"a"],["t",130,14,"I(t)","s"],["l",200,20,200,40],["t",206,34,"a","s"],["l",170,40,230,40],["l",170,40,170,60],["l",230,40,230,60],["p","M170 60 C179 60 179 71.42857142857143 170 71.42857142857143 C179 71.42857142857143 179 82.85714285714286 170 82.85714285714286 C179 82.85714285714286 179 94.28571428571428 170 94.28571428571428 C179 94.28571428571428 179 105.71428571428572 170 105.71428571428572 C179 105.71428571428572 179 117.14285714285714 170 117.14285714285714 C179 117.14285714285714 179 128.57142857142856 170 128.57142857142856 C179 128.57142857142856 179 140 170 140"],["p","M230 60 C239 60 239 71.42857142857143 230 71.42857142857143 C239 71.42857142857143 239 82.85714285714286 230 82.85714285714286 C239 82.85714285714286 239 94.28571428571428 230 94.28571428571428 C239 94.28571428571428 239 105.71428571428572 230 105.71428571428572 C239 105.71428571428572 239 117.14285714285714 230 117.14285714285714 C239 117.14285714285714 239 128.57142857142856 230 128.57142857142856 C239 128.57142857142856 239 140 230 140"],["l",170,140,170,160],["l",230,140,230,160],["l",170,160,230,160],["l",200,160,200,170],["c",200,160,2,"F"],["t",206,176,"b","s"],["l",140,172,200,172],["t",200,192,"(Q)","ms"],["t",158,104,"L","s"],["t",242,104,"L","s"],["t",262,110,"\u21D2","m"],["l",290,20,340,20,"a"],["t",290,14,"I(t)","s"],["l",340,20,340,40],["t",346,34,"a","s"],["p","M340 40 C349 40 349 57.14285714285714 340 57.14285714285714 C349 57.14285714285714 349 74.28571428571428 340 74.28571428571428 C349 74.28571428571428 349 91.42857142857143 340 91.42857142857143 C349 91.42857142857143 349 108.57142857142857 340 108.57142857142857 C349 108.57142857142857 349 125.71428571428571 340 125.71428571428571 C349 125.71428571428571 349 142.85714285714286 340 142.85714285714286 C349 142.85714285714286 349 160 340 160"],["t",354,104,"L/2","s"],["l",340,160,340,172],["c",340,168,2,"F"],["t",346,166,"b","s"],["l",300,172,340,172]]},sub:"Physics",ch:"Electromagnetic Induction"},{id:"neet-2026-q043",pn:43,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"Thermodynamics",st:"Work in adiabatic process",q:`In an adiabatic expansion, the temperature of one mole of an ideal monatomic gas (\u03B3 = 5/3) decreases from 60 K to 50 K. The work done by the gas in the process is:
(Take the universal gas constant as R = 8.3 J mol\u207B\xB9 K\u207B\xB9)`,o:["83 J","124.5 J","166 J","41.5 J"],c:1,s:`Work done in an adiabatic process: W = nR\u0394T/(1 \u2212 \u03B3).
\u0394T = \u221210 K.
W = (1 \xD7 8.3 \xD7 (\u221210))/(1 \u2212 5/3).
W = (83 \xD7 3)/2.
W = 124.5 J.`,sub:"Physics",ch:"Thermodynamics"},{id:"neet-2026-q044",pn:44,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"Mechanical Properties of Fluids",st:"Bernoulli's equation and continuity",q:`Water flows in a streamline motion through a horizontal pipe of circular cross-section as shown in the figure. The pressure difference of water between P and Q is 15 Nm\u207B\xB2. The area of cross-section at P and Q are 40 cm\xB2 and 20 cm\xB2, respectively. The rate of flow of water through the pipe, in cm\xB3s\u207B\xB9, is:
[Take density of water = 1000 kg m\u207B\xB3]`,o:["200","300","400","100"],c:2,s:`By continuity: AP VP = AQ VQ.
40 VP = 20 VQ, so VQ = 2VP.
Using Bernoulli's equation: PP \u2212 PQ = (1)/(2)\u03C1(VQ\xB2 \u2212 VP\xB2).
15 = (1)/(2) \xD7 10\xB3 \xD7 (4VP\xB2 \u2212 VP\xB2).
VP = \u221A(15/1500) = 0.1 m/s.
VQ = 0.2 m/s.
Rate = AP VP = 40 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9 m\xB3/s.
= 4 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 m\xB3/s.
= 4 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 \xD7 10\u2076 cm\xB3/s = 400 cm\xB3/s.`,fig:{w:300,h:100,alt:"A horizontal pipe, wide at the left with point P, narrowing at a constriction with point Q, and widening again to the right.",d:[["l",10,30,60,30],["p","M60 30 C90 30 100 40 130 40 C160 40 170 30 200 30"],["l",200,30,290,30],["l",10,70,60,70],["p","M60 70 C90 70 100 60 130 60 C160 60 170 70 200 70"],["l",200,70,290,70],["l",10,50,290,50],["t",30,22,"P","s"],["t",125,34,"Q","s"]]},sub:"Physics",ch:"Mechanical Properties of Fluids"},{id:"neet-2026-q045",pn:45,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"System of Particles and Rotational Motion",st:"Angular momentum of rigid body",q:"A thin horizontal disc is rotating about a vertical axis passing through its fixed centre O. Its angular momentum is LA and LB computed about points A and B, respectively, with OB = 2 \xD7 OA. The value of LA/LB is:",o:["1/2","1","2","1/4"],c:1,s:`Angular momentum: L = r \xD7 P + Icm \u03C9.
The disc rotates about its fixed centre, so the momentum P of the centre of mass is 0 for both points.
So LA = LB = Icm \u03C9.
LA/LB = 1.`,fig:{w:300,h:170,alt:"A disc with centre O and a rotation arrow near its upper left; along a dotted line from O, point A lies on the rim and point B lies outside, further right.",d:[["c",90,85,60],["c",90,85,2,"F"],["t",86,112,"O","s"],["l",90,85,150,85,"d"],["c",150,85,2,"F"],["t",147,112,"A","s"],["l",150,85,230,85,"d"],["c",230,85,2,"F"],["t",228,112,"B","s"],["p","M68 52 Q56 60 52 72 M52 72 L52 62 M52 72 L62 70"]]},sub:"Physics",ch:"System of Particles and Rotational Motion"},{id:"neet-2026-q046",pn:46,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure",st:"VSEPR shapes hybridization",q:`Match the species in List-I with their geometry in List-II.
A. PCl\u2085 \u2014 I. Tetrahedral
B. BrF\u2085 \u2014 II. Square Planar
C. BF\u2084\u207B \u2014 III. Trigonal bipyramidal
D. [Ni(CN)\u2084]\xB2\u207B \u2014 IV. Square pyramidal
Choose the correct answer from the options given below:`,o:["A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-III, B-I, C-II, D-IV","A-III, B-II, C-I, D-IV","A-IV, B-III, C-I, D-II"],c:0,s:`Find the hybridization of the central atom, then the shape.
A. PCl\u2085: sp\xB3d, trigonal bipyramidal.
B. BrF\u2085: sp\xB3d\xB2, square pyramidal.
C. BF\u2084\u207B: sp\xB3, tetrahedral.
D. [Ni(CN)\u2084]\xB2\u207B: dsp\xB2, square planar.
So A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II.`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure"},{id:"neet-2026-q047",pn:47,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques",st:"Lassaigne's test for elements",q:"The amino acid that gives a red-blood colour on treating its sodium fusion extract with sodium nitroprusside is",o:["threonine","methionine","serine","leucine"],c:1,s:`If an organic compound has both nitrogen and sulphur, the sodium fusion extract gives a blood-red colour with sodium nitroprusside.
Among the options only methionine has both N and S.
Methionine: H\u2082N\u2013CH(COOH)\u2013CH\u2082CH\u2082SCH\u2083.`,sfig:{w:260,h:104,alt:"Fischer-type structure of methionine: COOH at the top, H\u2082N on the left, H on the right and CH\u2082CH\u2082SCH\u2083 at the bottom of the central carbon.",d:[["t",10,60,"\u2234 Methionine"],["l",160,32,160,80],["t",160,26,"COOH","m"],["l",134,56,186,56],["t",132,60,"H\u2082N","e"],["t",188,60,"H"],["t",160,94,"CH\u2082CH\u2082SCH\u2083","m"]]},sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques"},{id:"neet-2026-q048",pn:48,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"Amines",st:"Basicity of substituted anilines",q:`Given below are two statements:
Statement-I: Oxidation of p-nitrotoluene with acidic KMnO\u2084 gives an acid that is stronger than benzoic acid.
Statement-II: Reduction of p-nitrotoluene with Sn/HCl followed by neutralization gives an amine that is more basic than aniline.
In light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below.`,o:["Both Statement-I and Statement-II are incorrect","Statement-I is correct but Statement-II is incorrect","Statement-I is incorrect but Statement-II is correct","Both Statement-I and Statement-II are correct"],c:3,s:`Acidic KMnO\u2084 oxidises the \u2013CH\u2083 group of p-nitrotoluene to \u2013COOH, giving p-nitrobenzoic acid.
The \u2013NO\u2082 group has \u2013I and \u2013M effects, so p-nitrobenzoic acid is a stronger acid than benzoic acid. Statement-I is correct.
Sn/HCl reduces \u2013NO\u2082 to \u2013NH\u2082, giving p-toluidine (p-methylaniline).
The \u2013CH\u2083 group has +I effect and hyperconjugation, so p-methylaniline is more basic than aniline. Statement-II is correct.`,sfig:{w:260,h:106,alt:"Two benzene rings: p-nitrobenzoic acid with COOH at the top and NO\u2082 at the bottom; p-methylaniline (p-toluidine) with CH\u2083 at the top and NH\u2082 at the bottom.",d:[["p","M70.0 47.0 L81.3 53.5 L81.3 66.5 L70.0 73.0 L58.7 66.5 L58.7 53.5 Z"],["c",70,60,7.15],["l",70,47,70,36],["t",70,31,"COOH","m"],["l",70,73,70,84],["t",70,97,"NO\u2082","m"],["p","M190.0 47.0 L201.3 53.5 L201.3 66.5 L190.0 73.0 L178.7 66.5 L178.7 53.5 Z"],["c",190,60,7.15],["l",190,47,190,36],["t",190,31,"CH\u2083","m"],["l",190,73,190,84],["t",190,97,"NH\u2082","m"]]},sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Amines"},{id:"neet-2026-q049",pn:49,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"Electrochemistry",st:"Electrode potential from Gibbs energy",q:`The standard electrode potential (E\xB0) for the half-cell reaction Fe\xB3\u207A + e\u207B \u2192 Fe\xB2\u207A at 298 K is
(Given : E\xB0(Fe\xB3\u207A/Fe) = \u20130.04 V and E\xB0(Fe\xB2\u207A/Fe) = \u20130.44 V at 298 K)`,o:["+0.76 V","\u20130.48 V","+0.92 V","+0.40 V"],c:0,s:`Fe\xB3\u207A \u2192 Fe (3 electrons, \u20130.04 V) is the sum of Fe\xB3\u207A \u2192 Fe\xB2\u207A (1 electron, E\xB0 unknown) and Fe\xB2\u207A \u2192 Fe (2 electrons, \u20130.44 V).
So \u0394G\xB0(Fe\xB3\u207A/Fe) = \u0394G\xB0(Fe\xB3\u207A/Fe\xB2\u207A) + \u0394G\xB0(Fe\xB2\u207A/Fe).
Use \u0394G\xB0 = \u2013nFE\xB0: \u20133F(\u20130.04) = \u20131F(E\xB0) + (\u20132F)(\u20130.44).
0.12 = \u2013E\xB0 + 0.88.
E\xB0 = 0.88 \u2013 0.12 = 0.76 V.`,sfig:{w:240,h:116,alt:"Cycle: Fe\xB3\u207A to Fe\xB2\u207A with E\xB0 marked ?, Fe\xB2\u207A to Fe marked \u20130.44 V, and Fe\xB3\u207A directly to Fe marked \u20130.04 V.",d:[["t",30,66,"Fe\xB3\u207A"],["l",64,62,104,62,"a"],["t",110,66,"Fe\xB2\u207A"],["l",144,62,184,62,"a"],["t",190,66,"Fe"],["p","M42 52 V28 H120 V50","a"],["t",81,22,"?","m"],["p","M134 52 V28 H198 V50","a"],["t",166,22,"\u20130.44 V","m"],["p","M42 70 V92 H198 V72","a"],["t",120,108,"\u20130.04 V","m"]]},sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Electrochemistry"},{id:"neet-2026-q050",pn:50,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"Aldehydes, Ketones and Carboxylic Acids",st:"Aldol condensation products",q:`The compound that CANNOT be obtained from the aldol condensation reaction shown below, is
2,2-dimethylcyclopentanone + PhCHO (NaOH, \u0394) \u2192`,o:["2,2-dimethylcyclopentanone with =CH\u2013Ph on the alpha carbon","two 2,2-dimethylcyclopentanone units joined by a C=C between their alpha carbons (alkylidene)","2,2-dimethylcyclopentanone with =CH\u2013Ph at the alpha carbon (drawn without the E/Z bond detail)","2,2-dimethylcyclopentanone linked by a single bond from its alpha carbon to a cyclopentene ring carrying gem-dimethyl"],c:3,s:`The ketone has alpha hydrogens on one side only (the other alpha carbon is C(CH\u2083)\u2082).
With PhCHO it gives the alpha,beta-unsaturated ketone =CH\u2013Ph (E and Z isomers) and water.
It can also react with another molecule of the ketone to give the C=C linked dimer (alpha,beta-unsaturated carbonyl).
Option 4 has a single bond to a cyclopentene ring, which cannot form by this reaction.
Note: Publisher solution text says 'compound 2 cannot be obtained', but its own drawn product matches option 2 (obtainable); printed answer is (4), kept. Option texts are my descriptions of drawn structures.`,fig:{w:360,h:240,alt:"Reaction: 2,2-dimethylcyclopentanone + PhCHO with NaOH, \u0394. Options: (1) the ketone with =CH\u2013Ph on its other alpha carbon; (2) the ketone joined by a C=C to a second ring carrying H\u2083C and CH\u2083; (3) the ketone with =CH\u2013Ph, Ph drawn upward; (4) the ketone joined by a single bond to a cyclopentene ring carrying H\u2083C and CH\u2083.",d:[["p","M60.0 27.0 L72.4 36.0 L67.6 50.5 L52.4 50.5 L47.6 36.0 Z"],["l",60,27,60,14],["l",63,26,63,15],["t",61.5,11,"O","ms"],["l",47.6,36,35.6,28],["l",47.6,36,35.6,44],["t",34.6,28,"H\u2083C","es"],["t",34.6,49,"H\u2083C","es"],["t",92,48,"+ PhCHO"],["l",150,44,200,44,"a"],["t",175,38,"NaOH, \u0394","ms"],["p","M80.0 99.0 L92.4 108.0 L87.6 122.5 L72.4 122.5 L67.6 108.0 Z"],["l",80,99,80,86],["l",83,98,83,87],["t",81.5,83,"O","ms"],["l",67.6,108,55.6,100],["l",67.6,108,55.6,116],["t",54.6,100,"H\u2083C","es"],["t",54.6,121,"H\u2083C","es"],["t",4,116,"(1)"],["l",92.4,108,106.4,102],["l",95.2,110,105.9,105.5],["l",106.4,102,116.4,102],["t",118.4,106,"Ph"],["p","M250.0 99.0 L262.4 108.0 L257.6 122.5 L242.4 122.5 L237.6 108.0 Z"],["l",250,99,250,86],["l",253,98,253,87],["t",251.5,83,"O","ms"],["l",237.6,108,225.6,100],["l",237.6,108,225.6,116],["t",224.6,100,"H\u2083C","es"],["t",224.6,121,"H\u2083C","es"],["t",174,116,"(2)"],["l",262.4,108,276.4,108],["l",264,105,274.7,105],["p","M276.4 108.0 L285.3 95.6 L299.9 100.3 L299.9 115.6 L285.3 120.3 Z"],["l",285.3,120.3,276.3,131.3],["l",285.3,120.3,294.3,131.3],["t",275.3,141.3,"H\u2083C","ms"],["t",297.3,141.3,"CH\u2083","ms"],["p","M80.0 177.0 L92.4 186.0 L87.6 200.5 L72.4 200.5 L67.6 186.0 Z"],["l",80,177,80,164],["l",83,176,83,165],["t",81.5,161,"O","ms"],["l",67.6,186,55.6,178],["l",67.6,186,55.6,194],["t",54.6,178,"H\u2083C","es"],["t",54.6,199,"H\u2083C","es"],["t",4,194,"(3)"],["l",92.4,186,106.4,190],["l",94.9,183.6,105.5,186.6],["l",106.4,190,108.4,176],["t",104.4,173,"Ph"],["p","M250.0 177.0 L262.4 186.0 L257.6 200.5 L242.4 200.5 L237.6 186.0 Z"],["l",250,177,250,164],["l",253,176,253,165],["t",251.5,161,"O","ms"],["l",237.6,186,225.6,178],["l",237.6,186,225.6,194],["t",224.6,178,"H\u2083C","es"],["t",224.6,199,"H\u2083C","es"],["t",174,194,"(4)"],["l",262.4,186,276.4,186],["p","M276.4 186.0 L285.3 173.6 L299.9 178.3 L299.9 193.6 L285.3 198.3 Z"],["l",279.6,186,286.4,176.7],["l",285.3,198.3,276.3,209.3],["l",285.3,198.3,294.3,209.3],["t",275.3,219.3,"H\u2083C","ms"],["t",297.3,219.3,"CH\u2083","ms"]]},sfig:{w:360,h:296,alt:"Two reactions with dil. NaOH, \u0394: the ketone (two \u03B1-H shown) + O=CH\u2013Ph gives H\u2082O + the =CH\u2013Ph product (E + Z isomers); the ketone + a second ketone molecule gives the ring=ring \u03B1,\u03B2-unsaturated carbonyl compound.",d:[["p","M50.0 27.0 L62.4 36.0 L57.6 50.5 L42.4 50.5 L37.6 36.0 Z"],["l",50,27,50,14],["l",53,26,53,15],["t",51.5,11,"O","ms"],["l",37.6,36,25.6,28],["l",37.6,36,25.6,44],["l",62.4,36,70.4,29],["t",71.4,29,"H","s"],["l",62.4,36,70.4,43],["t",71.4,50,"H","s"],["t",82,46,"+"],["t",100,46,"O","m"],["l",106,40,116,40],["l",106,44,116,44],["t",121,46,"C","m"],["l",126,38,136,30],["t",138,30,"Ph","s"],["l",126,46,136,54],["t",138,62,"H","s"],["l",158,42,212,42,"a"],["t",185,35,"dil. NaOH","ms"],["t",185,57,"\u0394","ms"],["t",220,46,"H\u2082O +"],["p","M250.0 83.0 L262.4 92.0 L257.6 106.5 L242.4 106.5 L237.6 92.0 Z"],["l",250,83,250,70],["l",253,82,253,71],["t",251.5,67,"O","ms"],["l",237.6,92,225.6,84],["l",237.6,92,225.6,100],["l",262.4,92,275.4,92],["l",263.9,89,273.8,89],["t",277.4,96,"CH\u2013Ph"],["t",250,130,"(E + Z isomers)","ms"],["p","M50.0 161.0 L62.4 170.0 L57.6 184.5 L42.4 184.5 L37.6 170.0 Z"],["l",50,161,50,148],["l",53,160,53,149],["t",51.5,145,"O","ms"],["l",37.6,170,25.6,162],["l",37.6,170,25.6,178],["l",62.4,170,70.4,163],["t",71.4,163,"H","s"],["l",62.4,170,70.4,177],["t",71.4,184,"H","s"],["t",82,180,"+"],["t",100,180,"O","m"],["l",106,174,118,174],["l",106,178,118,178],["p","M118.0 176.0 L127.0 163.6 L141.5 168.4 L141.5 183.6 L127.0 188.4 Z"],["l",127,188.4,118,199.4],["l",127,188.4,136,199.4],["l",170,176,230,176,"a"],["t",200,169,"dil. NaOH","ms"],["t",200,191,"\u0394","ms"],["p","M150.0 227.0 L162.4 236.0 L157.6 250.5 L142.4 250.5 L137.6 236.0 Z"],["l",150,227,150,214],["l",153,226,153,215],["t",151.5,211,"O","ms"],["l",137.6,236,125.6,228],["l",137.6,236,125.6,244],["l",162.4,236,176.4,236],["l",164,233,174.7,233],["p","M176.4 236.0 L185.3 223.6 L199.9 228.3 L199.9 243.6 L185.3 248.3 Z"],["l",185.3,248.3,176.3,259.3],["l",185.3,248.3,194.3,259.3],["t",175,286,"\u03B1, \u03B2-unsaturated carbonyl compound","ms"]]},sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Aldehydes, Ketones and Carboxylic Acids"},{id:"neet-2026-q051",pn:51,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"Electrochemistry",st:"Kohlrausch law limiting conductivity",q:`For a salt XY, which is a strong electrolyte, the plot of \u039Bm versus \u221Ac has a slope of \u201390.0 S cm\xB2 mol\u207B\xB3\u141F\xB2 L\xB9\u141F\xB2 at 298 K. At 0.01 M concentration of XY, the value of \u039Bm is 145.0 S cm\xB2 mol\u207B\xB9. The limiting molar conductivity of Y\u207B ion (\u03BB\xB0Y\u207B, in S cm\xB2 mol\u207B\xB9) at 298 K will be
(Given : \u03BB\xB0X\u207A = 74.0 S cm\xB2 mol\u207B\xB9)`,o:["100.0","90.0","76.0","80.0"],c:3,s:`Debye-Huckel-Onsager: \u039Bm = \u039B\xB0m \u2013 A\u221Ac, a straight line with slope \u2013A = \u201390.
\u221A0.01 = 0.1, so 145 = \u039B\xB0m \u2013 90 \xD7 0.1.
\u039B\xB0m = 154 S cm\xB2 mol\u207B\xB9.
\u039B\xB0m(XY) = \u03BB\xB0X\u207A + \u03BB\xB0Y\u207B, so 154 = 74 + \u03BB\xB0Y\u207B.
\u03BB\xB0Y\u207B = 80 S cm\xB2 mol\u207B\xB9.`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Electrochemistry"},{id:"neet-2026-q052",pn:52,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties",st:"Ionization enthalpy trends",q:`Given below are two statements: One is labelled as Assertion A and the other is labelled as Reason R.
Assertion A: The first ionization enthalpy of O is lower than that of N and F.
Reason R: The loss of an electron from O leads to stable half-filled p orbital.
In light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:`,o:["Both A and R are correct and R is NOT the correct explanation of A","A is correct but R is not correct.","A is not correct but R is correct","Both A and R are correct and R is the correct explanation of A"],c:3,s:`N: 1s\xB2 2s\xB2 2p\xB3. F: 1s\xB2 2s\xB2 2p\u2075.
First ionization enthalpy: N = 1402, O = 1314, F = 1681 kJ mol\u207B\xB9. So O is lower than both N and F.
N has a stable half-filled configuration, so its ionization enthalpy is higher than O.
O(g) \u2192 O\u207A(g) + e\u207B: 2s\xB22p\u2074 \u2192 2s\xB22p\xB3 (half-filled p subshell).
Losing an electron gives a stable half-filled p subshell, so R explains A.`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties"},{id:"neet-2026-q053",pn:53,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"Coordination Compounds",st:"Crystal field spectrochemical series",q:"According to crystal field theory, the correct order of ligands with respect to their decreasing order of field strength is",o:["CO > H\u2082O > NH\u2083 > Cl\u207B","Cl\u207B > H\u2082O > NH\u2083 > CO","Cl\u207B > NH\u2083 > H\u2082O > CO","CO > NH\u2083 > H\u2082O > Cl\u207B"],c:3,s:"From the spectrochemical series, the decreasing order of field strength is CO > NH\u2083 > H\u2082O > Cl\u207B.",sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Coordination Compounds"},{id:"neet-2026-q054",pn:54,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"Coordination Compounds",st:"Optical isomerism in complexes",q:`Given below are two statements:
Statement-I: [Fe(ox)\u2083]\xB3\u207B is chiral.
Statement-II: trans\u2013[Cr(H\u2082O)\u2082(ox)\u2082]\u207B is chiral.
(Given : oxH\u2082 = HOOC \u2013 COOH)
In light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:`,o:["Both Statement-I and Statement-II are incorrect","Statement-I is correct but Statement-II is incorrect","Statement-I is incorrect but Statement-II is correct","Both Statement-I and Statement-II are correct"],c:1,s:`[Fe(ox)\u2083]\xB3\u207B has two non-superimposable mirror images, so it is optically active and chiral. Statement-I is correct.
trans-[Cr(H\u2082O)\u2082(ox)\u2082]\u207B has a plane of symmetry.
So it is not chiral and is optically inactive. Statement-II is incorrect.`,sfig:{w:270,h:212,alt:"Top: [Fe(ox)\u2083]\xB3\u207B and its mirror image on either side of a mirror (three ox chelates around Fe, charge 3\u2013). Bottom: trans-[Cr(H\u2082O)\u2082(ox)\u2082]\u207B with H\u2082O above and below Cr and an ox chelate on each side.",d:[["t",60,54,"Fe","m"],["l",60,38,60,28],["l",60,28,51.8,25.8],["l",49.6,44,40.9,39],["l",40.9,39,43.2,30.8],["t",45,28,"ox","ms"],["l",60,62,60,72],["l",60,72,51.8,74.2],["l",49.6,56,40.9,61],["l",40.9,61,43.2,69.2],["t",45,80,"ox","ms"],["l",70.4,44,79.1,39],["l",79.1,39,85.1,45],["l",70.4,56,79.1,61],["l",79.1,61,85.1,55],["t",90,54,"ox","ms"],["p","M23 12 H18 V88 H23"],["p","M99 12 H104 V88 H99"],["t",106,20,"3\u2013","s"],["l",130,10,130,90],["l",130,16,136,11],["l",130,22,136,17],["l",130,28,136,23],["l",130,34,136,29],["l",130,40,136,35],["l",130,46,136,41],["l",130,52,136,47],["l",130,58,136,53],["l",130,64,136,59],["l",130,70,136,65],["l",130,76,136,71],["l",130,82,136,77],["l",130,88,136,83],["t",200,54,"Fe","m"],["l",200,38,200,28],["l",200,28,208.2,25.8],["l",210.4,44,219.1,39],["l",219.1,39,216.8,30.8],["t",215,28,"ox","ms"],["l",200,62,200,72],["l",200,72,208.2,74.2],["l",210.4,56,219.1,61],["l",219.1,61,216.8,69.2],["t",215,80,"ox","ms"],["l",189.6,44,180.9,39],["l",180.9,39,174.9,45],["l",189.6,56,180.9,61],["l",180.9,61,174.9,55],["t",170,54,"ox","ms"],["p","M161 12 H156 V88 H161"],["p","M237 12 H242 V88 H237"],["t",244,20,"3\u2013","s"],["t",130,164,"Cr","m"],["l",130,151,130,134],["t",130,130,"H\u2082O","m"],["l",130,169,130,186],["t",130,200,"H\u2082O","m"],["l",119.6,154,110.9,149],["l",110.9,149,104.9,155.1],["l",119.6,166,110.9,171],["l",110.9,171,104.9,164.9],["t",100,164,"ox","ms"],["l",140.4,154,149.1,149],["l",149.1,149,155.1,155.1],["l",140.4,166,149.1,171],["l",149.1,171,155.1,164.9],["t",160,164,"ox","ms"],["p","M93 116 H88 V204 H93"],["p","M167 116 H172 V204 H167"],["t",174,124,"\u2013","s"]]},sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Coordination Compounds"},{id:"neet-2026-q055",pn:55,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"Aldehydes, Ketones and Carboxylic Acids",st:"Haloform reaction",q:`For the following reaction sequence, choose the correct option
Benzene \u2192 (i. CH\u2083COCl, AlCl\u2083; ii. NaOCl) \u2192 P + Q`,o:["P and Q are aromatic compounds","If P gives a carboxylic acid on acidification, Q gives a poisonous gas on exposure to air and light","Both P and Q are carbonyl compounds","If P is the sodium salt of a carboxylic acid, Q is a primary alcohol"],c:1,s:`Benzene + CH\u2083COCl with AlCl\u2083 gives acetophenone (C\u2086H\u2085COCH\u2083).
NaOCl does the haloform reaction: P = sodium benzoate (C\u2086H\u2085COO\u207BNa\u207A), Q = CHCl\u2083 (chloroform).
Acidifying P gives benzoic acid.
Chloroform in air and light is oxidised: 2CHCl\u2083 + O\u2082 \u2192 2COCl\u2082 + 2HCl.
COCl\u2082 is phosgene, a poisonous gas.`,sfig:{w:270,h:282,alt:"Scheme: benzene + CH\u2083COCl (AlCl\u2083) gives acetophenone; acetophenone with NaOCl gives sodium benzoate (P) + CHCl\u2083 (Q); P on acidic hydrolysis gives benzoic acid; 2CHCl\u2083 + O\u2082 in air and light gives 2COCl\u2082 + 2HCl, phosgene gas (poisonous gas).",d:[["p","M30.0 27.0 L41.3 33.5 L41.3 46.5 L30.0 53.0 L18.7 46.5 L18.7 33.5 Z"],["c",30,40,7.15],["t",50,44,"+ CH\u2083COCl","s"],["l",108,40,168,40,"a"],["t",138,33,"AlCl\u2083","ms"],["p","M196.0 33.0 L207.3 39.5 L207.3 52.5 L196.0 59.0 L184.7 52.5 L184.7 39.5 Z"],["c",196,46,7.15],["l",196,33,196,22],["t",196,17,"COCH\u2083","ms"],["p","M30.0 103.0 L41.3 109.5 L41.3 122.5 L30.0 129.0 L18.7 122.5 L18.7 109.5 Z"],["c",30,116,7.15],["l",30,103,30,92],["t",30,87,"COCH\u2083","ms"],["l",76,112,136,112,"a"],["t",106,105,"NaOCl","ms"],["p","M170.0 103.0 L181.3 109.5 L181.3 122.5 L170.0 129.0 L158.7 122.5 L158.7 109.5 Z"],["c",170,116,7.15],["l",170,103,170,92],["t",170,87,"COO\u207BNa\u207A","ms"],["t",198,120,"+ CHCl\u2083","s"],["t",170,146,"(P)","ms"],["t",216,146,"(Q)","ms"],["p","M30.0 179.0 L41.3 185.5 L41.3 198.5 L30.0 205.0 L18.7 198.5 L18.7 185.5 Z"],["c",30,192,7.15],["l",30,179,30,168],["t",30,163,"COO\u207BNa\u207A","ms"],["t",30,222,"(P)","ms"],["l",66,192,166,192,"a"],["t",116,185,"Acidic Hydrolysis","ms"],["p","M196.0 179.0 L207.3 185.5 L207.3 198.5 L196.0 205.0 L184.7 198.5 L184.7 185.5 Z"],["c",196,192,7.15],["l",196,179,196,168],["t",196,163,"COOH","ms"],["t",30,248,"2CHCl\u2083 + O\u2082","ms"],["t",30,262,"(Q)","ms"],["l",80,246,140,246,"a"],["t",110,239,"In air","ms"],["t",110,261,"Light","ms"],["t",200,250,"2COCl\u2082 + 2HCl","ms"],["t",200,263,"Phosgene gas","ms"],["t",200,275,"(poisonous gas)","ms"]]},sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Aldehydes, Ketones and Carboxylic Acids"},{id:"neet-2026-q056",pn:56,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"Chemical Kinetics",st:"Arrhenius equation and plot",q:`For an elementary chemical reaction, the Arrhenius plot is given below.
If the energy of activation is 6.64 kJ mol\u207B\xB9 and R = 8.3 J K\u207B\xB9 mol\u207B\xB9, the temperature at which the rate constant becomes e\xB2 min\u207B\xB9, is`,o:["150 K","200 K","250 K","125 K"],c:1,s:`ln k = ln A \u2013 Ea/RT.
The graph shows ln k = 6 at the intercept (1/T = 0), so ln A = 6.
For k = e\xB2, ln k = 2.
2 = 6 \u2013 (6.64\xD710\xB3)/(8.3 \xD7 T).
(6.64\xD710\xB3)/(8.3 \xD7 T) = 4.
T = (6.64\xD710\xB3)/(8.3 \xD7 4) = 200 K.`,fig:{w:300,h:204,alt:"Arrhenius plot: ln k on the vertical axis (ticks 2, 4, 6, 8) against 1/T; a straight line falls from ln k = 6 on the vertical axis to about ln k = 1 at the right.",d:[["l",60,170,60,8],["l",60,170,280,170],["l",56,152,60,152],["l",56,134,60,134],["t",52,138,"2","e"],["l",56,116,60,116],["l",56,98,60,98],["t",52,102,"4","e"],["l",56,80,60,80],["l",56,62,60,62],["t",52,66,"6","e"],["l",56,44,60,44],["l",56,26,60,26],["t",52,30,"8","e"],["l",60,62,250,150.2,"B"],["l",22,125,22,71,"a"],["t",30,54.8,"lnk","s"],["l",120,192,148,192,"a"],["t",154,196,"1/T"]]},sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Chemical Kinetics"},{id:"neet-2026-q057",pn:57,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques",st:"Polarity of organic compounds",q:`Arrange the following compounds in the increasing order of polarity
A. CH\u2083CH\u2082OCH\u2082CH\u2083
B. CH\u2083CH\u2082OH
C. CH\u2083COCH\u2083
D. CH\u2083COOH
Choose the correct answer from the options given below.`,o:["C < A < D < B","C < A < B < D","A < C < B < D","A < B < C < D"],c:2,s:`On the basis of polarity, the order is C\u2082H\u2085OC\u2082H\u2085 < CH\u2083COCH\u2083 < CH\u2083CH\u2082OH < CH\u2083COOH.
So the correct order is A < C < B < D.`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques"},{id:"neet-2026-q058",pn:58,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"Thermodynamics (Chemistry)",st:"Entropy change at equilibrium",q:"A protein undergoes reversible thermal denaturation from its initial state N to denatured state D according to N \u21CC D. At 60\xBAC, the concentrations of both N and D are equal at equilibrium, and the standard enthalpy change of denaturation is 666 kJ mol\u207B\xB9. The standard entropy change (\u0394S\xB0 in kJ K\u207B\xB9 mol\u207B\xB9) of the protein upon denaturation at 60\xBAC is closest to",o:["2000.0","333.0","11.1","2.0"],c:3,s:`At equilibrium, \u0394S = \u0394H/T.
T = 60 + 273 = 333 K.
\u0394S = 666/333.
\u0394S = 2 kJ K\u207B\xB9 mol\u207B\xB9.`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Thermodynamics (Chemistry)"},{id:"neet-2026-q059",pn:59,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"Solutions",st:"Deviation from Raoult's law",q:`Consider the following statements about the solutions formed by mixing two liquids.
A. An ideal solution thus formed obeys Raoult\u2019s law throughout the composition range.
B. Mixture of chloroform and acetone shows negative deviation from Raoult\u2019s law.
C. Mixture of aniline and phenol shows positive deviation from Raoult\u2019s law.`,o:["B and C only","A only","A and C only","A and B only"],c:3,s:`In phenol and aniline, the hydrogen bond between the phenolic proton and the lone pair on the nitrogen of aniline is stronger than the hydrogen bonds between similar molecules.
So the mixture shows negative deviation, not positive. Statement C is wrong.
Statements A and B are correct.`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Solutions"},{id:"neet-2026-q060",pn:60,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"The p-Block Elements",st:"Halogens with conc. H\u2082SO\u2084 and MnO\u2082",q:`Given below are two statements :
Statement-I : Heating NaCl with concentrated H\u2082SO\u2084 and MnO\u2082 results in oxidation of Mn.
Statement-II : Heating NaI with concentrated H\u2082SO\u2084 and MnO\u2082 results in reduction of Mn.
In light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below.`,o:["Both Statement-I and Statement-II are incorrect","Statement-I is correct but Statement-II is incorrect","Statement-I is incorrect but Statement-II is correct","Both Statement-I and Statement-II are correct"],c:2,s:`Statement-I is false.
2NaCl + MnO\u2082 + 2H\u2082SO\u2084 \u2192 Na\u2082SO\u2084 + MnSO\u2084 + 2H\u2082O + Cl\u2082 (Mn goes from +4 in MnO\u2082 to +2 in MnSO\u2084).
So Mn is reduced, not oxidised.
Statement-II is true.
2NaI + MnO\u2082 + 2H\u2082SO\u2084 \u2192 Na\u2082SO\u2084 + MnSO\u2084 + I\u2082 + 2H\u2082O (Mn goes from +4 to +2).
So Mn is reduced.`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"The p-Block Elements"},{id:"neet-2026-q061",pn:61,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"Hydrocarbons",st:"Stereochemistry of addition to alkenes",q:`Given below are two statements :
Statement I : trans-But-2-ene upon treatment with Br\u2082 in CCl\u2084 gives the following product.
Statement II : cis-But-2-ene upon treatment with alkaline KMnO\u2084 gives the following product.
In the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below.`,o:["Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are correct"],c:2,s:`Statement I: trans-but-2-ene adds Br\u2082 in CCl\u2084 by anti addition and gives the meso product (2,3-dibromobutane).
The product drawn in Statement I is a Newman projection. Converted to a Fischer projection it is not meso.
So the product given in Statement I is not the one formed, and Statement I is incorrect.
Statement II: cis-but-2-ene with alkaline KMnO\u2084 adds two OH groups on the same side (syn addition) and gives the meso diol.
The Newman projection given in Statement II converts to the Fischer projection of the meso diol, so Statement II is correct.`,fig:{w:300,h:118,alt:"Two Newman projections. Statement I: front bonds Br (top), H (lower left), Me (lower right); back bonds H (upper left), Me (upper right), Br (bottom). Statement II: front OH (top), H (lower left), Me (lower right); back H (upper left), OH (upper right), Me (bottom).",d:[["t",10,14,"Statement I","s"],["c",70,62,15],["l",70,62,70,40],["t",70,36,"Br","m"],["l",70,62,50.9,73],["t",48,81,"H","e"],["l",70,62,89.1,73],["t",92,81,"Me"],["l",57,54.5,50.9,51],["t",48,51,"H","e"],["l",83,54.5,89.1,51],["t",92,51,"Me"],["l",70,77,70,84],["t",70,96,"Br","m"],["t",170,14,"Statement II","s"],["c",230,62,15],["l",230,62,230,40],["t",230,36,"OH","m"],["l",230,62,210.9,73],["t",208,81,"H","e"],["l",230,62,249.1,73],["t",252,81,"Me"],["l",217,54.5,210.9,51],["t",208,51,"H","e"],["l",243,54.5,249.1,51],["t",252,51,"OH"],["l",230,77,230,84],["t",230,96,"Me","m"]]},sfig:{w:340,h:380,alt:"Solution: trans-but-2-ene (H and CH\u2083 on each carbon, CH\u2083 groups opposite) + Br\u2082/CCl\u2084 gives the meso Fischer form (CH\u2083; H, Br; H, Br; CH\u2083). The Statement I Newman projection converts to the Fischer form Br; H, Me; Me, H; Br, which is not meso. cis-but-2-ene + alkaline KMnO\u2084 gives the meso diol (CH\u2083; H, OH; H, OH; CH\u2083). The Statement II Newman projection converts to OH; H, Me; HO, H; Me and then to OH; H, Me; H, Me; OH, which is meso.",d:[["t",40,44,"C","m"],["t",70,44,"C","m"],["l",47,37,63,37],["l",47,41,63,41],["l",35,33,28,28],["t",28,26,"H","e"],["l",35,45,28,52],["t",28,62,"CH\u2083","e"],["l",75,33,82,28],["t",82,26,"CH\u2083"],["l",75,45,82,52],["t",82,62,"H"],["l",110,40,160,40,"a"],["t",135,33,"Br\u2082","ms"],["t",135,55,"CCl\u2084","ms"],["l",230,24,230,58],["t",230,20,"CH\u2083","m"],["l",214,32,246,32],["t",212,36,"H","e"],["t",248,36,"Br"],["l",214,50,246,50],["t",212,54,"H","e"],["t",248,54,"Br"],["t",230,72,"CH\u2083","m"],["t",230,86,"Meso","ms"],["c",60,130,15],["l",60,130,60,108],["t",60,104,"Br","m"],["l",60,130,40.9,141],["t",38,149,"H","e"],["l",60,130,79.1,141],["t",82,149,"Me"],["l",47,122.5,40.9,119],["t",38,119,"H","e"],["l",73,122.5,79.1,119],["t",82,119,"Me"],["l",60,145,60,152],["t",60,164,"Br","m"],["l",110,130,160,130,"a"],["l",230,114,230,148],["t",230,110,"Br","m"],["l",214,122,246,122],["t",212,126,"H","e"],["t",248,126,"Me"],["l",214,140,246,140],["t",212,144,"Me","e"],["t",248,144,"H"],["t",230,162,"Br","m"],["t",230,172,"It is not meso","ms"],["t",40,224,"C","m"],["t",70,224,"C","m"],["l",47,217,63,217],["l",47,221,63,221],["l",35,213,28,208],["t",28,206,"CH\u2083","e"],["l",35,225,28,232],["t",28,242,"H","e"],["l",75,213,82,208],["t",82,206,"CH\u2083"],["l",75,225,82,232],["t",82,242,"H"],["l",110,220,160,220,"a"],["t",135,213,"Alkaline","ms"],["t",135,235,"KMnO\u2084","ms"],["l",230,204,230,238],["t",230,200,"CH\u2083","m"],["l",214,212,246,212],["t",212,216,"H","e"],["t",248,216,"OH"],["l",214,230,246,230],["t",212,234,"H","e"],["t",248,234,"OH"],["t",230,252,"CH\u2083","m"],["t",230,266,"Meso","ms"],["c",50,322,15],["l",50,322,50,300],["t",50,296,"OH","m"],["l",50,322,30.9,333],["t",28,341,"H","e"],["l",50,322,69.1,333],["t",72,341,"Me"],["l",37,314.5,30.9,311],["t",28,311,"H","e"],["l",63,314.5,69.1,311],["t",72,311,"OH"],["l",50,337,50,344],["t",50,356,"Me","m"],["l",96,322,128,322,"a"],["l",170,306,170,340],["t",170,302,"OH","m"],["l",154,314,186,314],["t",152,318,"H","e"],["t",188,318,"Me"],["l",154,332,186,332],["t",152,336,"HO","e"],["t",188,336,"H"],["t",170,354,"Me","m"],["l",214,322,246,322,"a"],["l",290,306,290,340],["t",290,302,"OH","m"],["l",274,314,306,314],["t",272,318,"H","e"],["t",308,318,"Me"],["l",274,332,306,332],["t",272,336,"H","e"],["t",308,336,"Me"],["t",290,354,"OH","m"],["t",290,368,"Meso","ms"]]},sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Hydrocarbons"},{id:"neet-2026-q062",pn:62,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure",st:"Molecular orbital configuration",q:"The highest occupied molecular orbital for Ne\u2082 is",o:["\u03C32p","\u03C0*2p","\u03C3*2p","\u03C02p"],c:2,s:`Ne\u2082 order: \u03C31s\xB2 < \u03C3*1s\xB2 < \u03C32s\xB2 < \u03C3*2s\xB2 < \u03C32p_z\xB2 < (\u03C02p_x\xB2 = \u03C02p_y\xB2) < (\u03C0*2p_x\xB2 = \u03C0*2p_y\xB2) < \u03C3*2p_z\xB2.
So \u03C3*2p_z, i.e. \u03C3*2p, is the highest occupied orbital.`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure"},{id:"neet-2026-q063",pn:63,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques",st:"Resonance and hyperconjugation",q:"The following carbocation is stabilized by the interaction of the empty p orbital with",o:["empty \u03C3 and empty \u03C0* orbitals","empty \u03C3* and filled \u03C0 orbitals","empty \u03C3* and empty \u03C0* orbitals","filled \u03C3 and filled \u03C0 orbitals"],c:3,s:`Due to resonance, the carbocation is stabilised by the filled \u03C0 orbital of the ring.
Due to hyperconjugation, it is stabilised by filled \u03C3 orbitals.`,fig:{w:240,h:120,alt:"Carbocation: a benzene ring bonded to a carbon carrying a positive charge, which is also bonded to CH\u2083.",d:[["p","M70 33L97.7 49L97.7 81L70 97L42.3 81L42.3 49Z",""],["l",73.9,42.3,87.7,50.3,""],["l",87.7,79.7,73.9,87.7,""],["l",48.4,73,48.4,57,""],["l",97.7,49,125.4,33,""],["l",125.4,33,148,46,""],["t",125.4,24,"\u2295","m"],["t",150,55,"CH\u2083",""]]},sfig:{w:240,h:120,alt:"Carbocation: a benzene ring bonded to a carbon carrying a positive charge, which is also bonded to CH\u2083.",d:[["p","M70 33L97.7 49L97.7 81L70 97L42.3 81L42.3 49Z",""],["l",73.9,42.3,87.7,50.3,""],["l",87.7,79.7,73.9,87.7,""],["l",48.4,73,48.4,57,""],["l",97.7,49,125.4,33,""],["l",125.4,33,148,46,""],["t",125.4,24,"\u2295","m"],["t",150,55,"CH\u2083",""]]},sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques"},{id:"neet-2026-q064",pn:64,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"The d- and f-Block Elements",st:"Thermal decomposition of KMnO\u2084",q:"The green paramagnetic species formed by heating KMnO\u2084 at 513 K is",o:["Mn\u2083O\u2084","MnO","KO\u2082","K\u2082MnO\u2084"],c:3,s:`2KMnO\u2084 \u2192 K\u2082MnO\u2084 + MnO\u2082 + O\u2082.
K\u2082MnO\u2084 forms on heating KMnO\u2084 and it is green in colour.`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"The d- and f-Block Elements"},{id:"neet-2026-q065",pn:65,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties",st:"Valence orbitals and covalency",q:"The correct statement is",o:["Beryllium has three valence orbitals.","Magnesium has a maximum covalency of four.","Aluminium has five valence orbitals.","Boron has a maximum covalency of four."],c:3,s:`Aluminium has nine valence orbitals.
Beryllium has four valence orbitals.
Magnesium has a maximum covalency of six.
Boron has a maximum covalency of four, as in BH\u2084\u207B.`,sfig:{w:200,h:130,alt:"Structure of BH\u2084\u207B: B\u207B in the centre bonded to one H above, one H below and two H at the lower left and lower right, inside brackets.",d:[["p","M58 10Q40 10 40 30L40 100Q40 120 58 120",""],["p","M142 10Q160 10 160 30L160 100Q160 120 142 120",""],["t",100,32,"H","m"],["l",100,37,100,53,""],["t",100,70,"B\u207B","m"],["l",100,76,100,92,""],["t",100,108,"H","m"],["l",92,72,74,92,""],["t",68,106,"H","m"],["l",108,72,126,92,""],["t",132,106,"H","m"]]},sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties"},{id:"neet-2026-q066",pn:66,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"Biomolecules (Chemistry)",st:"Structure of proteins",q:"The correct statement about peptides and proteins is",o:["Only the proteins having a quaternary structure are biologically active","In \u03B2-pleated sheet structures, peptide chains are held together by intermolecular hydrogen bonds","In \u03B1-helices, the polypeptide chain is twisted into a left-handed screw (helix) through intramolecular hydrogen bonds","Tertiary structure of proteins has two or more polypeptide subunits"],c:1,s:`In \u03B2-pleated sheet structure, peptide chains are held together by intermolecular hydrogen bonds.
This is the only correct statement.
Statements (1), (3) and (4) are incorrect.`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Biomolecules (Chemistry)"},{id:"neet-2026-q067",pn:67,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"Chemical Kinetics",st:"Zero order reaction",q:"2A \u2192 B (rate constant k) is a zero-order reaction, where k = 1.0 mol L\u207B\xB9 min\u207B\xB9. If the initial concentration of A is 2 M, then the time taken to complete 75% of the reaction will be",o:["0.75 min","1.0 min","2.0 min","1.5 min"],c:0,s:`For the zero order reaction, \u2212(1/2)(dA/dt) = k.
t = (A\u2080 \u2212 A\u209C)/2k.
75% complete leaves A\u209C = 0.5 M.
t = (2 \u2212 0.5)/2 = 0.75 min.`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Chemical Kinetics"},{id:"neet-2026-q068",pn:68,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"The d- and f-Block Elements",st:"Melting points of 3d metals",q:`Given below are two statements: One is labelled as Assertion A and the other is labelled as Reason R.
Assertion A: Generally, 3d transition metals have high melting points.
Reason R: Involvement of 3d-electrons in addition to 4s-electrons in the interatomic metallic bonding.
In light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:`,o:["Both A and R are correct and R is NOT the correct explanation of A","A is correct but R is not correct.","A is not correct but R is correct","Both A and R are correct and R is the correct explanation of A"],c:3,s:`The melting points of 3d transition metals are generally high.
This is because a greater number of electrons from (n \u2212 1)d, in addition to the ns electrons, take part in interatomic metallic bonding.`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"The d- and f-Block Elements"},{id:"neet-2026-q069",pn:69,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"Structure of Atom",st:"Radial nodes",q:`Consider the following schematic plots of orbital wavefunction (\u03C8r) against distance (r) from the nucleus.
The figure representing two radial nodes in the orbital is`,o:["B","C","D","A"],c:1,s:`A is 1s: radial nodes = 0.
B is 2s: radial nodes = 1.
C is 3s: radial nodes = 2.
D is 3p: radial nodes = 1.
So C has two radial nodes.`,fig:{w:330,h:250,alt:"Four schematic plots A, B, C and D of \u03C8\u1D63 against r: A decays without crossing zero, B crosses zero once, C crosses zero twice, D starts at zero, rises to a small hump, dips below zero once and returns to zero.",d:[["l",25,5,25,100,""],["l",25,100,150,100,""],["t",12,14,"\u2191","m s"],["t",12,30,"\u03C8\u1D63","m s"],["t",25,114,"0","m s"],["t",100,114,"r \u2192","s"],["p","M27 8C35 62 65 94 130 98",""],["t",17,100,"0","m s"],["t",90,40,"A","m"],["l",190,5,190,100,""],["l",190,100,315,100,""],["t",177,14,"\u2191","m s"],["t",177,30,"\u03C8\u1D63","m s"],["t",190,114,"0","m s"],["t",265,114,"r \u2192","s"],["l",190,45,315,45,"d"],["t",182,49,"0","m s"],["p","M192 8C198 60 206 85 215 85C225 85 228 45 245 45L315 45",""],["t",265,35,"B","m"],["l",25,130,25,225,""],["l",25,225,150,225,""],["t",12,139,"\u2191","m s"],["t",12,155,"\u03C8\u1D63","m s"],["t",25,239,"0","m s"],["t",100,239,"r \u2192","s"],["l",25,195,135,195,"d"],["p","M27 133C31 195 35 217 42 217C50 217 55 180 77 180C93 180 97 195 107 195L135 195",""],["t",17,199,"0","m s"],["t",77,173,"C","m"],["l",190,130,190,225,""],["l",190,225,315,225,""],["t",177,139,"\u2191","m s"],["t",177,155,"\u03C8\u1D63","m s"],["t",190,239,"0","m s"],["t",265,239,"r \u2192","s"],["l",190,195,310,195,"d"],["p","M190 195C195 180 200 177 205 177C215 177 220 195 225 205C228 213 230 215 233 215C240 215 240 195 252 195L310 195",""],["t",182,199,"0","m s"],["t",250,187,"D","m"]]},sfig:{w:330,h:320,alt:"The same four plots labelled: 1s, number of radial node = 0; 2s, number of radial node = 1; 3s, number of radial nodes = 2; 3p, number of radial node = 1.",d:[["l",25,5,25,100,""],["l",25,100,150,100,""],["t",12,14,"\u2191","m s"],["t",12,30,"\u03C8\u1D63","m s"],["t",25,114,"0","m s"],["t",100,114,"r \u2192","s"],["p","M27 8C35 62 65 94 130 98",""],["t",35,132,"(1s); number of","s"],["t",35,145,"radial node = 0","s"],["l",190,5,190,100,""],["l",190,100,315,100,""],["t",177,14,"\u2191","m s"],["t",177,30,"\u03C8\u1D63","m s"],["t",190,114,"0","m s"],["t",265,114,"r \u2192","s"],["l",190,45,315,45,"d"],["t",182,49,"0","m s"],["p","M192 8C198 60 206 85 215 85C225 85 228 45 245 45L315 45",""],["t",200,132,"(2s); number of","s"],["t",200,145,"radial node = 1","s"],["l",25,165,25,260,""],["l",25,260,150,260,""],["t",12,174,"\u2191","m s"],["t",12,190,"\u03C8\u1D63","m s"],["t",25,274,"0","m s"],["t",100,274,"r \u2192","s"],["l",25,230,135,230,"d"],["p","M27 168C31 230 35 252 42 252C50 252 55 215 77 215C93 215 97 230 107 230L135 230",""],["t",35,292,"(3s); number of","s"],["t",35,305,"radial nodes = 2","s"],["l",190,165,190,260,""],["l",190,260,315,260,""],["t",177,174,"\u2191","m s"],["t",177,190,"\u03C8\u1D63","m s"],["t",190,274,"0","m s"],["t",265,274,"r \u2192","s"],["l",190,230,310,230,"d"],["p","M190 230C195 215 200 212 205 212C215 212 220 230 225 240C228 248 230 250 233 250C240 250 240 230 252 230L310 230",""],["t",200,292,"(3p); number of","s"],["t",200,305,"radial node = 1","s"]]},sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Structure of Atom"},{id:"neet-2026-q070",pn:70,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"Amines",st:"Preparation of aniline",q:`Identify the reactions which give aniline as the major product.
A. Benzonitrile (C\u2086H\u2085CN) with LiAlH\u2084
B. Benzamide (C\u2086H\u2085CONH\u2082) with KOH, Br\u2082
C. Nitrobenzene (C\u2086H\u2085NO\u2082) with NaBH\u2084
D. Acetanilide (C\u2086H\u2085NHCOCH\u2083) with HCl, H\u2082O, \u0394
Choose the correct answer from the options given below.`,o:["B and D only","A and C only","C and D only","A and B only"],c:0,s:`A: benzonitrile with LiAlH\u2084 gives benzylamine (C\u2086H\u2085CH\u2082NH\u2082), not aniline.
B: benzamide with KOH/Br\u2082 (Hofmann bromamide) gives aniline.
D: acid hydrolysis of acetanilide gives aniline.
So B and D give aniline.`,fig:{w:300,h:330,alt:"Four reactions: A benzonitrile with LiAlH\u2084; B benzamide with KOH, Br\u2082; C nitrobenzene with NaBH\u2084; D acetanilide with HCl, H\u2082O and heat.",d:[["t",8,55,"A.",""],["p","M60 28L79.1 39L79.1 61L60 72L40.9 61L40.9 39Z",""],["l",62.7,34.4,72.2,39.9,""],["l",72.2,60.1,62.7,65.6,""],["l",45.1,55.5,45.1,44.5,""],["l",79.1,39,96.1,29,""],["t",97,30,"CN",""],["l",140,45,220,45,"a"],["t",180,37,"LiAlH\u2084","m s"],["t",8,140,"B.",""],["p","M60 113L79.1 124L79.1 146L60 157L40.9 146L40.9 124Z",""],["l",62.7,119.4,72.2,124.9,""],["l",72.2,145.1,62.7,150.6,""],["l",45.1,140.5,45.1,129.5,""],["l",79.1,124,96.1,114,""],["l",94.5,113,94.5,96,""],["l",98.5,113,98.5,96,""],["t",96.5,92,"O","m"],["l",96.5,114,112,123,""],["t",113,131,"NH\u2082",""],["l",160,130,240,130,"a"],["t",200,122,"KOH, Br\u2082","m s"],["t",8,220,"C.",""],["p","M60 193L79.1 204L79.1 226L60 237L40.9 226L40.9 204Z",""],["l",62.7,199.4,72.2,204.9,""],["l",72.2,225.1,62.7,230.6,""],["l",45.1,220.5,45.1,209.5,""],["l",79.1,204,96.1,194,""],["t",97,196,"NO\u2082",""],["l",140,210,220,210,"a"],["t",180,202,"NaBH\u2084","m s"],["t",8,305,"D.",""],["p","M60 278L79.1 289L79.1 311L60 322L40.9 311L40.9 289Z",""],["l",62.7,284.4,72.2,289.9,""],["l",72.2,310.1,62.7,315.6,""],["l",45.1,305.5,45.1,294.5,""],["l",79.1,289,96.1,279,""],["t",102,280,"N","m"],["t",102,264,"H","m"],["l",108,281,122,290,""],["l",120,291,120,309,""],["l",124,291,124,309,""],["t",122,322,"O","m"],["l",122,290,136,281,""],["t",137,284,"CH\u2083",""],["l",180,295,270,295,"a"],["t",225,287,"HCl, H\u2082O","m s"],["t",225,309,"\u0394","m s"]]},sfig:{w:340,h:285,alt:"Products: benzonitrile with LiAlH\u2084 gives C\u2086H\u2085\u2013CH\u2082\u2013NH\u2082; benzamide with KOH, Br\u2082 gives aniline; acetanilide with HCl, H\u2082O and heat gives aniline.",d:[["p","M40 30L57.3 40L57.3 60L40 70L22.7 60L22.7 40Z",""],["l",42.4,35.8,51.1,40.8,""],["l",51.1,59.2,42.4,64.2,""],["l",26.5,55,26.5,45,""],["l",57.3,40,72.3,31,""],["t",73,32,"CN",""],["l",100,45,170,45,"a"],["t",135,37,"LiAlH\u2084","m s"],["p","M215 30L232.3 40L232.3 60L215 70L197.7 60L197.7 40Z",""],["l",217.4,35.8,226.1,40.8,""],["l",226.1,59.2,217.4,64.2,""],["l",201.5,55,201.5,45,""],["l",232.3,40,247.3,31,""],["t",248,32,"CH\u2082",""],["l",277,27,292,27,""],["t",295,32,"NH\u2082",""],["p","M40 125L57.3 135L57.3 155L40 165L22.7 155L22.7 135Z",""],["l",42.4,130.8,51.1,135.8,""],["l",51.1,154.2,42.4,159.2,""],["l",26.5,150,26.5,140,""],["l",57.3,135,70.3,127,""],["t",76,130,"C","m"],["l",74,118,74,102,""],["l",78,118,78,102,""],["t",76,98,"O","m"],["l",83,128,96,135,""],["t",97,143,"NH\u2082",""],["l",130,140,210,140,"a"],["t",170,132,"KOH, Br\u2082","m s"],["p","M250 125L267.3 135L267.3 155L250 165L232.7 155L232.7 135Z",""],["l",252.4,130.8,261.1,135.8,""],["l",261.1,154.2,252.4,159.2,""],["l",236.5,150,236.5,140,""],["l",267.3,135,282.3,126,""],["t",283,127,"NH\u2082",""],["p","M40 225L57.3 235L57.3 255L40 265L22.7 255L22.7 235Z",""],["l",42.4,230.8,51.1,235.8,""],["l",51.1,254.2,42.4,259.2,""],["l",26.5,250,26.5,240,""],["l",57.3,235,70.3,227,""],["t",76,230,"N","m"],["t",76,200,"H","m"],["l",76,204,76,218,""],["l",82,229,94,236,""],["t",98,244,"C","m"],["l",104,234,116,226,""],["t",117,228,"CH\u2083",""],["l",96,248,96,262,""],["l",100,248,100,262,""],["t",98,275,"O","m"],["l",150,240,230,240,"a"],["t",190,232,"HCl, H\u2082O","m s"],["t",190,254,"\u0394","m s"],["p","M270 225L287.3 235L287.3 255L270 265L252.7 255L252.7 235Z",""],["l",272.4,230.8,281.1,235.8,""],["l",281.1,254.2,272.4,259.2,""],["l",256.5,250,256.5,240,""],["l",287.3,235,302.3,226,""],["t",303,227,"NH\u2082",""]]},sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Amines"},{id:"neet-2026-q071",pn:71,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"Coordination Compounds",st:"Magnetic moment of complexes",q:`Among the species given below, the spin-only magnetic moment is highest for
(Given: Atomic number of Ti = 22, Mn = 25, Fe = 26 and Co = 27)`,o:["[Fe(CN)\u2086]\xB3\u207B","[Co(NH\u2083)\u2086]\xB3\u207A","[Ti(H\u2082O)\u2086]\xB3\u207A","[Mn(CN)\u2086]\xB3\u207B"],c:3,s:`Spin-only magnetic moment is highest when the number of unpaired electrons is highest.
[Ti(H\u2082O)\u2086]\xB3\u207A: Ti\xB3\u207A = [Ar] 4s\u2070 3d\xB9, so unpaired electrons = 1.
[Mn(CN)\u2086]\xB3\u207B: Mn\xB3\u207A = [Ar] 4s\u2070 3d\u2074, CN\u207B is a strong ligand so the 4 electrons go into t\u2082g (2 paired, 2 unpaired), so unpaired electrons = 2.
[Fe(CN)\u2086]\xB3\u207B: Fe\xB3\u207A = [Ar] 4s\u2070 3d\u2075, low spin t\u2082g\u2075, so unpaired electrons = 1.
Highest is [Mn(CN)\u2086]\xB3\u207B with 2 unpaired electrons.
Note: the printed solution figure shows only four unpaired electrons for Fe\xB3\u207A (3d\u2075); a d\u2075 ion has five, so the figure here shows five.
Note: Publisher's solution does not show Co\xB3\u207A (0 unpaired electrons, low spin d\u2076); answer unaffected.`,sfig:{w:250,h:190,alt:"Crystal field splitting with strong-field CN\u207B: singly filled d boxes (four for Mn\xB3\u207A, five for Fe\xB3\u207A) split into empty eg and lower t\u2082g; for Mn\xB3\u207A the t\u2082g boxes hold paired, single, single electrons; for Fe\xB3\u207A they hold paired, paired, single.",d:[["t",10,14,"Mn\xB3\u207A (3d\u2074)","s"],["r",10,36,14,18,""],["p","M17 51L17 39L14 43",""],["r",27,36,14,18,""],["p","M34 51L34 39L31 43",""],["r",44,36,14,18,""],["p","M51 51L51 39L48 43",""],["r",61,36,14,18,""],["p","M68 51L68 39L65 43",""],["l",75,45,85,45,""],["l",85,45,105,18,""],["l",105,18,190,18,""],["t",194,16,"eg","s"],["l",85,45,109,74,""],["l",109,74,170,74,""],["r",170,65,14,18,""],["p","M175 80L175 68L172 72",""],["p","M179 68L179 80L182 76",""],["r",187,65,14,18,""],["p","M194 80L194 68L191 72",""],["r",204,65,14,18,""],["p","M211 80L211 68L208 72",""],["t",224,82,"t\u2082g","s"],["t",10,109,"Fe\xB3\u207A (3d\u2075)","s"],["r",10,131,14,18,""],["p","M17 146L17 134L14 138",""],["r",27,131,14,18,""],["p","M34 146L34 134L31 138",""],["r",44,131,14,18,""],["p","M51 146L51 134L48 138",""],["r",61,131,14,18,""],["p","M68 146L68 134L65 138",""],["r",78,131,14,18,""],["p","M85 146L85 134L82 138",""],["l",92,140,102,140,""],["l",102,140,122,113,""],["l",122,113,190,113,""],["t",194,111,"eg","s"],["l",102,140,126,169,""],["l",126,169,170,169,""],["r",170,160,14,18,""],["p","M175 175L175 163L172 167",""],["p","M179 163L179 175L182 171",""],["r",187,160,14,18,""],["p","M192 175L192 163L189 167",""],["p","M196 163L196 175L199 171",""],["r",204,160,14,18,""],["p","M211 175L211 163L208 167",""],["t",224,177,"t\u2082g","s"]]},sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Coordination Compounds"},{id:"neet-2026-q072",pn:72,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"Coordination Compounds",st:"Isomerism in coordination compounds",q:`The complex which has facial and meridional isomers is
(Given : py = pyridine and en = H\u2082N \u2013 CH\u2082 \u2013 CH\u2082 \u2013 NH\u2082)`,o:["[Cr(H\u2082O)\u2086]\xB3\u207A","[Co(NH\u2083)\u2084(H\u2082O)\u2082]\xB3\u207A","[Ni(en)\u2082(H\u2082O)\u2082]\xB2\u207A","[Cr(py)\u2083(Cl)\u2083]"],c:3,s:`Complexes of the type [Ma\u2083b\u2083] show facial and meridional isomers.
So [Cr(py)\u2083(Cl)\u2083] shows fac and mer isomers.`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Coordination Compounds"},{id:"neet-2026-q073",pn:73,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"Coordination Compounds",st:"Ionisation of coordination compounds",q:"A 1 : 3 electrolyte in an aqueous solution is",o:["[CoCl(NH\u2083)\u2085]Cl\u2082","[Co(NH\u2083)\u2086]Cl\u2083","[Co(NH\u2083)\u2083(NO\u2082)\u2083]","[CoCl\u2082(NH\u2083)\u2084]Cl"],c:1,s:`[Co(NH\u2083)\u2086]Cl\u2083 in water dissociates into [Co(NH\u2083)\u2086]\xB3\u207A and 3Cl\u207B ions.
So it is a 1 : 3 electrolyte.`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Coordination Compounds"},{id:"neet-2026-q074",pn:74,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"Thermodynamics (Chemistry)",st:"Work in reversible cyclic processes",q:`Consider the reversible processes for 1.0 mol of an ideal gas as shown in the figure.
w\u2081, w\u2082, w\u2083 and w\u2084 represent work done (in calories) in the processes 1, 2, 3 and 4, respectively; \u0394U\u2082 and \u0394U\u2084 are changes in the internal energy for the processes 2 and 4, respectively.
[use R = 2 cal K\u207B\xB9 mol\u207B\xB9]
The correct option is`,o:["w\u2082 + w\u2084 = \u0394U\u2082 \u2212 \u0394U\u2084","w\u2081 + w\u2082 = 2T\u2081 ln(V\u2082/V\u2081)","w\u2081 + w\u2082 + w\u2083 + w\u2084 = 0","w\u2081 + w\u2083 = \u22122T\u2081 ln(V\u2082/V\u2081) \u2212 2T\u2082 ln(V\u2084/V\u2083)"],c:3,s:`Process 1 is an isothermal reversible process at T\u2081.
w\u2081 = \u2212nRT\u2081 ln(V\u2082/V\u2081) = \u22121 \xD7 R \xD7 T\u2081 ln(V\u2082/V\u2081).
Process 3 is isothermal at T\u2082: w\u2083 = \u2212nRT\u2082 ln(V\u2084/V\u2083) = \u22121 \xD7 R \xD7 T\u2082 ln(V\u2084/V\u2083).
So w\u2081 + w\u2083 = \u2212nRT\u2081 ln(V\u2082/V\u2081) \u2212 nRT\u2082 ln(V\u2084/V\u2083).
Putting n = 1 and R = 2: w\u2081 + w\u2083 = \u22122T\u2081 ln(V\u2082/V\u2081) \u2212 2T\u2082 ln(V\u2084/V\u2083).`,fig:{w:360,h:300,alt:"Pressure\u2013volume diagram of a reversible cycle: process 1 from (p\u2081, V\u2081, T\u2081) to (p\u2082, V\u2082, T\u2081), process 2 down to (p\u2083, V\u2083, T\u2082), process 3 back to (p\u2084, V\u2084, T\u2082), process 4 up to (p\u2081, V\u2081, T\u2081); processes 2 and 4 are adiabatic.",d:[["r",70,10,290,262,""],["p","M123.5 58.5Q180 104 248 107","a"],["p","M248 107Q270 108 285 108",""],["p","M285 108Q285 135 287 150","a"],["p","M287 150Q292 188 318.5 203.6",""],["p","M318.5 203.6Q260 198 226 196","a"],["p","M226 196Q180 193 154 174",""],["p","M154 174Q136 160 132 138","a"],["p","M132 138Q126 100 123.5 58.5",""],["c",123.5,58.5,4,"F"],["c",285,108,4,"F"],["c",318.5,203.6,4,"F"],["c",154,174,4,"F"],["t",94,46,"p\u2081, V\u2081, T\u2081",""],["t",257,99,"p\u2082, V\u2082, T\u2081",""],["t",256,226,"p\u2083, V\u2083, T\u2082",""],["t",122,203,"p\u2084, V\u2084, T\u2082",""],["t",233,96,"1","m"],["t",300,150,"2","m"],["t",226,214,"3","m"],["t",120,148,"4","m"],["t",222,255,"Processes 2 and 4 are adiabatic","m"],["t",62,155,"Pressure","e"],["l",40,140,40,100,"a"],["t",170,292,"Volume","e"],["l",178,288,220,288,"a"]]},sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Thermodynamics (Chemistry)"},{id:"neet-2026-q075",pn:75,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"Some Basic Concepts of Chemistry",st:"Redox titration normality",q:"In an acidic medium, 10 mL of 0.25 M oxalic acid is titrated with KMnO\u2084 solution. If the volume of KMnO\u2084 solution required to reach end point is 10 mL, the strength of the KMnO\u2084 solution is",o:["0.20 M","0.25 M","0.15 M","0.10 M"],c:3,s:`Reaction: KMnO\u2084 + H\u2082C\u2082O\u2084 \u2192 Mn\xB2\u207A + 2CO\u2082 + H\u2082O (acidic medium).
At the end point, equivalents of KMnO\u2084 = equivalents of H\u2082C\u2082O\u2084.
Mn\u2077\u207A \u2192 Mn\xB2\u207A gains 5 electrons, oxalic acid loses 2 electrons.
10 \xD7 5 \xD7 M = 10 \xD7 0.25 \xD7 2
M = 5/50 = 1/10 = 0.10 M`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Some Basic Concepts of Chemistry"},{id:"neet-2026-q076",pn:76,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"The d- and f-Block Elements",st:"Lanthanoids electronic configuration",q:`The lanthanide ion having four unpaired electrons is
(Given : Atomic numbers of Ce = 58, Nd = 60, Tb = 65 and Ho = 67)`,o:["Ce\xB3\u207A","Tb\xB3\u207A","Ho\xB3\u207A","Nd\xB3\u207A"],c:2,s:`Write the outermost configuration of each ion.
Ho\xB3\u207A is 4f\xB9\u2070: three paired orbitals and four single electrons, so 4 unpaired electrons.
Nd\xB3\u207A is 4f\xB3: 3 unpaired electrons.
Ce\xB3\u207A is 4f\xB9: 1 unpaired electron.
Tb\xB3\u207A is 4f\u2078: one paired orbital and six single electrons, so 6 unpaired electrons.
So Ho\xB3\u207A has four unpaired electrons.`,sfig:{w:260,h:172,alt:"4f orbital box diagrams: Ho\xB3\u207A 4f\xB9\u2070 has three paired and four single boxes (4 unpaired electrons); Nd\xB3\u207A 4f\xB3 has 3 and Ce\xB3\u207A 4f\xB9 has 1 unpaired electron; Tb\xB3\u207A 4f\u2078 has one paired and six single boxes (6 unpaired electrons).",d:[["t",10,30,"Ho\xB3\u207A 4f\xB9\u2070",""],["r",80,15,24,20,""],["t",92,30,"\u2191\u2193","m"],["r",104,15,24,20,""],["t",116,30,"\u2191\u2193","m"],["r",128,15,24,20,""],["t",140,30,"\u2191\u2193","m"],["r",152,15,24,20,""],["t",164,30,"\u2191","m"],["r",176,15,24,20,""],["t",188,30,"\u2191","m"],["r",200,15,24,20,""],["t",212,30,"\u2191","m"],["r",224,15,24,20,""],["t",236,30,"\u2191","m"],["t",164,52,"4 unpaired electrons","ms"],["t",10,82,"Nd\xB3\u207A 4f\xB3  3 unpaired electrons",""],["t",10,106,"Ce\xB3\u207A 4f\xB9  1 unpaired electron",""],["t",10,140,"Tb\xB3\u207A 4f\u2078",""],["r",80,125,24,20,""],["t",92,140,"\u2191\u2193","m"],["r",104,125,24,20,""],["t",116,140,"\u2191","m"],["r",128,125,24,20,""],["t",140,140,"\u2191","m"],["r",152,125,24,20,""],["t",164,140,"\u2191","m"],["r",176,125,24,20,""],["t",188,140,"\u2191","m"],["r",200,125,24,20,""],["t",212,140,"\u2191","m"],["r",224,125,24,20,""],["t",236,140,"\u2191","m"],["t",164,162,"6 unpaired electrons","ms"]]},sub:"Chemistry",ch:"The d- and f-Block Elements"},{id:"neet-2026-q077",pn:77,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"Redox Reactions",st:"Oxidation number",q:"The correct decreasing order of oxidation state of the underlined atom in each molecule is",o:["N\u0332\u2082O\u2085 > A\u0332l\u0332\u2082O\u2083 > H\u2082S\u0332","P\u0332b\u0332O\u2082 > N\u0332\u2082O\u2083 > S\u0332O\u2083","P\u0332\u2084O\u2086 > C\u0332l\u0332\u2082O\u2087 > A\u0332l\u0332H\u2083","P\u0332\u2084O\u2081\u2080 > S\u0332O\u2083 > H\u2082O\u0332"],c:0,s:`Underlined atoms: option 1 N, Al, S; option 2 Pb, N, S; option 3 P, Cl, Al; option 4 P, S, O.
P\u2084O\u2086: P = +3, Cl\u2082O\u2087: Cl = +7, AlH\u2083: Al = +3.
P\u2084O\u2081\u2080: P = +5, SO\u2083: S = +6, H\u2082O: O = \u22122.
N\u2082O\u2085: N = +5, Al\u2082O\u2083: Al = +3, H\u2082S: S = \u22122.
PbO\u2082: Pb = +4, N\u2082O\u2083: N = +3, SO\u2083: S = +6.
Only option 1 (+5 > +3 > \u22122) is in decreasing order.
Note: the underlining is hard to see in the printed options; the underlined atoms are taken from the publisher's solution.`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Redox Reactions"},{id:"neet-2026-q078",pn:78,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"Coordination Compounds",st:"Nomenclature of coordination compounds",q:"The formula of tetraammineaquachloridocobalt(III) chloride is",o:["[Co(NH\u2083)\u2084]Cl\u2083 \xD7 H\u2082O","[Co(NH\u2083)\u2084(H\u2082O)Cl]Cl","[Co(NH\u2083)\u2084(H\u2082O)Cl]Cl\u2082","[Co(NH\u2083)\u2084Cl\u2082] \xD7 H\u2082O"],c:2,s:`Ligands: 4 ammine (NH\u2083), 1 aqua (H\u2082O), 1 chlorido (Cl\u207B); central metal Co\xB3\u207A.
Charge on the complex ion = +3 \u2212 1 = +2, so two Cl\u207B outside the bracket.
Formula: [Co(NH\u2083)\u2084(H\u2082O)Cl]Cl\u2082.`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Coordination Compounds"},{id:"neet-2026-q079",pn:79,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"Aldehydes, Ketones and Carboxylic Acids",st:"Etard reaction",q:`Consider the following reaction, and choose the correct option.
toluene \u2192 P  (i. CrO\u2082Cl\u2082, CS\u2082; ii. H\u2083O\u207A)`,o:["Compound P can be prepared by treating benzene with anhydrous AlCl\u2083 and CH\u2083COCl","On treatment with bromine water, compound P gives a white precipitate","Compound P is obtained by the hydrogenation of benzoyl chloride with Pd on BaSO\u2084","On treating compound P with saturated NaHCO\u2083 solution, brisk effervescence is observed"],c:2,s:`Toluene with CrO\u2082Cl\u2082 in CS\u2082 gives a chromium complex, C\u2086H\u2085CH(OCrOHCl\u2082)\u2082 (Etard reaction).
Hydrolysis with H\u2083O\u207A gives benzaldehyde, C\u2086H\u2085CHO. So P is benzaldehyde.
Benzoyl chloride with H\u2082 over Pd-BaSO\u2084 gives benzaldehyde (Rosenmund reaction).
Benzene with CH\u2083COCl and anhydrous AlCl\u2083 gives acetophenone, not P.`,sfig:{w:330,h:345,alt:"Toluene with CrO\u2082Cl\u2082, CS\u2082 gives C\u2086H\u2085CH(OCrOHCl\u2082)\u2082, which with H\u2083O\u207A gives benzaldehyde; benzoyl chloride with H\u2082, Pd-BaSO\u2084 gives benzaldehyde (Rosenmund reaction); benzene with CH\u2083COCl, anhydrous AlCl\u2083 gives acetophenone.",d:[["p","M40.0 42.0 L53.9 50.0 L53.9 66.0 L40.0 74.0 L26.1 66.0 L26.1 50.0 Z",""],["l",40,46.5,50,52.2,""],["l",50,63.8,40,69.5,""],["l",30,63.8,30,52.2,""],["l",40,42,40,34,""],["t",40,31,"CH\u2083","m"],["l",66,58,150,58,"a"],["t",108,50,"CrO\u2082Cl\u2082, CS\u2082","ms"],["p","M180.0 42.0 L193.9 50.0 L193.9 66.0 L180.0 74.0 L166.1 66.0 L166.1 50.0 Z",""],["l",180,46.5,190,52.2,""],["l",190,63.8,180,69.5,""],["l",170,63.8,170,52.2,""],["l",180,42,180,34,""],["t",180,31,"CH(OCrOHCl\u2082)\u2082","m"],["l",180,80,180,118,"a"],["t",186,103,"H\u2083O\u207A","s"],["p","M180.0 144.0 L193.9 152.0 L193.9 168.0 L180.0 176.0 L166.1 168.0 L166.1 152.0 Z",""],["l",180,148.5,190,154.2,""],["l",190,165.8,180,171.5,""],["l",170,165.8,170,154.2,""],["l",180,144,180,136,""],["t",180,133,"CHO","m"],["t",8,236,"Compound P is obtained by","s"],["p","M160.0 216.0 L173.9 224.0 L173.9 240.0 L160.0 248.0 L146.1 240.0 L146.1 224.0 Z",""],["l",160,220.5,170,226.2,""],["l",170,237.8,160,243.5,""],["l",150,237.8,150,226.2,""],["l",160,216,160,208,""],["t",160,205,"COCl","m"],["l",184,232,262,232,"a"],["t",223,226,"H\u2082","ms"],["t",223,245,"Pd-BaSO\u2084","ms"],["p","M290.0 216.0 L303.9 224.0 L303.9 240.0 L290.0 248.0 L276.1 240.0 L276.1 224.0 Z",""],["l",290,220.5,300,226.2,""],["l",300,237.8,290,243.5,""],["l",280,237.8,280,226.2,""],["l",290,216,290,208,""],["t",290,205,"CHO","m"],["t",290,262,"Rosenmund","ms"],["t",290,273,"reaction","ms"],["p","M40.0 302.0 L53.9 310.0 L53.9 326.0 L40.0 334.0 L26.1 326.0 L26.1 310.0 Z",""],["l",40,306.5,50,312.2,""],["l",50,323.8,40,329.5,""],["l",30,323.8,30,312.2,""],["l",62,318,152,318,"a"],["t",105,310,"CH\u2083COCl, Anhy.","ms"],["t",107,331,"AlCl\u2083","ms"],["p","M180.0 302.0 L193.9 310.0 L193.9 326.0 L180.0 334.0 L166.1 326.0 L166.1 310.0 Z",""],["l",180,306.5,190,312.2,""],["l",190,323.8,180,329.5,""],["l",170,323.8,170,312.2,""],["l",180,302,180,294,""],["t",180,291,"COCH\u2083","m"]]},sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Aldehydes, Ketones and Carboxylic Acids"},{id:"neet-2026-q080",pn:80,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"Solutions",st:"Ideal solutions",q:`Assertion A: For an ideal solution formed by mixing liquids P and Q, \u0394mixH = 0 and \u0394mixV = 0
Reason R: No interactions occur between P and Q
In the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below.`,o:["Both A and R are correct but R is NOT the correct explanation of A","A is correct but R is not correct","A is not correct but R is correct","Both A and R are correct and R is the correct explanation of A"],c:1,s:`For an ideal solution, \u0394mixH = 0 and \u0394mixV = 0, so A is correct.
The energy needed to break P\u2013P and Q\u2013Q interactions equals the energy released on forming P\u2013Q interactions.
So interactions do occur between P and Q, and R is not correct.`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Solutions"},{id:"neet-2026-q081",pn:81,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"Biomolecules (Chemistry)",st:"Vitamins and their sources",q:`Match the vitamins in List I with their sources in List II
A. vitamin A \u2014 I. meat
B. vitamin B\u2081\u2082 \u2014 II. sunflower oil
C. vitamin E \u2014 III. green leafy vegetables
D. vitamin K \u2014 IV. carrots
Choose the correct answer from the options given below.`,o:["A-IV, B-I, C-II, D-III","A-IV, B-II, C-I, D-III","A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II","A-II, B-III, C-IV, D-I"],c:0,s:`Vitamin A: carrot.
Vitamin B\u2081\u2082: meat.
Vitamin E: sunflower oil.
Vitamin K: green leafy vegetables.
So A-IV, B-I, C-II, D-III.`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Biomolecules (Chemistry)"},{id:"neet-2026-q082",pn:82,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"Hydrocarbons",st:"Conjugated dienes",q:"Among the following, the compound having conjugated double bonds is",o:["hepta-1,4-diene","hepta-1,5-diene","hepta-1,6-diene","hepta-1,3-diene"],c:3,s:`A conjugated diene has two double bonds separated by one single bond.
hepta-1,3-diene: CH\u2083\u2013CH\u2082\u2013CH\u2082\u2013CH=CH\u2013CH=CH\u2082, double bonds at C1 and C3, one single bond between them.
hepta-1,4-diene: CH\u2083\u2013CH\u2082\u2013CH=CH\u2013CH\u2082\u2013CH=CH\u2082 has C1=C2 and C4=C5 with a CH\u2082 between them (not conjugated).
hepta-1,5-diene: CH\u2083\u2013CH=CH\u2013CH\u2082\u2013CH\u2082\u2013CH=CH\u2082 is not conjugated.
hepta-1,6-diene: H\u2082C=CH\u2013CH\u2082\u2013CH\u2082\u2013CH\u2082\u2013CH=CH\u2082 is not conjugated.
Note: Publisher's printed chain for hepta-1,4-diene looks garbled; I wrote the correct structures.`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Hydrocarbons"},{id:"neet-2026-q083",pn:83,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"Amines",st:"Grignard reagent with amines",q:`One of the products formed in the following reaction is
cyclohexyl\u2013MgBr + cyclohexyl\u2013NH\u2082 \u2192`,o:["2-aminobicyclohexyl (two cyclohexane rings joined by a single bond, NH\u2082 on a ring carbon next to the junction)","bicyclohexyl (two cyclohexane rings joined by a single bond)","cyclohexane","dicyclohexylamine (two cyclohexane rings joined through NH)"],c:2,s:`Grignard reagents react with amines by taking a proton from N\u2013H.
The carbon of cyclohexyl\u2013MgBr (carbanion-like, \u03B4\u2212) takes H from the NH\u2082 group.
This gives cyclohexane as the hydrocarbon product.`,fig:{w:290,h:200,alt:"Cyclohexyl\u2013MgBr plus cyclohexyl\u2013NH\u2082 reacting (arrow). Options: (1) two cyclohexane rings joined by a bond with NH\u2082 on the ring carbon next to the junction; (2) two cyclohexane rings joined by a single bond; (3) cyclohexane; (4) two cyclohexane rings joined through an NH group.",d:[["p","M45.0 25.0 L37.5 38.0 L22.5 38.0 L15.0 25.0 L22.5 12.0 L37.5 12.0 Z",""],["l",45,25,55,25,""],["t",57,29,"MgBr",""],["t",92,29,"+",""],["p","M137.0 25.0 L129.5 38.0 L114.5 38.0 L107.0 25.0 L114.5 12.0 L129.5 12.0 Z",""],["l",137,25,147,25,""],["t",149,29,"NH\u2082",""],["l",178,25,215,25,"a"],["t",5,89,"(1)",""],["p","M45.0 70.0 L58.0 77.5 L58.0 92.5 L45.0 100.0 L32.0 92.5 L32.0 77.5 Z",""],["p","M87.0 62.5 L100.0 70.0 L100.0 85.0 L87.0 92.5 L74.0 85.0 L74.0 70.0 Z",""],["l",58,77.5,74,70,""],["l",58,92.5,66,102.5,""],["t",64,114.5,"NH\u2082",""],["t",165,89,"(2)",""],["p","M225.0 85.0 L217.5 98.0 L202.5 98.0 L195.0 85.0 L202.5 72.0 L217.5 72.0 Z",""],["l",225,85,237,85,""],["p","M267.0 85.0 L259.5 98.0 L244.5 98.0 L237.0 85.0 L244.5 72.0 L259.5 72.0 Z",""],["t",5,169,"(3)",""],["p","M45.0 150.0 L58.0 157.5 L58.0 172.5 L45.0 180.0 L32.0 172.5 L32.0 157.5 Z",""],["t",165,174,"(4)",""],["p","M205.0 155.0 L218.0 162.5 L218.0 177.5 L205.0 185.0 L192.0 177.5 L192.0 162.5 Z",""],["l",218,162.5,227,153.5,""],["t",231,152.5,"N","m"],["t",231,139.5,"H","m"],["l",235,153.5,244,162.5,""],["p","M257.0 155.0 L270.0 162.5 L270.0 177.5 L257.0 185.0 L244.0 177.5 L244.0 162.5 Z",""]]},sfig:{w:290,h:112,alt:"Mechanism: a curved arrow from the \u03B4\u207B carbon of the C\u2013MgBr bond to an H on the NH\u2082 of cyclohexylamine; the product is cyclohexane.",d:[["p","M45.0 40.0 L37.5 53.0 L22.5 53.0 L15.0 40.0 L22.5 27.0 L37.5 27.0 Z",""],["l",45,40,58,40,""],["t",44,30,"\u03B4\u207B","s"],["t",59,44,"MgBr",""],["t",95,44,"+",""],["p","M137.0 40.0 L129.5 53.0 L114.5 53.0 L107.0 40.0 L114.5 27.0 L129.5 27.0 Z",""],["l",137,40,149,40,""],["t",150,44,"N",""],["l",160,35,169,27,""],["t",170,27,"H",""],["l",160,45,169,53,""],["t",170,62,"H",""],["p","M 50 47 C 58 92 165 92 171 70","a"],["p","M 165 53 Q 160 64 153 48","a"],["l",190,40,225,40,"a"],["p","M250.0 25.0 L263.0 32.5 L263.0 47.5 L250.0 55.0 L237.0 47.5 L237.0 32.5 Z",""],["t",250,72,"(Cyclohexane)","ms"]]},sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Amines"},{id:"neet-2026-q084",pn:84,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"Thermodynamics (Chemistry)",st:"Entropy change, free expansion",q:`Two moles of an ideal gas undergo free expansion from 10 L to 100 L at 300 K. The values of \u0394S_system and \u0394S_surroundings are
(R is universal gas constant)`,o:["\u0394S_system = 4.606 R; \u0394S_surroundings = \u2013 4.606 R","\u0394S_system = 0; \u0394S_surroundings = 4.606 R","\u0394S_system = 4.606 R; \u0394S_surroundings = 0","\u0394S_system = 0; \u0394S_surroundings = 0"],c:2,s:`In free expansion, \u0394S_system = nR ln(V\u2082/V\u2081).
\u0394S_system = 2.303 \xD7 2 \xD7 R \xD7 log(100/10).
\u0394S_system = 4.606 R.
In free expansion q = 0, so \u0394S_surroundings = 0.`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Thermodynamics (Chemistry)"},{id:"neet-2026-q085",pn:85,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties",st:"Electron gain enthalpy trend",q:"Among the following options, the correct trend in the electron gain enthalpy is",o:["Br > Cl > F > I","Cl > F > Br > I","I > Br > Cl > F","F > Cl > Br > I"],c:1,s:`Electron gain enthalpies of group 17 (kJ/mol): F = \u2013328, Cl = \u2013349, Br = \u2013325, I = \u2013295.
So the correct order of electron gain enthalpy is Cl > F > Br > I.`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties"},{id:"neet-2026-q086",pn:86,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"Equilibrium",st:"Solubility product and solubility",q:`The correct order of solubility of the given salts in water at 298 K is
Salt \u2014 Ksp at 298 K
AgBr \u2014 5.0 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\xB3
Zn(OH)\u2082 \u2014 1.0 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2075
Hg\u2082Cl\u2082 \u2014 1.3 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2078`,o:["AgBr > Zn(OH)\u2082 > Hg\u2082Cl\u2082","Hg\u2082Cl\u2082 > AgBr > Zn(OH)\u2082","Zn(OH)\u2082 > AgBr > Hg\u2082Cl\u2082","Hg\u2082Cl\u2082 > Zn(OH)\u2082 > AgBr"],c:2,s:`AgBr(s) \u21CC Ag\u207A(aq) + Br\u207B(aq).
Ksp = 5 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\xB3 = S\xB2, so S = \u221A(5 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\xB3) = 7.07 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 mol/L.
Zn(OH)\u2082(s) \u21CC Zn\xB2\u207A(aq) + 2OH\u207B(aq).
Ksp = 10\u207B\xB9\u2075 = (S)(2S)\xB2 = 4S\xB3, so S = 0.63 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 = 6.3 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 mol/L.
Hg\u2082Cl\u2082(s) \u21CC Hg\u2082\xB2\u207A(aq) + 2Cl\u207B(aq).
Ksp = 1.3 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2078 = 4S\xB3, so S = 0.69 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 = 6.9 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 mol/L.
Correct order of solubility: Zn(OH)\u2082 > AgBr > Hg\u2082Cl\u2082.`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Equilibrium"},{id:"neet-2026-q087",pn:87,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"Hydrocarbons",st:"Alkyne reduction and HBr addition",q:`Consider the following reaction sequences and choose the correct option.
Ph\u2013C\u2261C\u2013Me on treatment with H\u2082, Pd/C (Lindlar's catalyst) gives K; K on treatment with HBr gives M.
Ph\u2013C\u2261C\u2013Me on treatment with Na/liq. NH\u2083 gives L; L on treatment with HBr and benzoyl peroxide gives N.`,o:["K and L are enantiomers","M and N are geometrical isomers","M and N are stereoisomers","K and L are geometrical isomers"],c:3,s:`Lindlar's catalyst gives the cis alkene K (Ph and Me on the same side).
Na/liq. NH\u2083 gives the trans alkene L (Ph and CH\u2083 on opposite sides).
K and L differ only in the arrangement about the double bond, so they are geometrical isomers.
HBr adds to K to give M = Ph\u2013CH(Br)\u2013CH\u2082\u2013CH\u2083 (Br on the carbon next to Ph).
HBr with benzoyl peroxide adds to L in the anti-Markovnikov way to give N = Ph\u2013CH\u2082\u2013CH(Br)\u2013CH\u2083.`,fig:{w:330,h:110,alt:"Ph\u2013C\u2261C\u2013Me with H\u2082, Pd/C (Lindlar\u2019s catalyst) gives K, and K with HBr gives M; Ph\u2013C\u2261C\u2013Me with Na/liq. NH\u2083 gives L, and L with HBr, benzoyl peroxide gives N.",d:[["t",14,24,"L","m"],["l",88,20,25,20,"a"],["t",56,13,"Na/liq. NH\u2083","m"],["t",150,24,"Ph \u2013 C \u2261 C \u2013 Me","m"],["l",215,20,295,20,"a"],["t",255,13,"H\u2082, Pd/C","m"],["t",255,34,"(Lindlar\u2019s Catalyst)","ms"],["t",305,24,"K","m"],["l",14,30,14,88,"a"],["t",24,48,"HBr","s"],["t",24,61,"benzoyl peroxide","s"],["t",14,103,"N","m"],["l",305,30,305,88,"a"],["t",312,60,"HBr","s"],["t",305,103,"M","m"]]},sfig:{w:455,h:172,alt:"Solution scheme: Ph\u2013C\u2261C\u2013Me with Na/liq NH\u2083 gives trans alkene L (Ph and H on one carbon, H and CH\u2083 on the other, Ph and CH\u2083 opposite) and with H\u2082, Pd/C (Lindlar\u2019s catalyst) gives cis alkene K (Ph and Me on the same side). L with HBr, benzoyl peroxide gives N = Ph\u2013CH\u2082\u2013CH(Br)\u2013CH\u2083; K with HBr gives M = Ph\u2013CH(Br)\u2013CH\u2082\u2013CH\u2083.",d:[["t",40,34,"C=C","m"],["l",28,27,20,19,""],["t",18,18,"Ph","e"],["l",52,27,60,19,""],["t",62,18,"H",""],["l",28,33,20,41,""],["t",18,49,"H","e"],["l",52,33,60,41,""],["t",62,49,"CH\u2083",""],["t",40,58,"(L)","ms"],["l",85,26,140,26,"a"],["t",112,19,"Na/liq NH\u2083","ms"],["t",205,30,"Ph \u2013 C \u2261 C \u2013 Me","m"],["l",270,26,345,26,"a"],["t",307,19,"H\u2082, Pd/C","ms"],["t",307,40,"(Lindlar\u2019s Catalyst)","ms"],["t",385,34,"C=C","m"],["l",373,27,365,19,""],["t",363,18,"Ph","e"],["l",397,27,405,19,""],["t",407,18,"Me",""],["l",373,33,365,41,""],["t",363,49,"H","e"],["l",397,33,405,41,""],["t",407,49,"H",""],["t",385,58,"(K)","ms"],["l",40,66,40,108,"a"],["t",47,80,"HBr","s"],["t",47,92,"benzoyl peroxide","s"],["t",8,124,"Ph \u2013 CH\u2082 \u2013 CH \u2013 CH\u2083",""],["l",77,128,77,138,""],["t",77,150,"Br","m"],["t",77,164,"(N)","ms"],["l",385,66,385,96,"a"],["t",392,84,"HBr","s"],["t",385,112,"Ph \u2013 CH \u2013 CH\u2082 \u2013 CH\u2083","m"],["l",363,116,363,126,""],["t",363,138,"Br","m"],["t",363,152,"(M)","ms"]]},sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Hydrocarbons"},{id:"neet-2026-q088",pn:88,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"Some Basic Concepts of Chemistry",st:"Stoichiometry, combustion",q:`The amount of carbon dioxide evolved upon complete combustion of 116 g of n-butane is
(Given: atomic mass in amu H = 1, C = 12 and O = 16)`,o:["322 g","176 g","362 g","352 g"],c:3,s:`C\u2084H\u2081\u2080 + 13/2 O\u2082 \u2192 4CO\u2082 + 5H\u2082O.
1 mole of C\u2084H\u2081\u2080 produces 4 mole of CO\u2082.
58 g C\u2084H\u2081\u2080 gives 4 \xD7 44 g CO\u2082.
So 116 g C\u2084H\u2081\u2080 gives (4 \xD7 44 / 58) \xD7 116 = 352 g CO\u2082.`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Some Basic Concepts of Chemistry"},{id:"neet-2026-q089",pn:89,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"Some Basic Concepts of Chemistry",st:"Rounding off and significant figures",q:"The numbers 17.0145 and 21.0235 were rounded to three figures after the decimal point. The resulting numbers, respectively, are",o:["17.015 and 21.023","17.014 and 21.024","17.015 and 21.024","17.014 and 21.023"],c:1,s:`Rule: if the digit to be removed is 5, the preceding digit stays the same if it is even.
If the preceding digit is odd, it is increased by one.
In 17.0145 the preceding digit is 4 (even), so it becomes 17.014.
In 21.0235 the preceding digit is 3 (odd), so it becomes 21.024.`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Some Basic Concepts of Chemistry"},{id:"neet-2026-q090",pn:90,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"The p-Block Elements",st:"Potash alum formula",q:"In potash alum, the ratio of K\u207A and SO\u2084\xB2\u207B ions is",o:["2 : 1","2 : 3","3 : 2","1 : 2"],c:3,s:`Potash alum is K\u2082SO\u2084\xB7Al\u2082(SO\u2084)\u2083\xB724H\u2082O.
No. of K\u207A = 2.
No. of SO\u2084\xB2\u207B = 4.
Ratio K\u207A : SO\u2084\xB2\u207B = 2/4 = 1/2.`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"The p-Block Elements"},{id:"neet-2026-q091",pn:91,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"Locomotion and Movement",st:"Human vertebral column",q:"The number of vertebrae in a human is ________.",o:["12","26","206","7"],c:1,s:`The number of vertebrae in an adult human is 26.
Note: Printed under the BIOLOGY heading at 91 (booklet order says Botany); question content is Zoology, so sub set to Zoology.`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Locomotion and Movement"},{id:"neet-2026-q092",pn:92,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"Chemical Coordination and Integration",st:"Pancreatic islet hormones",q:"Which one of the following statements is incorrect?",o:["\u03B1-cells of pancreas secrete insulin","Glucagon stimulates glycogenolysis","\u03B2-cells of pancreas secrete insulin","\u03B1-cells of pancreas secrete glucagon"],c:0,s:`The two main cell types of the islets of Langerhans are \u03B1-cells and \u03B2-cells.
\u03B1-cells secrete glucagon, while \u03B2-cells secrete insulin.
Glucagon stimulates glycogenolysis, while insulin stimulates glycogenesis.
So the statement that \u03B1-cells secrete insulin is incorrect.`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Chemical Coordination and Integration"},{id:"neet-2026-q093",pn:93,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"Animal Kingdom",st:"Reptilia characteristics",q:`Given below are two statements:
Statement I : The class name Reptilia refers to creeping or crawling mode of locomotion.
Statement II : All organisms belonging to Reptilia have three chambered heart.
In the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:`,o:["Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are correct"],c:1,s:`The name Reptilia refers to their creeping or crawling mode of locomotion.
The heart is usually three-chambered, but four-chambered in crocodiles.
So not all reptiles have a three-chambered heart.`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Animal Kingdom"},{id:"neet-2026-q094",pn:94,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"Chemical Coordination and Integration",st:"Pituitary gland",q:`Which of the following statements related to pituitary gland are correct?
(a) It is divided anatomically into adenohypophysis and neurohypophysis
(b) It secretes follicle stimulating hormone
(c) It secretes melanocyte stimulating hormone
(d) It does not secrete prolactin
Choose the correct answer from the options given below :`,o:["(a), (b) and (c) only","(c) and (d) only","(b) and (c) only","(a) and (b) only"],c:0,s:`Adenohypophysis of the pituitary gland also secretes prolactin, so (d) is wrong.
(a), (b) and (c) are correct.`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Chemical Coordination and Integration"},{id:"neet-2026-q095",pn:95,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"Biomolecules (Biology)",st:"Functions of biomolecules",q:`Match List-I with List-II.
A. Starch \u2014 I. Fights infection
B. Antibody \u2014 II. Energy storage
C. Concanavalin A \u2014 III. Glucose transport
D. Glut-4 \u2014 IV. Lectin
Choose the correct answer from the options given below :`,o:["A-II, B-I, C-IV, D-III","A-II, B-I, C-III, D-IV","A-I, B-II, C-III, D-IV","A-I, B-II, C-IV, D-III"],c:0,s:`Starch \u2192 Energy storage.
Antibody \u2192 Fights infection.
Concanavalin A \u2192 Lectin.
Glut-4 \u2192 Glucose transport.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Biomolecules (Biology)"},{id:"neet-2026-q096",pn:96,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"Structural Organisation in Animals",st:"Frog respiration",q:"In water, frogs respire using _____________.",o:["buccal cavity","lungs","trachea","skin"],c:3,s:`Frogs respire differently on land and in water.
In water, the skin acts as the respiratory organ (cutaneous respiration).
Dissolved oxygen in water is exchanged through the skin by diffusion.
On land, the buccal cavity, skin and lungs act as respiratory organs.`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Structural Organisation in Animals"},{id:"neet-2026-q097",pn:97,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"Cell: The Unit of Life",st:"Cell organelles structure",q:`Match List-I with List-II.
A. Cristae \u2014 I. Flat membrane sacs in stroma of chloroplast
B. Cisternae \u2014 II. Infoldings in mitochondria
C. Thylakoids \u2014 III. Cell membrane
D. Phospholipid \u2014 IV. Disc shaped sacs in the Golgi apparatus
Choose the correct answer from the options given below:`,o:["A-II, B-IV, C-I, D-III","A-II, B-IV, C-III, D-I","A-IV, B-III, C-I, D-II","A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II"],c:0,s:`Cristae \u2192 Infoldings in mitochondria.
Cisternae \u2192 Disc shaped sacs in the Golgi apparatus.
Thylakoids \u2192 Flat membrane sacs in stroma of chloroplast.
Phospholipid \u2192 Cell membrane.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Cell: The Unit of Life"},{id:"neet-2026-q098",pn:98,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"Morphology of Flowering Plants",st:"Phyllotaxy",q:"Phyllotaxy is the pattern of arrangement of ________.",o:["flowers","fruits","sepals","leaves"],c:3,s:"Phyllotaxy is the pattern of arrangement of leaves on the stem.",sub:"Botany",ch:"Morphology of Flowering Plants"},{id:"neet-2026-q099",pn:99,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"Biological Classification",st:"Bacterial shapes",q:`Match List-I with List-II.
A. Spherical \u2014 I. Vibrio
B. Rod \u2014 II. Cocci
C. Comma \u2014 III. Spirilla
D. Spirillum \u2014 IV. Bacilli
Choose the correct answer from the options given below :`,o:["A-III, B-II, C-I, D-IV","A-II, B-I, C-IV, D-III","A-II, B-IV, C-I, D-III","A-I, B-III, C-II, D-IV"],c:2,s:`Bacteria are grouped into four categories by shape.
Spherical \u2192 Coccus.
Rod-shaped \u2192 Bacillus.
Comma-shaped \u2192 Vibrio.
Spiral \u2192 Spirillum.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Biological Classification"},{id:"neet-2026-q100",pn:100,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"Biomolecules (Biology)",st:"Elements in human body",q:`Arrange the following elements in descending order of their contribution to percentage weight of the human body.
(a) Oxygen
(b) Carbon
(c) Hydrogen
(d) Nitrogen
Choose the correct answer from the options given below :`,o:["(c), (a), (b), (d)","(b), (c), (d), (a)","(b), (a), (c), (d)","(a), (b), (c), (d)"],c:3,s:`The descending order of percentage weight in the human body is:
Oxygen (65%) \u2192 Carbon (18.5%) \u2192 Hydrogen (9.5%) \u2192 Nitrogen (3.3%).`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Biomolecules (Biology)"},{id:"neet-2026-q101",pn:101,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"Plant Growth and Development",st:"Arithmetic growth",q:"Length of the stem at time 0 is 20 cm. The arithmetic growth rate is 30 cm per day. What is the length of the stem at the end of the 7\u1D57\u02B0 day?",o:["170 cm","230 cm","460 cm","50 cm"],c:1,s:`Arithmetic growth: L\u209C = L\u2080 + rt.
Here L\u209C = length at time t, L\u2080 = length at time zero, r = growth rate.
L(7th day) = 20 + 30 \xD7 7
= 20 + 210
= 230 cm.
So the stem is 230 cm long at the end of the 7\u1D57\u02B0 day.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Plant Growth and Development"},{id:"neet-2026-q102",pn:102,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"Plant Kingdom",st:"Gymnosperms",q:`Given below are two statements:
Statement I: In gymnosperms, the male and female gametophytes remain within the sporangia.
Statement II: In gymnosperms, seeds are not covered.
In the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:`,o:["Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are correct"],c:3,s:`In gymnosperms the male and female gametophytes have no independent free-living existence.
They stay within the sporangia, retained on the sporophyte.
Gymnosperm ovules are not enclosed by any ovary wall and stay exposed before and after fertilisation.
The seeds that develop after fertilisation are not covered, i.e. naked.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Plant Kingdom"},{id:"neet-2026-q103",pn:103,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"Cell: The Unit of Life",st:"Endoplasmic reticulum",q:"Smooth endoplasmic reticulum ___________ .",o:["is the major site for the synthesis of lipids","is actively involved in protein synthesis","is a site for the synthesis of carbohydrates","has ribosomes attached to its surface"],c:0,s:`Smooth endoplasmic reticulum (SER) makes lipids and steroidal hormones.
It has no ribosomes on it, so it looks smooth.
Rough ER is the one actively involved in protein synthesis.
Carbohydrate synthesis occurs in chloroplasts.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Cell: The Unit of Life"},{id:"neet-2026-q104",pn:104,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"Animal Kingdom",st:"Characters of chordates",q:"Which of the following is not a characteristic of chordates?",o:["Central nervous system is dorsal","Absence of gills","Presence of post anal part (tail)","Presence of notochord"],c:1,s:`Members of phylum Chordata show these features at some stage of life:
Presence of notochord.
Pharynx perforated by gill slits.
Dorsal, hollow and single central nervous system.
Ventral heart.
A post-anal tail.
Gill slits are absent in non-chordates, so 'absence of gills' is not a chordate character.
Note: Question sits in the Botany block of the booklet but is Zoology content; sub set to Zoology.`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Animal Kingdom"},{id:"neet-2026-q105",pn:105,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"Respiration in Plants",st:"Glycolysis products",q:"How many molecules of pyruvic acid are produced at the end of glycolysis from 206 molecules of glucose?",o:["309","103","412","206"],c:2,s:`One glucose molecule gives two molecules of pyruvic acid at the end of glycolysis.
So 206 glucose molecules give 206 \xD7 2 = 412 molecules of pyruvic acid.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Respiration in Plants"},{id:"neet-2026-q106",pn:106,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"Cell Cycle and Cell Division",st:"Phases of cell cycle",q:"The correct sequence of adult cell cycle phases is ________.",o:["G1-M-G2-S","G1-S-G2-M","S-M-G2-G1","G1-G2-S-M"],c:1,s:`Gap 1 (G1) is the first phase of interphase.
After G1, the synthesis (S) phase starts and DNA replicates.
Then the Gap 2 (G2) phase occurs.
Finally the cell cycle ends with M phase (mitosis).
Sequence: G1-S-G2-M.`,sfig:{w:385,h:345,alt:"Cell cycle circle: G\u2081 (largest sector), then S, then G\u2082, then a narrow M Phase sector divided into Prophase, Metaphase, Anaphase, Telophase and Cytokinesis; a thick arrow runs clockwise round the outer ring; cells leave the cycle at the top into G\u2080.",d:[["c",240,205,125,""],["c",240,205,108,""],["p","M 240 205 L 170.6 122.3 A 108 108 0 0 1 346.4 186.2 Z",""],["p","M 240 205 L 346.4 186.2 A 108 108 0 0 1 285.6 302.9 Z",""],["p","M 240 205 L 285.6 302.9 A 108 108 0 0 1 138.5 241.9 Z",""],["p","M 240 205 L 138.5 241.9 A 108 108 0 0 1 133.1 220.0 Z","f"],["l",104,216,161.4,224.6,"d"],["t",100,219,"Prophase","es"],["p","M 240 205 L 133.1 220.0 A 108 108 0 0 1 132.3 197.5 Z","f"],["l",104,202,159,207.8,"d"],["t",100,205,"Metaphase","es"],["p","M 240 205 L 132.3 197.5 A 108 108 0 0 1 136.2 175.2 Z","f"],["l",104,188,160.2,190.9,"d"],["t",100,191,"Anaphase","es"],["p","M 240 205 L 136.2 175.2 A 108 108 0 0 1 144.6 154.3 Z","f"],["l",104,174,164.9,174.7,"d"],["t",100,177,"Telophase","es"],["p","M 240 205 L 144.6 154.3 A 108 108 0 0 1 170.6 122.3 Z","f"],["l",104,160,177.1,154,"d"],["t",100,163,"Cytokinesis","es"],["p","M 130.5 244.8 A 116.5 116.5 0 0 1 165.1 115.8","B"],["p","M 163.7 96.1 A 133 133 0 0 1 263.1 74.0","Ba"],["t",260.3,149.2,"G\u2081","m"],["t",297.7,238.4,"S","m"],["t",214.9,262.8,"G\u2082","m"],["t",8,115,"M Phase",""],["c",137.6,91.3,9,"f"],["c",138.6,92.3,3.5,"F"],["c",170.5,68.7,9,"f"],["c",171.5,69.7,3.5,"F"],["c",261.3,53.5,9,"f"],["c",262.3,54.5,3.5,"F"],["p","M 299.3 45.5 A 10 10 0 1 0 285.3 43.5","a"],["t",291.3,55.5,"G\u2080","ms"]]},sub:"Botany",ch:"Cell Cycle and Cell Division"},{id:"neet-2026-q107",pn:107,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"Locomotion and Movement",st:"Bones of lower limb",q:"Which of the following represents the correct sequence of arrangement of bones in the lower limb of humans?",o:["Patella-femur-tibia-tarsal","Femur-patella-tibia-tarsal","Femur-tarsal-patella-tibia","Femur-tibia-patella-tarsal"],c:1,s:`Each hindlimb has 30 bones.
The lower limb bones are femur (thigh bone), patella, tibia and fibula, tarsals (ankle bones), metatarsals and phalanges.
The cup-shaped patella (knee cap) covers the knee ventrally.
So the sequence is femur - patella - tibia - tarsal.
Note: Question sits in the Botany block of the booklet but is Zoology content; sub set to Zoology.`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Locomotion and Movement"},{id:"neet-2026-q108",pn:108,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"Cell: The Unit of Life",st:"Cell theory",q:"Cell theory was formulated by ______________.",o:["Robert Brown","Singer and Nicolson","Antonie Von Leeuwenhoek","Schleiden and Schwann"],c:3,s:"Cell theory was formulated by Schleiden and Schwann.",sub:"Botany",ch:"Cell: The Unit of Life"},{id:"neet-2026-q109",pn:109,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"Cell Cycle and Cell Division",st:"Meiosis",q:`Given below are two statements :
Statement I : Chromosomes are fully condensed at the end of prophase I.
Statement II : Meiosis I resembles mitosis.
In the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below :`,o:["Both Statement I and Statement II are false","Statement I is correct, but Statement II is false","Statement I is incorrect, but Statement II is true","Both Statement I and Statement II are true"],c:1,s:`The last stage of meiotic prophase I is diakinesis.
At the end of prophase I the chromosomes are fully condensed and the meiotic spindle is assembled, ready to separate the homologous chromosomes.
So Statement I is correct.
It is meiosis II that resembles mitosis, as it is an equational division.
So Statement II is false.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Cell Cycle and Cell Division"},{id:"neet-2026-q110",pn:110,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants",st:"Photorespiration",q:`Which of the following statements regarding photorespiration are correct?
(a) Do not occur in C3 plants
(b) CO\u2082 is consumed and O\u2082 is generated
(c) Phosphoglycolate is formed
(d) No synthesis of ATP and NADPH
Choose the correct answer from the options given below:`,o:["(c) and (d) only","(b) and (d) only","(a) and (b) only","(a) and (d) only"],c:0,s:`Photorespiration can occur in C3 plants, so (a) is wrong.
In photorespiration O\u2082 is consumed and CO\u2082 is released, so (b) is wrong.
Phosphoglycolate is formed as the first product, so (c) is correct.
There is no synthesis of ATP and NADPH in photorespiration, so (d) is correct.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants"},{id:"neet-2026-q111",pn:111,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"Cell: The Unit of Life",st:"Prokaryotic cell",q:`Which of the following are characteristics of prokaryotic cells?
(a) Ribosomes are made of 50S and 30S subunits
(b) They can have plasmids
(c) They contain mesosome
(d) They have peroxisomes
Choose the correct answer from the options given below :`,o:["(a) and (c) only","(a), (c) and (d) only","(a), (b) and (c) only","(b) and (c) only"],c:2,s:`Ribosomes are made of 50S and 30S subunits (70S ribosomes).
They can have plasmids (extra-chromosomal DNA).
They contain mesosomes (infoldings of the plasma membrane).
They do not have membrane-bound organelles such as peroxisomes.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Cell: The Unit of Life"},{id:"neet-2026-q112",pn:112,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"Body Fluids and Circulation",st:"Cardiac impulse, SAN",q:"The number of action potentials generated by sino-atrial node (SAN) in a healthy human is _______ per minute.",o:["70 \u2013 75","100 \u2013 110","120 - 140","28 - 30"],c:0,s:`The SAN in a healthy human generates 70 \u2013 75 action potentials per minute.
Note: Question sits in the Botany block of the booklet but is Zoology content; sub set to Zoology.`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Body Fluids and Circulation"},{id:"neet-2026-q113",pn:113,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"Cell: The Unit of Life",st:"Endomembrane system",q:"Endomembrane system includes _______.",o:["endoplasmic reticulum, chloroplast, peroxisomes and vacuole","mitochondria, chloroplast, peroxisomes and vacuole","Golgi complex, chloroplast, peroxisomes and vacuole","endoplasmic reticulum, Golgi complex, lysosomes and vacuole"],c:3,s:`The endomembrane system includes organelles whose functions are coordinated.
It has four organelles: endoplasmic reticulum, Golgi body, lysosomes and vacuole.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Cell: The Unit of Life"},{id:"neet-2026-q114",pn:114,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"The Living World",st:"Taxonomic categories",q:`Match List-I with List-II.
A. Family \u2014 I. Sapindales
B. Genus \u2014 II. Dicotyledonae
C. Class \u2014 III. Anacardiaceae
D. Phylum \u2014 IV. Angiospermae
E. Order \u2014 V. Mangifera
Choose the correct answer from the options given below :`,o:["A-II, B-I, C-III, D-IV, E-V","A-II, B-III, C-V, D-I, E-IV","A-III, B-V, C-II, D-IV, E-I","A-I, B-V, C-II, D-IV, E-III"],c:2,s:`Mangifera is a genus.
Anacardiaceae is a family.
Sapindales is an order.
Dicotyledonae is a class.
Angiospermae is given as phylum (the publisher notes it should correctly be 'division').
So A-III, B-V, C-II, D-IV, E-I.
Note: Printed 'Answer (3*) (Conceptual errata)': Angiospermae is a division, not a phylum in plants; answer 3 kept.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"The Living World"},{id:"neet-2026-q115",pn:115,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"Plant Growth and Development",st:"Gibberellin",q:"Which of the following plant growth regulators promotes internode elongation prior to flowering in cabbage?",o:["Gibberellin","Indole butyric acid","Ethephon","Abscisic acid"],c:0,s:"Gibberellin promotes internode elongation prior to flowering in cabbage.",sub:"Botany",ch:"Plant Growth and Development"},{id:"neet-2026-q116",pn:116,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"Cell: The Unit of Life",st:"Plastids",q:"The plastid that stores xanthophyll is known as ___________.",o:["chromoplast","aleuroplast","amyloplast","chloroplast"],c:0,s:"The plastid that stores xanthophyll is called a chromoplast.",sub:"Botany",ch:"Cell: The Unit of Life"},{id:"neet-2026-q117",pn:117,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants",st:"Photorespiration",q:`Photorespiration reaction catalyzed by RuBisCo is shown below:
RuBP + O\u2082 \u2192 3-Phosphoglycerate + X.
Identify "X" from the given options:`,o:["2-Phosphoglycolate","Oxaloacetate","Malate","Phosphoenolpyruvate"],c:0,s:`In photorespiration RuBP binds with O\u2082.
Instead of giving 2 molecules of PGA, it gives one molecule each of phosphoglycerate and phosphoglycolate.
So X is 2-phosphoglycolate.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants"},{id:"neet-2026-q118",pn:118,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"Morphology of Flowering Plants",st:"Placentation",q:`Match List-I with List-II.
A. Marginal placentation \u2014 I. Argemone
B. Axile placentation \u2014 II. Tomato
C. Parietal placentation \u2014 III. Primrose
D. Free central placentation \u2014 IV. Pea
Choose the correct answer from the options given below :`,o:["A-IV, B-II, C-III, D-I","A-IV, B-III, C-I, D-II","A-IV, B-II, C-I, D-III","A-II, B-IV, C-I, D-III"],c:2,s:`Marginal placentation \u2013 Pea.
Axile placentation \u2013 Tomato.
Parietal placentation \u2013 Argemone.
Free central placentation \u2013 Primrose.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Morphology of Flowering Plants"},{id:"neet-2026-q119",pn:119,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"Morphology of Flowering Plants",st:"Family Solanaceae",q:`Which of the following are characteristic features of Solanaceae family?
(a) Flowers are bisexual and actinomorphic
(b) Calyx have five sepals and are united
(c) Androecium have five stamens and are epipetalous
(d) Ovary is inferior
Choose the correct answer from the options given below :`,o:["(d) only","(a) and (b) only","(b), (c) and (d) only","(a), (b) and (c) only"],c:3,s:`In Solanaceae the flowers are bisexual and actinomorphic.
The calyx has five sepals that are united.
The androecium has five stamens and is epipetalous.
The ovary is superior, so (d) is wrong.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Morphology of Flowering Plants"},{id:"neet-2026-q120",pn:120,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants",st:"Reaction centres",q:"Which pigment has absorption peak at 700 nm in the photosynthetic reaction centre PS I (P700)?",o:["Chlorophyll a","Xanthophylls","Carotenoids","Chlorophyll b"],c:0,s:`In PS I the reaction centre chlorophyll a has an absorption peak at 700 nm.
Hence it is called P\u2087\u2080\u2080.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants"},{id:"neet-2026-q121",pn:121,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"Cell: The Unit of Life",st:"Mitochondria",q:"Mitochondrial inner membrane encloses ___________ .",o:["cytosol","mucus","aqueous humor","matrix"],c:3,s:"The mitochondrial inner membrane encloses the matrix.",sub:"Botany",ch:"Cell: The Unit of Life"},{id:"neet-2026-q122",pn:122,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"Body Fluids and Circulation",st:"Blood coagulation",q:"Which of the following statements is incorrect?",o:["Blood clot consists of fibrins","Fibrin is produced from fibrinogen","Fibrinogen is produced from fibrin","Blood coagulates in response to an injury"],c:2,s:`Fibrins are formed by the conversion of inactive fibrinogen in the plasma by the enzyme thrombin.
So fibrinogen is not produced from fibrin; that statement is incorrect.
Note: Question sits in the Botany block of the booklet but is Zoology content; sub set to Zoology.`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Body Fluids and Circulation"},{id:"neet-2026-q123",pn:123,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"Biological Classification",st:"Lichens",q:"Symbiotic association between fungi and algae are called ________.",o:["sponges","mycorrhiza","chrysophytes","lichens"],c:3,s:`Lichens are symbiotic (mutually useful) associations between algae and fungi.
Mycorrhiza are associations between fungi and roots of higher plants.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Biological Classification"},{id:"neet-2026-q124",pn:124,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"Neural Control and Coordination",st:"Human neural system",q:"Which of the following is not a part of human central neural system?",o:["Dura mater","Pia mater","Pericardium","Arachnoid"],c:2,s:`Pericardium is a double-walled membranous bag that protects the heart.
Arachnoid, dura mater and pia mater are meninges that cover the brain inside the skull.`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Neural Control and Coordination"},{id:"neet-2026-q125",pn:125,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"Plant Growth and Development",st:"Plant growth regulators",q:"Which of the following plant growth regulators is used as herbicide?",o:["Kinetin","Abscisic acid","Gibberellin","2, 4-D"],c:3,s:`2, 4-D is an artificial auxin.
It is widely used as a herbicide.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Plant Growth and Development"},{id:"neet-2026-q126",pn:126,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants",st:"Calvin cycle",q:"How many turns of Calvin cycle are required for the formation of three molecules of glucose?",o:["3","1","18","6"],c:2,s:`One molecule of glucose needs 6 turns of the cycle.
For three molecules: 6 \xD7 3 = 18 turns.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants"},{id:"neet-2026-q127",pn:127,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"The Living World",st:"Taxonomic categories",q:`Arrange the following taxonomic categories in ascending order.
(a) Genus
(b) Class
(c) Order
(d) Phylum
(e) Family
(f) Kingdom
(g) Species
Choose the correct answer from the options given below :`,o:["(a), (c), (d), (g), (f), (b), (e)","(g), (c), (d), (b), (e), (a), (f)","(f), (c), (b), (g), (d), (e), (a)","(g), (a), (e), (c), (b), (d), (f)"],c:3,s:`Ascending order of taxonomic categories:
Species (g), Genus (a), Family (e), Order (c), Class (b), Phylum (d), Kingdom (f).`,sub:"Botany",ch:"The Living World"},{id:"neet-2026-q128",pn:128,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"Structural Organisation in Animals",st:"Frog anatomy",q:"In frogs, the number of pairs of cranial nerves arising from the brain are ______.",o:["9","10","12","6"],c:1,s:"In frogs, ten pairs of cranial nerves arise from the brain.",sub:"Zoology",ch:"Structural Organisation in Animals"},{id:"neet-2026-q129",pn:129,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants",st:"Early experiments",q:"Select the correct sequence of experiments that led to a gradual understanding of photosynthesis in green plants.",o:["Role of air \u2192 release of oxygen \u2192 production of glucose \u2192 absorption spectra of chlorophyll a and b","Release of oxygen \u2192 production of glucose \u2192 absorption spectra of chlorophyll a and b \u2192 role of air","Production of glucose \u2192 role of air \u2192 release of oxygen \u2192 absorption spectra of chlorophyll a and b","Absorption spectra of chlorophyll a and b \u2192 production of glucose \u2192 release of oxygen \u2192 role of air"],c:0,s:`The correct sequence of experiments is:
Role of air in making glucose (Joseph Priestley's bell jar experiment, 1770).
Release of oxygen (Jan Ingenhousz's experiment with an aquatic plant).
Production of glucose (Julius von Sachs, 1854).
Absorption spectra of chlorophyll a and b (T.W. Engelmann's experiment).`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants"},{id:"neet-2026-q130",pn:130,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"Plant Kingdom",st:"Pteridophytes",q:"Sphenopsida class belongs to ______________.",o:["angiosperms","gymnosperms","pteridophytes","bryophytes"],c:2,s:"Class Sphenopsida belongs to pteridophytes.",sub:"Botany",ch:"Plant Kingdom"},{id:"neet-2026-q131",pn:131,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"Plant Kingdom",st:"Life cycles of plants",q:`Match List-I with List-II.
A. Fusion of protoplasms between gametes \u2014 I. Meiosis
B. Fusion of two nuclei \u2014 II. Plasmogamy
C. Generation of haploid spores \u2014 III. Karyogamy
Choose the correct answer from the options given below :`,o:["A-II, B-I, C-III","A-III, B-II, C-I","A-I, B-III, C-II","A-II, B-III, C-I"],c:3,s:`Plasmogamy is fusion of protoplasms (A-II).
Karyogamy is fusion of two nuclei (B-III).
Meiosis leads to the production of haploid spores (C-I).`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Plant Kingdom"},{id:"neet-2026-q132",pn:132,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"Biological Classification",st:"Prokaryotes",q:"Which of the following is not a prokaryote?",o:["Blue green algae","Mycoplasma","Fungi","Bacteria"],c:2,s:`Prokaryotic cells are found in bacteria, blue-green algae, Mycoplasma and PPLO.
Fungi are eukaryotic organisms.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Biological Classification"},{id:"neet-2026-q133",pn:133,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"Animal Kingdom",st:"Symmetry in animals",q:`Given below are two statements :
Statement I : When any plane passing through the central axis of the body divides the organism into two identical halves, it is called radial symmetry.
Statement II : In phylum Echinodermata, both adults and larvae are radially symmetrical.
In the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below :`,o:["Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are correct"],c:1,s:`Statement I is correct: a plane through the central axis giving two identical halves means radial symmetry.
Echinoderms show radial or bilateral symmetry depending on the stage.
Adults are radially symmetrical, but larvae are bilaterally symmetrical.
So Statement II is incorrect.`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Animal Kingdom"},{id:"neet-2026-q134",pn:134,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"The Living World",st:"Taxonomic categories",q:"Genus represents __________.",o:["a population of plants and animals","a group of closely related species","a group of closely related families","an individual plant or animal"],c:1,s:`A genus is a group of related species.
These species have more characters in common than with species of other genera.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"The Living World"},{id:"neet-2026-q135",pn:135,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"Biological Classification",st:"Viruses, viroids and prions",q:"Mad cow disease is caused by _______.",o:["viroids","Aspergillus sp.","Mycoplasma sp.","prions"],c:3,s:"Mad cow disease is caused by prions.",sub:"Botany",ch:"Biological Classification"},{id:"neet-2026-q136",pn:136,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"Body Fluids and Circulation",st:"Human heart",q:"The opening between the right atrium and the right ventricle is guarded by _________.",o:["tricuspid valve","semilunar valve","sino-atrial node","bicuspid valve"],c:0,s:`The opening between the right atrium and right ventricle is guarded by a valve of three muscular flaps (cusps), the tricuspid valve.
A bicuspid (mitral) valve guards the opening between the left atrium and left ventricle.
The openings of the ventricles into the pulmonary artery and the aorta have semilunar valves.`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Body Fluids and Circulation"},{id:"neet-2026-q137",pn:137,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"Biotechnology and its Applications",st:"Bt toxin",q:"The inactive form of Bt toxin is converted to the active form in the insect gut _______",o:["due to acidic pH","by proteases","by nucleases","due to alkaline pH"],c:3,s:`Bt toxin protein exists as inactive protoxin.
When an insect eats it, the alkaline pH of the gut solubilises the crystals.
This converts the protoxin into the active toxin.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Biotechnology and its Applications"},{id:"neet-2026-q138",pn:138,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes",st:"Recombinant DNA technology",q:`Match List-I with List-II.
A. Transformation \u2014 I. Restriction enzyme
B. Cloning site \u2014 II. Transfer DNA to host bacteria
C. Selection \u2014 III. Replication
D. Ori \u2014 IV. Antibiotic
Choose the correct answer from the options given below:`,o:["A-I, B-II, C-IV, D-III","A-III, B-IV, C-II, D-I","A-IV, B-I, C-III, D-II","A-II, B-I, C-IV, D-III"],c:3,s:`Transformation is transfer of DNA to the host bacteria (A-II).
Cloning site is a DNA segment in a plasmid vector with unique recognition sequences for restriction enzymes (B-I).
Antibiotics help in selection of recombinants (C-IV).
Ori is the DNA sequence where the host's replication machinery starts copying the plasmid (D-III).`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes"},{id:"neet-2026-q139",pn:139,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants",st:"Anther structure",q:"How many theca are present in each lobe of a typical bilobed angiosperm anther?",o:["6","8","12","2"],c:3,s:`A typical anther is bilobed.
Each lobe has two theca, so the anther is dithecous.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants"},{id:"neet-2026-q140",pn:140,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"Human Reproduction",st:"Placental hormones",q:"Which of the following hormone is not secreted by human placenta?",o:["Estrogen","Progesterone","LH","hCG"],c:2,s:`The placenta acts as a temporary endocrine gland in pregnancy.
It secretes hCG, human placental lactogen (hPL), estrogen, progestogens and others.
LH is secreted by the anterior pituitary, not the placenta.`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Human Reproduction"},{id:"neet-2026-q141",pn:141,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"Human Reproduction",st:"Spermatozoa",q:"Sperm motility is due to ___________.",o:["ciliary movement","amoeboid movement","muscular movement","flagellar movement"],c:3,s:"Sperm travels through the fallopian tube by flagellar movement.",sub:"Zoology",ch:"Human Reproduction"},{id:"neet-2026-q142",pn:142,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"Principles of Inheritance and Variation",st:"Mendel's monohybrid cross",q:`Given below are two statements : one is labelled as Assertion A and the other is labelled as Reason R.
Assertion A : In an experiment, Mendel observed that the F1 progeny plants are all tall and none are dwarf.
Reason R : Stem height is a contrasting trait, with tall being dominant and dwarf being recessive.
In the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below :`,o:["Both A and R are correct but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is correct but R is not correct","A is not correct but R is correct","Both A and R are correct and R is the correct explanation of A"],c:3,s:`Mendel crossed tall and dwarf plants and found all F\u2081 plants tall.
The tall trait (T) is dominant over the dwarf trait (t).
Because of this dominance, all F\u2081 plants are tall.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Principles of Inheritance and Variation"},{id:"neet-2026-q143",pn:143,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes",st:"PCR",q:"During PCR, primers bind to the DNA strands in the ______step.",o:["extension","annealing","ligation","denaturation"],c:1,s:`PCR has three steps: denaturation, annealing and extension.
Primers bind to the DNA strands in the annealing step.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes"},{id:"neet-2026-q144",pn:144,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants",st:"Seed structure, endosperm",q:"Which of the following plant produces non-albuminous seeds?",o:["Maize","Barley","Pea","Wheat"],c:2,s:`Non-albuminous seeds have no residual endosperm.
The endosperm is completely used up during embryo development.
Example: pea.
Wheat, maize and barley are albuminous seeds.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants"},{id:"neet-2026-q145",pn:145,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"Principles of Inheritance and Variation",st:"ABO blood group inheritance",q:"For a person with blood group \u2018O\u2019, which of the following is not a possible combination of parents\u2019 blood group genotypes?",o:["Father : IAi and Mother : IAi","Father : IBi and Mother : IBi","Father : IAIB and Mother : IAi","Father : IAi and Mother : IBi"],c:2,s:`When IA and IB are present together, both express their own type of sugars.
So an IAIB parent can never give a child of blood group O.
For a child to have type O blood, both parents must carry the recessive \u2018O\u2019 allele (i) and pass it down.`,sfig:{w:290,h:190,alt:"Cross: male I\u1D2CI\u1D2E \xD7 female I\u1D2Ci; lines from the gametes give I\u1D2CI\u1D2C (\u2018A\u2019 blood group), I\u1D2Ci (\u2018A\u2019 blood group), I\u1D2CI\u1D2E (\u2018AB\u2019 blood group) and I\u1D2Ei (\u2018B\u2019 blood group).",d:[["t",20,22,"\u2642","m"],["t",45,22,"I\u1D2C","m"],["t",80,22,"I\u1D2E","m"],["t",160,22,"\xD7","m"],["t",215,22,"I\u1D2C","m"],["t",245,22,"i","m"],["t",275,22,"\u2640","m"],["l",45,28,55,125,""],["l",45,28,120,125,""],["l",80,28,185,125,""],["l",80,28,250,125,""],["l",215,28,55,125,""],["l",215,28,185,125,""],["l",245,28,120,125,""],["l",245,28,250,125,""],["t",55,142,"I\u1D2CI\u1D2C","m"],["t",55,158,"(\u2018A\u2019 blood","ms"],["t",55,170,"group)","ms"],["t",120,142,"I\u1D2Ci","m"],["t",120,158,"(\u2018A\u2019 blood","ms"],["t",120,170,"group)","ms"],["t",185,142,"I\u1D2CI\u1D2E","m"],["t",185,158,"(\u2018AB\u2019","ms"],["t",185,170,"blood","ms"],["t",185,182,"group)","ms"],["t",250,142,"I\u1D2Ei","m"],["t",250,158,"(\u2018B\u2019 blood","ms"],["t",250,170,"group)","ms"]]},sub:"Zoology",ch:"Principles of Inheritance and Variation"},{id:"neet-2026-q146",pn:146,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"Microbes in Human Welfare",st:"Microbes as medicine",q:"Which of the following is used as a clot buster?",o:["Penicillin","Cyclosporin A","Statins","Streptokinase"],c:3,s:`Streptokinase is produced by the bacterium Streptococcus.
It is modified by genetic engineering.
It is used as a clot-buster to remove clots from blood vessels.
It helps patients who have had myocardial infarction (heart attack).`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Microbes in Human Welfare"},{id:"neet-2026-q147",pn:147,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"Biodiversity and Conservation",st:"Species diversity patterns",q:`Arrange the following in descending order of number of species in the Amazonian rain forest.
(a) Plants
(b) Birds
(c) Fishes
(d) Invertebrates
(e) Mammals
Choose the correct answer from the options given below :`,o:["(d) > (a) > (c) > (b) > (e)","(e) > (b) > (a) > (c) > (d)","(b) > (a) > (d) > (c) > (e)","(c) > (b) > (d) > (e) > (a)"],c:0,s:`The Amazonian rain forest has more than 40,000 species of plants.
It has 3,000 species of fishes, 1300 of birds, 427 of mammals and 378 of reptiles.
It has more than 1,25,000 invertebrates.
So the decreasing order is (d) > (a) > (c) > (b) > (e).`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Biodiversity and Conservation"},{id:"neet-2026-q148",pn:148,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes",st:"Cloning vectors, plasmids",q:`Given below are two statements :
Statement I : Plasmids are autonomously replicating DNA.
Statement II : Plasmids are extrachromosomal DNA.
In the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below :`,o:["Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are correct"],c:3,s:`Plasmids are used as cloning vectors in genetic engineering.
Plasmids are autonomously replicating circular extra-chromosomal DNA.
They can replicate inside bacterial cells independent of the control of the chromosomal DNA.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes"},{id:"neet-2026-q149",pn:149,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"Evolution",st:"Homologous organs",q:`Given below are two statements : one is labelled as Assertion A and the other is labelled as Reason R.
Assertion A : Forelimbs of human and bats are homologous.
Reason R : Forelimbs of humans and bats have similar anatomical structure.
In the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below.`,o:["Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true","Both A and R are correct and R is the correct explanation of A"],c:3,s:`Forelimbs of bats and humans have a similar pattern of bone arrangement.
They do different functions but have similar anatomical structure: humerus, radius, ulna, carpals, metacarpals and phalanges.
The same structure developed in different directions due to adaptation to different needs.
This is divergent evolution, so these structures are homologous.`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Evolution"},{id:"neet-2026-q150",pn:150,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"Excretory Products and their Elimination",st:"Henle's loop, urine concentration",q:`Which of the following statements about the reabsorption process in Henle's loop are correct?
(a) The descending limb of Henle's loop is permeable to water but almost impermeable to electrolytes.
(b) Urine gets concentrated in Henle's loop.
(c) Reabsorption of Na\u207A and water takes place in Henle's loop.
(d) Active or passive transport of electrolytes occurs in the ascending limb of Henle's loop.
Choose the correct answer from the options given below:`,o:["(b), (c) and (d) only","(a), (b) and (c) only","(a), (b) and (d) only","(a) and (b) only"],a:"Answer (*); Sol. None of the given options are correct",s:`None of the given options is correct.
Note: Printed answer is (*): the publisher says none of the options is correct.`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Excretory Products and their Elimination"},{id:"neet-2026-q151",pn:151,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"Human Health and Disease",st:"Lymphoid organs",q:`Which of the following are secondary lymphoid organs?
(a) Bone marrow
(b) Tonsils
(c) Spleen
(d) Thymus
Choose the correct answer from the options given below:`,o:["(b) and (c) only","(b) and (d) only","(a) and (d) only","(a) and (b) only"],c:0,s:`Bone marrow and thymus are primary lymphoid organs.
Tonsils and spleen are secondary lymphoid organs.`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Human Health and Disease"},{id:"neet-2026-q152",pn:152,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"Locomotion and Movement",st:"Vertebral column",q:"Which of the following is the correct order of arrangement of vertebrate column from the head to toe?",o:["Sacrum, lumbar vertebra, thoracic vertebra, cervical vertebra","Cervical vertebra, lumbar vertebra, thoracic vertebra, sacrum","Cervical vertebra, thoracic vertebra, lumbar vertebra, sacrum","Cervical vertebra, thoracic vertebra, sacrum, lumbar vertebra"],c:2,s:`The vertebral column is formed by 26 serially arranged units called vertebrae and lies on the dorsal side.
Starting from the skull it has cervical (7), thoracic (12), lumbar (5), sacral (1, fused) and coccygeal (1, fused) regions.
So the order is cervical, thoracic, lumbar, sacrum and coccyx.`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Locomotion and Movement"},{id:"neet-2026-q153",pn:153,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants",st:"Endosperm ploidy",q:"If the diploid chromosome number of typical angiosperm is 36, what would be the chromosome number in its endosperm?",o:["36","54","72","18"],c:1,s:`The endosperm is triploid.
2n = 36, so n = 18.
3n = n + n + n
= 18 + 18 + 18
= 54`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants"},{id:"neet-2026-q154",pn:154,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"Breathing and Exchange of Gases",st:"Respiratory organs in animals",q:"Sponges exchange O\u2082 with CO\u2082 by __________.",o:["moist cuticle","tracheal tubes","gills","simple diffusion over their entire body surfaces"],c:3,s:`Breathing mechanisms differ among animals, mainly by habitat and level of organisation.
Lower invertebrates like sponges, coelenterates and flatworms exchange O\u2082 with CO\u2082 by simple diffusion over their entire body surfaces.
Earthworms use moist cuticle.
Insects have tracheal tubes.
Gills are used by most aquatic arthropods and molluscs.`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Breathing and Exchange of Gases"},{id:"neet-2026-q155",pn:155,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"Human Health and Disease",st:"Sexually transmitted diseases",q:"Which of the following disease is not sexually transmitted?",o:["Tuberculosis","Gonorrhoea","Genital warts","Syphilis"],c:0,s:`Genital warts, syphilis and gonorrhoea are sexually transmitted diseases.
Genital warts are caused by Human papilloma virus.
Syphilis is caused by Treponema pallidum.
Gonorrhoea is caused by Neisseria gonorrhoeae.
Tuberculosis is caused by Mycobacterium tuberculosis and spreads through air.`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Human Health and Disease"},{id:"neet-2026-q156",pn:156,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants",st:"Embryo sac, synergids",q:"Which of the following in female gametophyte of an angiosperm helps in guiding the pollen tube for fertilizing the eggs?",o:["Synergids","Central cells","Polar nucleus","Antipodals"],c:0,s:"Synergids guide the entry of the pollen tube for fertilizing the egg in a typical angiospermic embryo sac.",sub:"Botany",ch:"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants"},{id:"neet-2026-q157",pn:157,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"Chemical Coordination and Integration",st:"Hormones and functions",q:`Match List-I with List-II.
A. Excess growth hormone \u2014 I. Reabsorption of water and electrolytes in kidney
B. Luteinizing hormone \u2014 II. Contraction of uterus during child birth
C. Vasopressin \u2014 III. Acromegaly
D. Oxytocin \u2014 IV. Ovulation
Choose the correct answer from the options given below:`,o:["A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-II, B-IV, C-I, D-III","A-IV, B-III, C-I, D-II","A-III, B-IV, C-II, D-I"],c:0,s:`Excess growth hormone \u2192 Acromegaly
Luteinizing hormone \u2192 Ovulation
Vasopressin \u2192 Reabsorption of water and electrolytes in kidneys
Oxytocin \u2192 Contraction of uterus during child birth`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Chemical Coordination and Integration"},{id:"neet-2026-q158",pn:158,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"Human Reproduction",st:"Ovulation, oogenesis",q:"The covering of ovum at ovulation is __________.",o:["zona radiata","zona pellucida","chorion","endometrium"],c:1,s:`The Graafian follicle ruptures and releases the secondary oocyte (ovum) from the ovary. This is ovulation.
The secondary oocyte has a membrane around it called zona pellucida.
Endometrium is the innermost layer of the uterine wall.
Chorion is the outermost extraembryonic membrane around the embryo.`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Human Reproduction"},{id:"neet-2026-q159",pn:159,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"Organisms and Populations",st:"Population interactions",q:`Match List-I with List-II.
A. Both species are harmed \u2014 I. Predation
B. One species is harmed and the other is benefited \u2014 II. Mutualism
C. Both species are benefited \u2014 III. Competition
D. One is benefited while the other has no effect \u2014 IV. Commensalism
Choose the correct answer from the options given below:`,o:["A-I, B-II, C-III, D-IV","A-II, B-I, C-IV, D-III","A-III, B-I, C-II, D-IV","A-III, B-IV, C-II, D-I"],c:2,s:`Both species are harmed in competition.
One species is harmed and the other benefited in predation.
Both species are benefited in mutualism.
One species is benefited while the other has no effect in commensalism.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Organisms and Populations"},{id:"neet-2026-q160",pn:160,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"Human Reproduction",st:"Male reproductive system",q:"Which of the following structure is not a part of the male reproductive system?",o:["Epididymis","Vasa efferentia","Infundibulum","Rete testis"],c:2,s:`Male accessory ducts include rete testis, vasa efferentia, epididymis and vas deferens.
Oviducts, uterus and vagina are the female accessory ducts.
Infundibulum is a part of the oviduct.`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Human Reproduction"},{id:"neet-2026-q161",pn:161,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"Ecosystem",st:"Food chain, trophic levels",q:"Which of the following are primary consumers in a food chain?",o:["Predators","Herbivores","Carnivores","Parasites"],c:1,s:"Primary consumers in a food chain are the herbivores.",sub:"Botany",ch:"Ecosystem"},{id:"neet-2026-q162",pn:162,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"Organisms and Populations",st:"Competition",q:`Given below are two statements : one is labelled as Assertion A and the other is labelled as Reason R.
Assertion A : Abingdon tortoise in Galapagos islands became extinct within a decade after goats were introduced.
Reason R : Goats were more efficient at browsing than Abingdon tortoise.
In the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:`,o:["Both A and R are correct but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is correct but R is not correct","A is not correct but R is correct","Both A and R are correct and R is the correct explanation of A"],c:3,s:`Abingdon tortoise in Galapagos islands became extinct within a decade after goats were introduced there.
This was apparently due to the greater browsing efficiency of the goats.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Organisms and Populations"},{id:"neet-2026-q163",pn:163,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"Molecular Basis of Inheritance",st:"Lac operon",q:"Which of the following statements about lac-operon is correct?",o:["Lactose activates repressor to bind to the operator","Genes i, z, y and a share single common promoter","Galactose can act as an inducer of lac operon","Gene i is constitutively expressed"],c:3,s:`Galactose cannot act as an inducer of lac operon.
Gene i is constitutively expressed in lac operon.
The repressor binds to the operator when lactose is not available.
Genes z, y and a are structural genes and have a different promoter from that of gene i.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Molecular Basis of Inheritance"},{id:"neet-2026-q164",pn:164,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"Human Health and Disease",st:"Malaria, Plasmodium life cycle",q:"Which of the following statements is correct about Plasmodium?",o:["Reproduces sexually in RBCs","Gametocytes develop in mosquito gut","Fertilization takes place in mosquito gut","Reproduces sexually in liver cells"],c:2,s:`Plasmodium reproduces asexually in liver cells.
It also reproduces asexually in red blood cells, which burst and cause cycles of fever. Released parasites infect new RBCs.
A female Anopheles mosquito takes up gametocytes with its blood meal.
Fertilization and development take place in the mosquito's gut.
The sexual stages (gametocytes) develop in RBCs.`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Human Health and Disease"},{id:"neet-2026-q165",pn:165,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"Evolution",st:"Hardy-Weinberg principle",q:"A population of diploid organisms is at Hardy-Weinberg equilibrium. If the frequency of allele A is 0.1, the frequency of AA is ________.",o:["0.02","0.10","0.99","0.01"],c:3,s:`Frequency of allele A, p = 0.1.
At Hardy-Weinberg equilibrium, frequency of AA = p\xB2.
p\xB2 = (0.1)\xB2 = 0.01.`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Evolution"},{id:"neet-2026-q166",pn:166,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"Evolution",st:"Adaptive radiation",q:"Adaptive radiation in placental mammals and Australian Marsupials leading to similarity between distant species is an example of ________.",o:["convergent evolution","founder effect","genetic drift","divergent evolution"],c:0,s:`When more than one adaptive radiation appears in an isolated geographical area (representing different habitats), it is called convergent evolution.
Placental mammals in Australia also show adaptive radiation into varieties.
Each of them looks similar to a corresponding marsupial.`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Evolution"},{id:"neet-2026-q167",pn:167,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"Human Health and Disease",st:"Immunity and antibodies",q:"Colostrum, secreted by mother during initial days of lactation, is abundant in ________.",o:["IgM","IgA","IgD","IgG"],c:1,s:`Colostrum, secreted by the mother in the initial days of lactation, is abundant in IgA antibody.
IgG antibody passes through the placenta and gives natural passive immunity.`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Human Health and Disease"},{id:"neet-2026-q168",pn:168,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes",st:"Isolation of genetic material",q:`Given below are two statements: one is labelled as Assertion A and the other is labelled as Reason R.
Assertion A: In recombinant DNA technology, lysozyme is used for disrupting bacterial cells while cellulase is for plant cells.
Reason R: Isolation of genetic material needs disruption of cells.
In the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:`,o:["Both A and R are correct but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is correct but R is not correct","A is not correct but R is correct","Both A and R are correct and R is the correct explanation of A"],c:3,s:`DNA is enclosed within membranes, so the cell must be broken open to release it.
Other macromolecules such as RNA, proteins, polysaccharides and lipids are released along with it.
This is done with enzymes: lysozyme (bacteria), cellulase (plant cells), chitinase (fungus).
So both A and R are correct and R explains A.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes"},{id:"neet-2026-q169",pn:169,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"Human Reproduction",st:"Menstrual cycle",q:`Given below are two statements:
Statement I: Ovulation is caused by LH surge leading to rupture of Graafian follicles.
Statement II: Graafian follicle remaining after ovulation transform into corpus luteum and secretes large amount of estrogen.
In the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:`,o:["Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are correct"],c:1,s:`A rapid rise of LH to its maximum in mid-cycle is the LH surge.
It makes the Graafian follicle rupture and release the ovum (ovulation). So Statement I is correct.
The rest of the follicle becomes the corpus luteum.
The corpus luteum secretes large amounts of progesterone, not estrogen. So Statement II is incorrect.`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Human Reproduction"},{id:"neet-2026-q170",pn:170,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"Evolution",st:"Natural selection",q:`Natural selection can lead to ________.
(a) stabilisation
(b) genetic drift
(c) directional change
(d) disruption
Choose the correct answer from the options given below:`,o:["(a), (c) and (d) only","(a), (b), (c) and (d)","(a) and (c) only","(a) only"],c:0,s:`Natural selection can lead to stabilisation, directional change or disruption.
In natural selection, heritable variations that help better survival let organisms reproduce and leave more progeny.
Variation from mutation, recombination, gene flow or genetic drift changes the frequency of genes and alleles in the next generation.`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Evolution"},{id:"neet-2026-q171",pn:171,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"Reproductive Health",st:"Assisted reproductive technologies",q:"The method of directly of injecting a sperm into ovum in assisted reproductive technology is called:",o:["Zygote intra fallopian transfer (ZIFT)","Intra cytoplasmic sperm injection (ICSI)","Embryo transfer (ET)","Gamete intra fallopian transfer (GIFT)"],c:1,s:`ICSI is a procedure to form an embryo in the laboratory in which a sperm is injected directly into the ovum.
ZIFT transfers the zygote or early embryo (up to 8 blastomeres) into the fallopian tube.
GIFT transfers an ovum collected from a donor into the fallopian tube of another female who cannot produce one but can give a suitable environment for fertilisation and development.`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Reproductive Health"},{id:"neet-2026-q172",pn:172,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"Human Health and Disease",st:"Drugs and abuse",q:"Which of the following is used as an effective sedative and painkiller for treating post-surgery patients?",o:["Antibiotics","Morphine","Anti-retroviral drugs","Interferon"],c:1,s:`Morphine is a very effective sedative and painkiller, useful for patients who have had surgery.
Interferon belongs to the cytokine barrier of innate immunity.
Antibiotics are used to stop the growth of bacteria.`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Human Health and Disease"},{id:"neet-2026-q173",pn:173,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"Principles of Inheritance and Variation",st:"Chromosomal disorders",q:`Given below are two statements:
Statement I: Down's syndrome is caused by the absence of one of the X-chromosomes.
Statement II: Turner's syndrome is caused by the presence of an additional copy of the chromosomes.
In the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below:`,o:["Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are correct"],c:0,s:`Down's syndrome is caused by an additional copy of chromosome 21 (trisomy 21). So Statement I is incorrect.
Turner's syndrome is caused by absence of one X-chromosome, i.e. 45 with XO. So Statement II is incorrect.`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Principles of Inheritance and Variation"},{id:"neet-2026-q174",pn:174,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"Evolution",st:"Evidences for evolution",q:"Which of the following is not evidence for evolution?",o:["Paleontological evidence from fossil records","Embryological support for evolution as proposed by Ernst Heckel","Divergent evolution of anatomical structures such as forelimbs","Convergent evolution of traits like wings of birds and butterflies"],a:"2/4*",s:`Divergent evolution of structures such as forelimbs shows homology.
Homology shows common ancestry and is evidence for evolution.
When the same structure develops in different directions for different needs, the structures are homologous.
Convergent evolution is not evidence, because species can develop similar features without a common ancestor.
Fossils are remains of hard parts of life forms found in rocks.
Studying fossils in different sedimentary layers shows the geological period in which they lived.
Embryological support was proposed by Ernst Heckel from features common to vertebrate embryos but absent in adults.
Later this proposal was disproved by Karl Ernst von Baer.
Note: Printed answer is '2/4*' (two options, 2 or 4, with an asterisk); solution supports both: Heckel's proposal was disapproved by von Baer, and convergent evolution is not evidence of common ancestry.`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Evolution"},{id:"neet-2026-q175",pn:175,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"Organisms and Populations",st:"Population growth models",q:`Given below are two statements: one is labelled as Assertion A and the other is labelled as Reason R.
Assertion A: The logistic growth model of populations is considered more realistic than the exponential growth model.
Reason R: Resources are finite.
In the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:`,o:["Both A and R are correct but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is correct but R is not correct","A is not correct but R is correct","Both A and R are correct and R is the correct explanation of A"],c:3,s:`The logistic model is more realistic than the exponential model because resources in nature are limited (finite).
So both A and R are correct and R explains A.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Organisms and Populations"},{id:"neet-2026-q176",pn:176,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"Evolution",st:"Origin of man",q:`Given below are two statements:
Statement I: Modern Homo sapiens arose in Australia and moved across continents.
Statement II: Homo sapiens arose around 75000 to 10000 years ago.
In the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:`,o:["Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are correct"],c:2,s:`Homo sapiens arose in Africa and moved across continents, developing into distinct races. So Statement I is incorrect.
Modern Homo sapiens arose during the ice age, between 75,000 and 10,000 years ago. So Statement II is correct.`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Evolution"},{id:"neet-2026-q177",pn:177,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"Organisms and Populations",st:"Population growth models",q:"Consider a population of 10 million cells. Given the per-capita birth rate of 0.002 (per unit time) and the per-capita death rate of 0.002 (per unit time), the expected number of cells after 10 generations is ______.",o:["5 million","10 million","100 million","1 million"],c:1,s:`Per-capita birth rate b = 0.002.
Per-capita death rate d = 0.002.
The population equation is dN/dt = (b \u2212 d)N.
r = (b \u2212 d) = 0.002 \u2212 0.002 = 0.
So dN/dt = 0.
The population stays constant, so after 10 generations it is still 10 million.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Organisms and Populations"},{id:"neet-2026-q178",pn:178,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"Ecosystem",st:"Energy flow",q:`Which of the following statements are Correct?
(a) Energy flow from producers to consumers is unidirectional
(b) Energy pyramid can never be inverted
(c) Transfer of energy follows the 1% law
Choose the correct answer from the options given below:`,o:["(a) and (b) only","(a) and (c) only","(b) and (c) only","(a), (b) and (c)"],c:0,s:`Energy flow is unidirectional: plants capture solar energy, then food passes from producers to decomposers.
The pyramid of energy is always upright and can never be inverted, because some energy is lost as heat at each step.
Energy transfer follows the 10 per cent law: only 10 per cent of the energy passes to the next trophic level.
So (c), the 1% law, is wrong.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Ecosystem"},{id:"neet-2026-q179",pn:179,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"Locomotion and Movement",st:"Muscle contraction",q:"Muscle contraction is initiated by a signal sent by the central nervous system by the release of _____ .",o:["acetyl coenzyme A","cyclic guanine monophosphate","cyclic adenine monophosphate","acetyl choline"],c:3,s:`The CNS sends the signal through a motor neuron.
The junction of a motor neuron and the sarcolemma of the muscle fibre is the neuromuscular junction (motor-end plate).
A signal reaching it releases a neurotransmitter, acetylcholine.
Acetylcholine generates an action potential in the sarcolemma.`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Locomotion and Movement"},{id:"neet-2026-q180",pn:180,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"Molecular Basis of Inheritance",st:"Transcription",q:"Which of the following enzymes synthesizes precursor mRNA?",o:["RNA polymerase II","RNA polymerase III","DNA polymerase","RNA polymerase I"],c:0,s:"RNA polymerase II transcribes the precursor of mRNA, the heterogeneous nuclear RNA (hnRNA).",sub:"Botany",ch:"Molecular Basis of Inheritance"}]})();
